$$A=\int_\alpha^{\pi+\alpha}(\sin x-2\cos x)\,dx$$
where $0<\alpha<\dfrac{\pi}{2}$ and $\tan\alpha=2$.

[Right triangle with opposite 2, adjacent 1, hypotenuse $\sqrt{5}$.]
$$\begin{aligned}A&=\left[-\cos x-2\sin x\right]_\alpha^{\pi+\alpha}\\ &=-\{\cos(\pi+\alpha)-\cos\alpha\}-2[\sin(\pi+\alpha)-\sin\alpha]\\ &=2\cos\alpha+4\sin\alpha\\ &=2\left(\dfrac{1}{\sqrt{5}}\right)+4\left(\dfrac{2}{\sqrt{5}}\right)\\ &=\dfrac{10}{\sqrt{5}}\end{aligned}$$
Area enclosed by the two curves is $2\sqrt{5}$ sq. units.','3: Writes and evaluates, in simplest exact form, a definite integral for the area between the curves; 2: Substantial progress eg. obtains the anti-derivative and evaluates in terms of alpha, beta; 1: Some progress eg. writes the definite integral and anti-derivative is partially correct',1308,NULL,'parse-v1',NULL,NULL,'[[3, "Correct answer"], [2, "Substantial progress, e.g. obtains the anti-derivative and evaluates in terms of $\\alpha$, $\\beta$"], [1, "Some progress, e.g. writes the definite integral and anti-derivative is partially correct"]]','{"top_criterion_original": "Writes and evaluates, in simplest exact form, a definite integral for the area between the curves"}');

