$$\begin{aligned} u&=4x & v&=\sqrt{x-2}\\ u''&=4 & v''&=\dfrac{1}{2\sqrt{x-2}}\end{aligned}$$
$$\begin{aligned}f''(x)&=\dfrac{4\sqrt{x-2}-\frac{4x}{2\sqrt{x-2}}}{x-2}\\ &=\dfrac{4\sqrt{x-2}-\frac{2x}{\sqrt{x-2}}}{x-2}\end{aligned}$$
Solve $f''(x)=0$ to find any stationary points:
$$\begin{aligned}4\sqrt{x-2}-\dfrac{2x}{\sqrt{x-2}}&=0\\ 4(x-2)-2x&=0\\ 4x-8-2x&=0\\ 2x&=8\end{aligned}$$
Hence $x=4$.
$$f(x)=\dfrac{4\times 4}{\sqrt{4-2}}=8\sqrt{2}$$
A stationary point exists at $(4,8\sqrt{2})$.

Using a table of gradients to determine the nature of the stationary point:

\extable{Ts71_1}

Hence the stationary point is a local minimum.','3: Provides correct solution; 2: Finds the location of the stationary point, or equivalent merit; 1: Finds the first derivative, or equivalent merit',1346,NULL,'parse-v1',NULL,NULL,'[[3, "Correct answer"], [2, "Finds the location of the stationary point, or equivalent merit"], [1, "Finds the first derivative, or equivalent merit"]]','{"top_criterion_original": "Provides correct solution", "source_original": {"latex": "By the quotient rule:\n$$\\begin{aligned}f''(x)&=\\dfrac{4\\sqrt{x-2}-\\frac{4x}{2\\sqrt{x-2}}}{x-2}\\\\ &=\\dfrac{4\\sqrt{x-2}-\\frac{2x}{\\sqrt{x-2}}}{x-2}\\end{aligned}$$\nSolve $f''(x)=0$ to find any stationary points:\n$$\\begin{aligned}4\\sqrt{x-2}-\\dfrac{2x}{\\sqrt{x-2}}&=0\\\\ 4(x-2)-2x&=0\\\\ 4x-8-2x&=0\\\\ 2x&=8\\end{aligned}$$\nHence $x=4$.\n$$f(x)=\\dfrac{4\\times 4}{\\sqrt{4-2}}=8\\sqrt{2}$$\nA stationary point exists at $(4,8\\sqrt{2})$.\n\nUsing a table of gradients to determine the nature of the stationary point:\n\n\\extable{Ts71_1}\n\nHence the stationary point is a local minimum."}}');

