$$\begin{aligned} \text{Area} &= \int_0^3 x^3-3x^2+3x+4\,dx \\ &= \left[\dfrac{x^4}{4}-x^3+\dfrac{3x^2}{2}+4x\right]_0^3 \\ &= \dfrac{75}{4}\text{ or }18.75 \end{aligned}$$
Two applications of the trapezoidal rule from $x=0$ to $x=3$ means two strips, each of width
$$h=\dfrac{3-0}{2}=1.5$$
so the function values are needed at $x=0,\ 1.5,\ 3$:
$$f(0)=4,\qquad f(1.5)=5.125,\qquad f(3)=13$$
Estimate using the trapezoidal rule:
$$\int_a^b f(x)\,dx\approx\dfrac{h}{2}\left\{f(x_0)+2f(x_1)+f(x_2)\right\}$$
$$\begin{aligned} \text{Area} &\approx \dfrac{1.5}{2}[4+2\times 5.125+13] \\ &\approx 20.4375 \end{aligned}$$
$$20.4375-18.75=1.6875$$
$\therefore$ The trapezoidal rule overestimates the exact area under the curve by $1.6875 \text{ units}^2$.','3: Provides correct solution; 2: Finds the area using integration and the trapezoidal rule, or equivalent merit; 1: Finds the area using either the trapezoidal rule or integration, or equivalent merit',1346,NULL,'parse-v1',NULL,NULL,'[[3, "Correct answer"], [2, "Finds the area using integration and the trapezoidal rule, or equivalent merit"], [1, "Finds the area using either the trapezoidal rule or integration, or equivalent merit"]]','{"top_criterion_original": "Provides correct solution", "source_original": {"latex": "Exact area under the curve:\n$$\\begin{aligned} \\text{Area} &= \\int_0^3 x^3-3x^2+3x+4\\,dx \\\\ &= \\left[\\dfrac{x^4}{4}-x^3+\\dfrac{3x^2}{2}+4x\\right]_0^3 \\\\ &= \\dfrac{75}{4}\\text{ or }18.75 \\end{aligned}$$\nEstimate of the area under the curve using two applications of the trapezoidal rule:\n$$\\begin{aligned} \\text{Area} &\\approx \\dfrac{1.5}{2}[4+2\\times 5.125+13] \\\\ &\\approx 20.4375 \\end{aligned}$$\n$$20.4375-18.75=1.6875$$\n$\\therefore$ The trapezoidal rule overestimates the exact area under the curve by $1.6875 \\text{ units}^2$."}, "explained": "Working explained: how h is found and the function values (owner request, 11 Nov)."}');

The height after the $n$th bounce is the $n$th term of the geometric progression with first term $a=\dfrac{qH}{100}$ (the first rebound) and common ratio $r=\dfrac{q}{100}$, $0<q<100$.

Height after the second bounce:
$$\begin{aligned} h_n &= T_n=ar^{n-1} \\ h_2 &= \dfrac{qH}{100}\times\dfrac{q}{100} \\ &= \dfrac{q^2H}{(100)^2} \end{aligned}$$','2: Provides correct solution; 1: Attempts to model height of bounce as a geometric progression, or equivalent merit',1346,NULL,'parse-v1',NULL,NULL,'[[2, "Correct answer"], [1, "Attempts to model height of bounce as a geometric progression, or equivalent merit"]]','{"top_criterion_original": "Provides correct solution", "source_original": {"latex": "Initial height of the ball is $H$ metres.\n\nThe height after the $n$th bounce is the $n$th term of the geometric progression with first term $a=H$ and common ratio $r=\\dfrac{q}{100}$, $0<q<100$.\n\nHeight after the second bounce:\n$$\\begin{aligned} h_n &= T_n=ar^{n-1} \\\\ h_2 &= H\\times\\left(\\dfrac{q}{100}\\right)^2 \\\\ &= \\dfrac{q^2H}{(100)^2} \\end{aligned}$$", "criteria": [[2, "Correct answer"], [1, "Attempts to model height of bounce as a geometric progression, or equivalent merit"]]}, "corrected": "Corrected from source: the first term was given as $H$, which would make $h_2=\\dfrac{qH}{100}$."}');
$$\begin{aligned} S_\infty &= H+2(h_1+h_2+h_3+\cdots) \\ &= H+2\left(\dfrac{\frac{qH}{100}}{1-\frac{q}{100}}\right) \\ &= H+\dfrac{2qH}{100-q} \\ &= \dfrac{H(100-q)}{100-q}+\dfrac{2qH}{100-q} \\ &= \dfrac{100H-qH+2qH}{100-q} \\ &= \dfrac{100H+qH}{100-q} \\ &= \dfrac{H(100+q)}{100-q} \end{aligned}$$','3: Provides correct solution; 2: Attempts to simplify the expression involving the limiting sum, or equivalent merit; 1: Correctly models the total distance involving the limiting sum, or equivalent merit',1346,NULL,'parse-v1',NULL,NULL,'[[3, "Correct answer"], [2, "Attempts to simplify the expression involving the limiting sum, or equivalent merit"], [1, "Correctly models the total distance involving the limiting sum, or equivalent merit"]]','{"top_criterion_original": "Provides correct solution"}');
