Let $A$ be the area of the cross-section of the tent.
$$\begin{aligned} A &= 5xy+\dfrac{1}{2}\times 5x\times h \\ &= 5xy+\dfrac{5xh}{2} \end{aligned}$$
From parts (a) and (b):
$$\begin{aligned} A &= 5x(10-4x)+\dfrac{5\sqrt{39}x^2}{4} \\ &= 50x-20x^2+\dfrac{5\sqrt{39}x^2}{4} \\ &= 50x-\left(20-\dfrac{5\sqrt{39}}{4}\right)x^2 \end{aligned}$$
Determine the stationary point and its nature:
$$\dfrac{dA}{dx}=50-2\left(20-\dfrac{5\sqrt{39}}{4}\right)x$$
$$\begin{aligned} 50-2\left(20-\dfrac{5\sqrt{39}}{4}\right)x &= 0 \\ \left(20-\dfrac{5\sqrt{39}}{4}\right)x &= 25 \\ x &= 25\div\left(20-\dfrac{5\sqrt{39}}{4}\right) \\ &= 2.05 \end{aligned}$$
$$\dfrac{d^2A}{dx^2}=-2\left(20-\dfrac{5\sqrt{39}}{4}\right)=-24.387\ldots<0$$
$\therefore$ Maximum volume at $x=2.05$.','3: Provides correct solution; 2: Finds stationary point, or equivalent merit; 1: Finds correct expression for surface area of tent, or equivalent merit',1346,NULL,'parse-v1',NULL,NULL,'[[3, "Correct answer"], [2, "Finds stationary point, or equivalent merit"], [1, "Finds correct expression for area of the cross-section of the tent, or equivalent merit"]]','{"top_criterion_original": "Provides correct solution", "source_original": {"latex": "Maximise the volume by maximising the area of the cross-section, since the depth is constant.\n\nLet $A$ be the area of the cross-section of the tent.\n$$\\begin{aligned} A &= 5xy+\\dfrac{1}{2}\\times 5x\\times h \\\\ &= 5xy+\\dfrac{5xh}{2} \\end{aligned}$$\nFrom parts (a) and (b):\n$$\\begin{aligned} A &= 5x(10-4x)+\\dfrac{5\\sqrt{39}x^2}{4} \\\\ &= 50x-20x^2+\\dfrac{5\\sqrt{39}x^2}{4} \\\\ &= 50x-\\left(20-\\dfrac{5\\sqrt{39}}{4}\\right)x^2 \\end{aligned}$$\nDetermine the stationary point and its nature:\n$$\\dfrac{dA}{dx}=50-2\\left(20-\\dfrac{5\\sqrt{39}}{4}\\right)x$$\n$$\\begin{aligned} 50-2\\left(20-\\dfrac{5\\sqrt{39}}{4}\\right)x &= 0 \\\\ \\left(20-\\dfrac{5\\sqrt{39}}{4}\\right)x &= 25 \\\\ x &= 25\\div\\left(20-\\dfrac{5\\sqrt{39}}{4}\\right) \\\\ &= 2.05 \\end{aligned}$$\n$$\\dfrac{d^2A}{dx^2}=-2\\left(20-\\dfrac{5\\sqrt{39}}{4}\\right)=-24.387\\ldots<0$$\n$\\therefore$ Maximum volume at $x=2.05$.", "criteria": [[3, "Correct answer"], [2, "Finds stationary point, or equivalent merit"], [1, "Finds correct expression for surface area of tent, or equivalent merit"]]}, "corrected": "Corrected from source: the 1-mark criterion said \"surface area\"; the working finds the cross-sectional area."}');

From part (c):
$$V=6\times\left(50x-\left(20-\dfrac{5\sqrt{39}}{4}\right)x^2\right),\quad x=2.05$$
$$\begin{aligned} V &= 6\times\left(50\times 2.05-\left(20-\dfrac{5\sqrt{39}}{4}\right)\times 2.05^2\right) \\ &= 307.5345\ldots \\ &\approx 307.5\text{ m}^3 \end{aligned}$$','2: Provides correct solution; 1: Attempts to calculate the volume using values of $x,y,h$ or using the area of the cross-section from part (c), or equivalent merit',1346,NULL,'parse-v1',NULL,NULL,'[[2, "Correct answer"], [1, "Attempts to calculate the volume using values of $x$, $y$, $h$ or using the area of the cross-section from part (c), or equivalent merit"]]','{"top_criterion_original": "Provides correct solution"}');
$$\begin{aligned} x^3-2x &= x^2 \\ x^3-x^2-2x &= 0 \\ x(x^2-x-2) &= 0 \\ x(x+1)(x-2) &= 0 \end{aligned}$$
Hence the $x$-coordinate of $A$ is $-1$ and the $x$-coordinate of $B$ is $2$.

Area between the two curves is given by
$$\begin{aligned} \text{Area} &= \left|\int_{-1}^{0}x^3-x^2-2x\,dx\right|+\left|\int_0^2 x^3-x^2-2x\,dx\right| \\ &= \left|\left[\dfrac{x^4}{4}-\dfrac{x^3}{3}-x^2\right]_{-1}^{0}\right|+\left|\left[\dfrac{x^4}{4}-\dfrac{x^3}{3}-x^2\right]_0^2\right| \\ &= \left|\dfrac{1}{4}+\dfrac{1}{3}-1\right|+\left|4-\dfrac{8}{3}-4\right| \\ &= \dfrac{5}{12}+\dfrac{8}{3} \\ &= \dfrac{37}{12} \end{aligned}$$','4: Provides correct solution; 3: Correctly sets up a sum of two integrals, including correct bounds for each integral, and attempts to find the area between the two curves, or equivalent merit; 2: Finds the $x$-coordinates of $A$ and $B$, or equivalent merit; 1: Attempts to find the $x$-coordinates of $A$ and $B$ by equating the functions, or equivalent merit',1346,NULL,'parse-v1',NULL,NULL,'[[4, "Correct answer"], [3, "Correctly sets up a sum of two integrals, including correct bounds for each integral, and attempts to find the area between the two curves, or equivalent merit"], [2, "Finds the $x$-coordinates of $A$ and $B$, or equivalent merit"], [1, "Attempts to find the $x$-coordinates of $A$ and $B$ by equating the functions, or equivalent merit"]]','{"top_criterion_original": "Provides correct solution"}');
$$\begin{aligned} \int\dfrac{1-\ln x}{x\ln x}\,dx &= \int\dfrac{\frac{1-\ln x}{x^2}}{\frac{x\ln x}{x^2}}\,dx \\ &= \int\dfrac{\frac{1-\ln x}{x^2}}{\frac{\ln x}{x}}\,dx \end{aligned}$$
Using
$$\dfrac{d}{dx}\left(\dfrac{\ln x}{x}\right)=\dfrac{1-\ln x}{x^2}$$
and
$$\int\dfrac{f''(x)}{f(x)}\,dx=\ln|f(x)|+c$$

$$\int\dfrac{\frac{1-\ln x}{x^2}}{\frac{\ln x}{x}}\,dx\text{ is in the form }\int\dfrac{f''(x)}{f(x)}\,dx$$
Hence,
$$\begin{aligned} \int\dfrac{1-\ln x}{x\ln x}\,dx &= \ln\left|\dfrac{\ln x}{x}\right|+c \\ &= \ln|\ln x|-\ln|x|+c \end{aligned}$$','3: Provides correct solution; 2: Makes substantial progress toward correct solution, such as recognising that the integral is in the form $\int\frac{f''(x)}{f(x)}$ and hence will result in a log function by using the fact that $\frac{d}{dx}\left(\frac{\ln x}{x}\right)=\frac{1-\ln x}{x^2}$, or equivalent merit; 1: Divides numerator and denominator by $x^2$, or equivalent merit',1346,NULL,'parse-v1',NULL,NULL,'[[3, "Correct answer"], [2, "Makes substantial progress toward correct solution, such as recognising that the integral is in the form $\\displaystyle \\int\\dfrac{f''(x)}{f(x)}$ and hence will result in a log function by using the fact that $\\dfrac{d}{dx}\\left(\\dfrac{\\ln x}{x}\\right)=\\dfrac{1-\\ln x}{x^2}$, or equivalent merit"], [1, "Divides numerator and denominator by $x^2$, or equivalent merit"]]','{"top_criterion_original": "Provides correct solution", "source_original": {"latex": "Divide the numerator and denominator of the integrand by $x^2$ such that:\n$$\\begin{aligned} \\int\\dfrac{1-\\ln x}{x\\ln x}\\,dx &= \\int\\dfrac{\\frac{1-\\ln x}{x^2}}{\\frac{x\\ln x}{x^2}}\\,dx \\\\ &= \\int\\dfrac{\\frac{1-\\ln x}{x^2}}{\\frac{\\ln x}{x}}\\,dx \\end{aligned}$$\nUsing\n$$\\dfrac{d}{dx}\\left(\\dfrac{\\ln x}{x}\\right)=\\dfrac{1-\\ln x}{x^2}$$\nand\n$$\\int\\dfrac{f''(x)}{f(x)}\\,dx=\\ln x+c$$\n\n$$\\int\\dfrac{\\frac{1-\\ln x}{x^2}}{\\frac{\\ln x}{x}}\\,dx\\text{ is in the form }\\int\\dfrac{f''(x)}{f(x)}\\,dx$$\nHence,\n$$\\begin{aligned} \\int\\dfrac{1-\\ln x}{x\\ln x}\\,dx &= \\ln\\left|\\dfrac{\\ln x}{x}\\right|+c \\\\ &= \\ln|\\ln x|-\\ln|x|+c \\end{aligned}$$", "criteria": [[3, "Correct answer"], [2, "Makes substantial progress toward correct solution, such as recognising that the integral is in the form $\\displaystyle \\int\\dfrac{f''(x)}{f(x)}$ and hence will result in a log function by using the fact that $\\dfrac{d}{dx}\\left(\\dfrac{\\ln x}{x}\\right)=\\dfrac{1-\\ln x}{x^2}$, or equivalent merit"], [1, "Divides numerator and denominator by $x^2$, or equivalent merit"]]}, "corrected": "Corrected from source: the standard result was written $\\ln x+c$."}');

