$$P(X)=\dfrac{6}{36}=\dfrac16$$
A sum of $7$ comes from $(1,6),(2,5),(3,4),(4,3),(5,2),(6,1)$, so
$$P(Y)=\dfrac{6}{36}=\dfrac16$$
Both events happen only for $(1,6)$, so
$$\begin{aligned} P(X\cap Y)&=\dfrac{1}{36}\\ &=\dfrac16\times\dfrac16\\ &=P(X)\times P(Y) \end{aligned}$$
$\therefore$ $X$ and $Y$ are independent.

($X$ and $Y$ can both happen, so they are not mutually exclusive; $X$ is not a subset of $Y$; $P(X)=P(Y)$.)','1: A',1365,NULL,'parse-v1',NULL,NULL,'[[1, "Correct answer"]]','{"source_original": "Table of outcomes of two rolls; the sum 7 occurs once in each row: $(1,6), (2,5), \\ldots, (6,1)$.", "explained": true, "owner_note_round3": true}');
