$$\therefore\ f''(1)=\dfrac{1}{(e+1)^2}$$
$$m_{\text{normal}}=-(e+1)^2$$
$$f(1)=\dfrac{1}{e+1}$$
$$\begin{aligned} y-\dfrac{1}{e+1} &= -(e+1)^2(x-1) \\ (e+1)y-1 &= -(e+1)^3x+(e+1)^3 \\ (e+1)^3x+(e+1)y-(1+(e+1)^3) &= 0 \end{aligned}$$
Alternatively, using $y=mx+c$:
$$\begin{aligned} \dfrac{1}{e+1} &= -(e+1)^2(1)+c \\ c &= \dfrac{1}{e+1}+(e+1)^2 \end{aligned}$$
$$\therefore\ y=-(e+1)^2x+\dfrac{1}{e+1}+(e+1)^2$$
$$\therefore\ (e+1)^3x+(e+1)y-1-(e+1)^3=0$$','1: $f''(1)$; 1: gradient of normal; 1: general form. GENERAL FORM! A few lost one mark due to leaving their expression with fractional coefficients.',1365,NULL,'parse-v1',NULL,NULL,'[[3, "Correct answer"], [2, "Finds the gradient of the normal, or equivalent merit"], [1, "Finds $f''(1)$, or equivalent merit"]]','{"marker_note": "GENERAL FORM! A few lost one mark due to leaving their expression with fractional coefficients.", "top_criterion_original": "General form", "source_original": {"latex": "$$\\begin{aligned} f''(x) &= \\dfrac{(e^x+1)-e^xx}{(e^x+1)^2} \\\\ &= \\dfrac{e^x(1-x)+1}{(e^x+1)^2} \\end{aligned}$$\n$$\\therefore\\ f''(1)=\\dfrac{1}{(e+1)^2}$$\n$$m_{\\text{normal}}=-(e+1)^2$$\n$$f(1)=\\dfrac{1}{e+1}$$\n$$\\begin{aligned} y-\\dfrac{1}{e+1} &= -(e+1)^2(x-1) \\\\ (e+1)y-1 &= -(e+1)^3x+(e+1)^2 \\\\ (e+1)^3x+(e+1)y-(1+(e+1)^3) &= 0 \\end{aligned}$$\nAlternatively, using $y=mx+c$:\n$$\\begin{aligned} \\dfrac{1}{e+1} &= -(e+1)^2(1)+c \\\\ c &= \\dfrac{1}{e+1}+(e+1)^2 \\end{aligned}$$\n$$\\therefore\\ y=-(e+1)^2x+\\dfrac{1}{e+1}+(e+1)^2$$\n$$\\therefore\\ (e+1)^3x+(e+1)y-1-(e+1)^3=0$$", "criteria": [[1, "$f''(1)$"], [1, "Gradient of normal"], [1, "Correct answer"]]}, "corrected": "Corrected from source: one line ended $+(e+1)^2$ instead of $+(e+1)^3$.", "criteria_original": [[1, "$f''(1)$"], [1, "Gradient of normal"], [1, "Correct answer"]], "rubric_note": "Rewritten top-down from a one-mark-per-step rubric (owner rule, 11 Nov)."}');
