$$\begin{aligned} S_n &= \dfrac{n}{2}(2a+(n-1)d) \\ 925 &= \dfrac{n}{2}(2(1)+(n-1)(3)) \\ 1850 &= n(2+3n-3) \\ 1850 &= n(3n-1) \\ 3n^2-n-1850 &= 0 \end{aligned}$$
$$\begin{aligned} n &= \dfrac{1\pm\sqrt{1^2-4(3)(-1850)}}{2(3)} \\ &= 25,\ -\dfrac{74}{3} \end{aligned}$$
$\therefore\ n=25$, since $n\in\mathbb{Z}^+$
$$\begin{aligned} x &= a+(n-1)d \\ &= 1+(25-1)(3) \\ &= 73 \end{aligned}$$
$$\therefore\ x=73$$','1: $S_n$ equation; 1: $n=25$; 1: $x=73$. Question done well. A few students forgot to work out $x$.',1365,NULL,'parse-v1',NULL,NULL,'[[3, "Correct answer"], [2, "Obtains $n=25$, or equivalent merit"], [1, "Forms the $S_n$ equation, or equivalent merit"]]','{"marker_note": "Question done well. A few students forgot to work out $x$.", "top_criterion_original": "$x=73$", "criteria_original": [[1, "$S_n$ equation"], [1, "$n=25$"], [1, "Correct answer"]], "rubric_note": "Rewritten top-down from a one-mark-per-step rubric (owner rule, 11 Nov)."}');
$$\begin{aligned} \dfrac{H-h}{H} &= \dfrac{\frac{x}{2}}{R} \\ x &= 2R\left(\dfrac{H-h}{H}\right) \\ &= 2R\left(1-\dfrac{h}{H}\right) \end{aligned}$$','1: correct ratio. Do NOT need any geometric reasoning. Be careful that the correct ratio is used.',1365,NULL,'parse-v1',NULL,NULL,'[[1, "Correct answer"]]','{"marker_note": "Be careful that the correct ratio is used.", "top_criterion_original": "Correct ratio. Do NOT need any geometric reasoning", "source_original": {"latex": "$$\\begin{aligned} \\dfrac{H-h}{H} &= \\dfrac{\\frac12x}{R} \\\\ x &= 2R\\left(\\dfrac{H-h}{H}\\right) \\\\ x &= 2R\\left(1-\\dfrac{h}{H}\\right) \\end{aligned}$$", "criteria": [[1, "Correct answer"]]}, "corrected": "Corrected from source: the question named the radius $r$ but part (a) and this working use $R$; the question now says $R$.", "explained": "Similar-triangle reasoning explained in words (owner request, 11 Nov)."}');
$$\dfrac{dV}{dh}=4R^2-\dfrac{16R^2h}{H}+\dfrac{12R^2h^2}{H^2}$$
For stationary points, $\dfrac{dV}{dh}=0$:
$$\begin{aligned} 4R^2\left(1-\dfrac{4h}{H}+\dfrac{3h^2}{H^2}\right) &= 0 \\ 4R^2\left(1-\dfrac{h}{H}\right)\left(1-\dfrac{3h}{H}\right) &= 0 \end{aligned}$$
$$\therefore\ h=H \text{ or } h=\dfrac{H}{3}$$
$$\dfrac{d^2V}{dh^2}=-\dfrac{16R^2}{H}+\dfrac{24R^2h}{H^2}$$
At $h=H$:
$$\dfrac{d^2V}{dh^2}=\dfrac{8R^2}{H}>0$$
$\therefore$ concave up.

At $h=\dfrac{H}{3}$:
$$\begin{aligned} \dfrac{d^2V}{dh^2} &= -\dfrac{16R^2}{H}+\dfrac{24R^2(\frac{H}{3})}{H^2} \\ &= -\dfrac{16R^2}{H}+\dfrac{8R^2}{H} \\ &= -\dfrac{8R^2}{H}<0 \end{aligned}$$
$\therefore$ concave down.

