i.e. $e^{-2x}-e^{-x}-k=0$ has 2 solutions

i.e. $\left(e^{-x}\right)^2-\left(e^{-x}\right)-k=0$ has 2 solutions.

Using the quadratic formula,
$$e^{-x}=\dfrac{1\pm\sqrt{1+4k}}{2}$$
For two real solutions we want $1+4k>0$, i.e. $k>-\dfrac{1}{4}$.

But for two solutions for $e^{-x}$, both the real solutions to the quadratic must be positive.
$$\begin{aligned}\therefore\ \sqrt{1+4k}&<1\\\therefore\ 1+4k&<1\\\therefore\ k&<0\end{aligned}$$
$$\therefore\ -\dfrac{1}{4}<k<0$$','3: Provides correct solution; 2: Uses the discriminant to find $k>-\dfrac{1}{4}$, or equivalent merit; 1: Attempts to form a quadratic equation, or equivalent merit',295,NULL,'parse-v1',NULL,NULL,'[[3, "Correct answer"], [2, "Uses the discriminant to find $k>-\\dfrac{1}{4}$, or equivalent merit"], [1, "Attempts to form a quadratic equation, or equivalent merit"]]','{"top_criterion_original": "Provides correct solution"}');

