$$\begin{aligned} x^3-2x &= x^2 \\ x^3-x^2-2x &= 0 \\ x(x^2-x-2) &= 0 \\ x(x+1)(x-2) &= 0 \end{aligned}$$

Hence the $x$-coordinate of $A$ is $-1$ and the $x$-coordinate of $B$ is $2$.

Area between the two curves is given by
$$\begin{aligned} \text{Area} &= \left|\int_{-1}^{0}x^3-x^2-2x\,dx\right|+\left|\int_0^2 x^3-x^2-2x\,dx\right| \\ &= \left|\left[\dfrac{x^4}{4}-\dfrac{x^3}{3}-x^2\right]_{-1}^{0}\right|+\left|\left[\dfrac{x^4}{4}-\dfrac{x^3}{3}-x^2\right]_0^2\right| \\ &= \left|\dfrac{1}{4}+\dfrac{1}{3}-1\right|+\left|4-\dfrac{8}{3}-4\right| \\ &= \dfrac{5}{12}+\dfrac{8}{3} \\ &= \dfrac{37}{12} \end{aligned}$$','4: Provides correct solution; 3: Correctly sets up a sum of two integrals, including correct bounds for each integral, and attempts to find the area between the two curves, or equivalent merit; 2: Finds the $x$-coordinates of $A$ and $B$, or equivalent merit; 1: Attempts to find the $x$-coordinates of $A$ and $B$ by equating the functions, or equivalent merit [reused from duplicate 67]',1346,NULL,'dup-reuse-v1',NULL,NULL,'[[4, "Correct answer"], [3, "Correctly sets up a sum of two integrals, including correct bounds for each integral, and attempts to find the area between the two curves, or equivalent merit"], [2, "Finds the $x$-coordinates of $A$ and $B$, or equivalent merit"], [1, "Attempts to find the $x$-coordinates of $A$ and $B$ by equating the functions, or equivalent merit"]]','{"reused_from": 67, "top_criterion_original": "Provides correct solution"}');
$$\begin{aligned} \int\dfrac{1-\ln x}{x\ln x}\,dx &= \int\dfrac{\frac{1-\ln x}{x^2}}{\frac{x\ln x}{x^2}}\,dx \\ &= \int\dfrac{\frac{1-\ln x}{x^2}}{\frac{\ln x}{x}}\,dx \end{aligned}$$

Using
$$\dfrac{d}{dx}\left(\dfrac{\ln x}{x}\right)=\dfrac{1-\ln x}{x^2}$$
and
$$\int\dfrac{f''(x)}{f(x)}\,dx=\ln|f(x)|+c$$

$$\int\dfrac{\frac{1-\ln x}{x^2}}{\frac{\ln x}{x}}\,dx\text{ is in the form }\int\dfrac{f''(x)}{f(x)}\,dx$$

Hence,
$$\begin{aligned} \int\dfrac{1-\ln x}{x\ln x}\,dx &= \ln\left|\dfrac{\ln x}{x}\right|+c \\ &= \ln|\ln x|-\ln|x|+c \end{aligned}$$','3: Provides correct solution; 2: Makes substantial progress toward correct solution, such as recognising that the integral is in the form $\int\frac{f''(x)}{f(x)}$ and hence will result in a log function by using the fact that $\frac{d}{dx}\left(\frac{\ln x}{x}\right)=\frac{1-\ln x}{x^2}$, or equivalent merit; 1: Divides numerator and denominator by $x^2$, or equivalent merit [reused from duplicate 69]',1346,NULL,'dup-reuse-v1',NULL,NULL,'[[3, "Correct answer"], [2, "Makes substantial progress toward correct solution, such as recognising that the integral is in the form $\\displaystyle \\int\\dfrac{f''(x)}{f(x)}$ and hence will result in a log function by using the fact that $\\dfrac{d}{dx}\\left(\\dfrac{\\ln x}{x}\\right)=\\dfrac{1-\\ln x}{x^2}$, or equivalent merit"], [1, "Divides numerator and denominator by $x^2$, or equivalent merit"]]','{"reused_from": 69, "top_criterion_original": "Provides correct solution", "source_original": {"latex": "Divide the numerator and denominator of the integrand by $x^2$ such that:\n$$\\begin{aligned} \\int\\dfrac{1-\\ln x}{x\\ln x}\\,dx &= \\int\\dfrac{\\frac{1-\\ln x}{x^2}}{\\frac{x\\ln x}{x^2}}\\,dx \\\\ &= \\int\\dfrac{\\frac{1-\\ln x}{x^2}}{\\frac{\\ln x}{x}}\\,dx \\end{aligned}$$\n\nUsing\n$$\\dfrac{d}{dx}\\left(\\dfrac{\\ln x}{x}\\right)=\\dfrac{1-\\ln x}{x^2}$$\nand\n$$\\int\\dfrac{f''(x)}{f(x)}\\,dx=\\ln x+c$$\n\n$$\\int\\dfrac{\\frac{1-\\ln x}{x^2}}{\\frac{\\ln x}{x}}\\,dx\\text{ is in the form }\\int\\dfrac{f''(x)}{f(x)}\\,dx$$\n\nHence,\n$$\\begin{aligned} \\int\\dfrac{1-\\ln x}{x\\ln x}\\,dx &= \\ln\\left|\\dfrac{\\ln x}{x}\\right|+c \\\\ &= \\ln|\\ln x|-\\ln|x|+c \\end{aligned}$$", "criteria": [[3, "Correct answer"], [2, "Makes substantial progress toward correct solution, such as recognising that the integral is in the form $\\displaystyle \\int\\dfrac{f''(x)}{f(x)}$ and hence will result in a log function by using the fact that $\\dfrac{d}{dx}\\left(\\dfrac{\\ln x}{x}\\right)=\\dfrac{1-\\ln x}{x^2}$, or equivalent merit"], [1, "Divides numerator and denominator by $x^2$, or equivalent merit"]]}, "corrected": "Corrected from source: the standard result was written $\\ln x+c$."}');
$$v = \int(-2t+3)\,dt = -t^2 + 3t + C$$
The particle is at rest at $t = 1$ and $t = 2$, so $v = 0$ there:
$$\begin{aligned} -1 + 3 + C &= 0 \\ C &= -2 \end{aligned}$$
Hence
$$v = -t^2 + 3t - 2 = -(t-1)(t-2)$$
Sign of $v$:
$$\begin{aligned} v &< 0 &&\text{for } 0 \le t < 1 &&\text{displacement decreasing}\\ v &> 0 &&\text{for } 1 < t < 2 &&\text{displacement increasing}\\ v &< 0 &&\text{for } t > 2 &&\text{displacement decreasing}\end{aligned}$$
So the displacement has a local minimum at $t = 1$ and a local maximum at $t = 2$.

Graph A is the only graph with a minimum at $t = 1$, a maximum at $t = 2$ and decreasing afterwards. (Graph B has the turning points the wrong way round; C and D do not have stationary points at both $t = 1$ and $t = 2$.)','1: v = -(t-1)(t-2), so x has a min at t=1 and a max at t=2 and decreases after t=2: graph A [confidence 0.95; check: passed]',NULL,'claude-opus-5-5 (subagent)','gen-v1','human:approved','2026-11-10T23:47:21.517Z','[[1, "Correct answer"]]','{"confidence": 0.95, "check": "passed"}');
