$$y''=\sec^2 x$$
When $x=c$: $y=\tan c$ and $y''=\sec^2 c$.

Equation of the tangent, using $y-y_1=m(x-x_1)$:
$$\begin{aligned} y-\tan c&=\sec^2 c\,(x-c)\\ y&=x\sec^2 c-c\sec^2 c+\tan c \end{aligned}$$','3: Correct proof; 2: Finds $y=\tan c$ and the gradient $\sec^2 c$, or equivalent merit; 1: Finds $y=\tan c$ or $y''=\sec^2 c$ at $x=c$, or equivalent merit',1370,NULL,'parse-v1',NULL,NULL,'[[3, "Correct answer"], [2, "Finds $y=\\tan c$ and the gradient $\\sec^2 c$, or equivalent merit"], [1, "Finds $y=\\tan c$ or $y''=\\sec^2 c$ at $x=c$, or equivalent merit"]]','{"concern": "transcribed from handwritten marking guidelines (scanned pages, no text layer)", "criteria_original": [[3, "Correct proof"], [2, "Finds $y=\\tan c$ and the gradient $\\sec^2 c$, or equivalent merit"], [1, "Finds $y=\\tan c$ or $y''=\\sec^2 c$ at $x=c$, or equivalent merit"]], "top_criterion_original": "Correct proof"}');
$$\begin{aligned} -c&=-c\sec^2 c+\tan c\\ -c&=-c(1+\tan^2 c)+\tan c\\ -c&=-c-c\tan^2 c+\tan c\\ c\tan^2 c-\tan c&=0\\ \tan c\,(c\tan c-1)&=0 \end{aligned}$$
$\tan c=0$ gives $c=0$, not possible since $c\in\left(0,\dfrac{\pi}{2}\right)$. So $\tan c=\dfrac{1}{c}$.
$$\begin{aligned} \tan^2 c&=\dfrac{1}{c^2}\\ 1+\tan^2 c&=\dfrac{1}{c^2}+1\\ \sec^2 c&=\dfrac{1+c^2}{c^2} \end{aligned}$$
$$\therefore\ \text{gradient}=\dfrac{c^2+1}{c^2}$$
Alternative: from $\tan c=\dfrac{1}{c}$ and the points $(0,-c)$, $(c,\tan c)$,
$$\begin{aligned} m&=\dfrac{\tan c+c}{c-0}\\ &=\dfrac{\frac{1}{c}+c}{c}\\ &=\dfrac{1+c^2}{c}\times\dfrac{1}{c}\\ &=\dfrac{1+c^2}{c^2} \end{aligned}$$','1: Correct proof (finding $\tan c=\dfrac{1}{c}$ is required)',1370,NULL,'parse-v1',NULL,NULL,'[[1, "Correct answer"]]','{"concern": "transcribed from handwritten marking guidelines (scanned pages, no text layer)", "criteria_original": [[1, "Correct proof (finding $\\tan c=\\dfrac{1}{c}$ is required)"]], "top_criterion_original": "Correct proof (finding $\\tan c=\\dfrac{1}{c}$ is required)", "marker_note": "Finding $\\tan c=\\dfrac{1}{c}$ is required."}');
$$P(t)=200-44\left(1+10e^{-0.2t}\right)^{-1}$$
Chain rule:
$$\begin{aligned} P''(t)&=44\left(-2e^{-0.2t}\right)\left(1+10e^{-0.2t}\right)^{-2}\\ &=\dfrac{-88e^{-0.2t}}{\left(1+10e^{-0.2t}\right)^2} \end{aligned}$$','2: Correct proof; 1: Some correct differentiation, or equivalent merit',1370,NULL,'parse-v1',NULL,NULL,'[[2, "Correct answer"], [1, "Some correct differentiation, or equivalent merit"]]','{"concern": "transcribed from handwritten marking guidelines (scanned pages, no text layer); guideline gives no breakdown of the 2 marks", "criteria_original": [[2, "Correct proof"], [1, "Some correct differentiation, or equivalent merit"]], "top_criterion_original": "Correct proof"}');
$$\begin{aligned} P(0)&=200-\dfrac{44}{1+10e^0}\\ &=196 \end{aligned}$$
As $t\to\infty$, $e^{-0.2t}\to 0$, so $P(t)\to 200-44=156$.
$$\therefore\ 156<P(t)\le 196$$','2: Correct answer; 1: Correct lower bound $156<P(t)$ or upper bound $P(t)\le 196$',1370,NULL,'parse-v1',NULL,NULL,'[[2, "Correct answer"], [1, "Correct lower bound $156<P(t)$ or upper bound $P(t)\\le 196$, or equivalent merit"]]','{"concern": "transcribed from handwritten marking guidelines (scanned pages, no text layer)", "criteria_original": [[2, "Correct answer"], [1, "Correct lower bound $156<P(t)$ or upper bound $P(t)\\le 196$"]]}');
$$\begin{aligned} P''(t)&=\dfrac{-88e^{-0.2t}}{\left(1+10e^{-0.2t}\right)^2}\\ &=\dfrac{2e^{-0.2t}}{1+10e^{-0.2t}}\times\dfrac{-44}{1+10e^{-0.2t}}\\ &=\dfrac{2e^{-0.2t}}{1+10e^{-0.2t}}\times(P-200)\\ &=\dfrac{1}{220}\left(\dfrac{440e^{-0.2t}}{1+10e^{-0.2t}}\right)(P-200)\\ &=\dfrac{1}{220}(P-200)\left(\dfrac{44+440e^{-0.2t}-44}{1+10e^{-0.2t}}\right)\\ &=\dfrac{1}{220}(P-200)\left(\dfrac{44(1+10e^{-0.2t})}{1+10e^{-0.2t}}-\dfrac{44}{1+10e^{-0.2t}}\right)\\ &=\dfrac{1}{220}(P-200)\left(44-\dfrac{44}{1+10e^{-0.2t}}\right)\\ &=\dfrac{1}{220}(P-200)\left(200-\dfrac{44}{1+10e^{-0.2t}}-156\right)\\ &=\dfrac{1}{220}(P-200)(P-156) \end{aligned}$$','2: Correct proof with all steps correct, without manipulating the equation to be proven',1370,NULL,'parse-v1',NULL,NULL,'[[2, "Correct answer"]]','{"concern": "transcribed from handwritten marking guidelines (scanned pages, no text layer)", "criteria_original": [[2, "Correct proof with all steps correct, without manipulating the equation to be proven"]], "top_criterion_original": "Correct proof with all steps correct, without manipulating the equation to be proven", "marker_note": "The proof must not manipulate the equation to be proven. If $P=200-\\dfrac{44}{1+10e^{-0.2t}}$ is substituted into $\\dfrac{1}{220}(P-156)(P-200)$, 2 marks are awarded only if all steps are completely correct."}');
$$P''(t)=\dfrac{1}{220}(P-156)(P-200)$$
Its graph against $P$ is a concave-up parabola with $P$-intercepts $156$ and $200$, so the minimum value of $P''(t)$ (fastest decrease) occurs midway between them:
$$P=\dfrac{156+200}{2}=178$$
$\therefore$ the population is decreasing at the fastest rate when $P=178$.','1: Correct answer',1370,NULL,'parse-v1',NULL,NULL,'[[1, "Correct answer"]]','{"concern": "transcribed from handwritten marking guidelines (scanned pages, no text layer)"}');

