For the $x$ intercepts, $y=0$:
$$\begin{aligned} 0&=x^3-12x^2+36x\\ 0&=x(x^2-12x+36)\\ 0&=x(x-6)^2 \end{aligned}$$
$\therefore$ the $x$ intercepts are $x=0$ and $x=6$.','2: Correct answer. 1: Finds the x or y intercepts',1363,NULL,'parse-v1',NULL,NULL,'[[2, "Correct answer"], [1, "Finds the $x$ or $y$ intercepts, or equivalent merit"]]','{"criteria_original": [[2, "Correct answer. 1: Finds the x or y intercepts"]], "top_criterion_original": "Correct answer. 1: Finds the x or y intercepts"}');
Stationary points occur when $y''=0$:
$$\begin{aligned} 3x^2-24x+36&=0\\ x^2-8x+12&=0\\ (x-2)(x-6)&=0\\ x&=2\text{ or }x=6 \end{aligned}$$
For $x=2$:
$$\begin{aligned} y&=2^3-12(2)^2+36(2)\\ &=32\\ y''''&=6(2)-24\\ &=-12<0 \end{aligned}$$
so the curve is concave down at $x=2$.

For $x=6$:
$$\begin{aligned} y&=6^3-12(6)^2+36(6)\\ &=0\\ y''''&=6(6)-24\\ &=12>0 \end{aligned}$$
so the curve is concave up at $x=6$.

$\therefore$ $(2,32)$ is a local maximum and $(6,0)$ is a local minimum.','3: Correct answer. 2: Finds the location of the two stationary points. 1: Finds dy/dx and equates it to zero.',1363,NULL,'parse-v1',NULL,NULL,'[[3, "Correct answer"], [2, "Finds the location of the two stationary points, or equivalent merit"], [1, "Finds $\\dfrac{dy}{dx}$ and equates it to zero, or equivalent merit"]]','{"criteria_original": [[3, "Correct answer. 2: Finds the location of the two stationary points. 1: Finds dy/dx and equates it to zero."]], "top_criterion_original": "Correct answer. 2: Finds the location of the two stationary points. 1: Finds dy/dx and equates it to zero."}');
