If all $10$ contributions were $\$750$, the future value (table: rate $0.01$, period $10$) would be
$$750\times10.4622=\$7846.65$$
Doubling a contribution adds another $\$750$, so if the last $m$ contributions are doubled, the extra amount is $\$750$ times the future value of an annuity of $\$1$ for $m$ periods, $\text{FV}_m$. We need
$$\begin{aligned} 7846.65+750\times\text{FV}_m&\ge14\,000\\ \text{FV}_m&\ge\dfrac{14\,000-7846.65}{750}\\ \text{FV}_m&\ge8.2045\end{aligned}$$
From the table, $\text{FV}_8=8.2857\ge8.2045$, while $\text{FV}_7=\dfrac{1.01^7-1}{0.01}\approx7.2135<8.2045$.

So at least the last $8$ contributions must be doubled, leaving at most $10-8=2$ contributions of $\$750$.

Check: $750\times10.4622+750\times8.2857=\$14\,060.93\ge\$14\,000$.
$$\therefore\ \text{maximum number of } \$750 \text{ contributions}=2$$',NULL,1632,'claude-opus-5-5','gen-v2',NULL,NULL,'[[4, "Correct answer"], [3, "Uses the table to find that at least $8$ contributions must be doubled, or equivalent merit"], [2, "Forms a correct inequality or equation for the future value of the mixed contributions, or equivalent merit"], [1, "Uses $r=0.01$ and $n=10$ with the table, or equivalent merit"]]','{"confidence": 0.9, "check": "Python: total FV with n contributions of 750 then 1500: n=2 gives 14060.91, n=3 gives 13256.81 (end-of-period). Annuity-due timing also gives n=2.", "concern": "Question wording (contributions at start or end of each period, whether ''number of payments'' counts only the $750 ones) is slightly ambiguous; all readings tried give 2."}');
