By the chain rule, $\dfrac{d}{dx}e^{g(x)} = g''(x)e^{g(x)}$:
$$\begin{aligned} f''(x) &= (15x^2 - 10)e^{5x^3 - 10x} \\ &= 5(3x^2 - 2)e^{5x^3 - 10x} \end{aligned}$$
Since $e^{5x^3 - 10x} > 0$ for all $x$, only the factor $3x^2 - 2$ decides the sign, so we need
$$3x^2 - 2 > 0$$
The roots of $3x^2 - 2 = 0$ are $x = \pm\sqrt{\dfrac{2}{3}}$, and the parabola is positive outside its roots:
$$\therefore\ x \in \left(-\infty, -\sqrt{\dfrac{2}{3}}\right) \cup \left(\sqrt{\dfrac{2}{3}}, \infty\right)$$','3: Correct answer; 2: Reduces to $3x^2 - 2 > 0$, or equivalent merit; 1: Correct derivative, or equivalent merit',1357,NULL,'parse-v1',NULL,NULL,'[[3, "Correct answer"], [2, "Reduces to $3x^2 - 2 > 0$, or equivalent merit"], [1, "Correct derivative, or equivalent merit"]]','{"concern": "transcribed from handwritten solutions copy (images, no text layer)"}');
