$$\sin\alpha = \dfrac{y}{d}$$
Using Pythagoras'' theorem:
$$\begin{aligned} d^2 &= y^2 + a^2 \\ d &= \left(y^2 + a^2\right)^{\frac{1}{2}} \quad \text{as } d > 0 \end{aligned}$$
Substitute into the given formula:
$$\begin{aligned} I &= \dfrac{k\sin\alpha}{d^2} \\ &= \dfrac{k \times \frac{y}{d}}{d^2} \\ &= \dfrac{ky}{d^3} \\ &= \dfrac{ky}{\left(y^2 + a^2\right)^{\frac{3}{2}}} \end{aligned}$$
as required.','3: Correct $\sin\alpha$, correct $d$, correctly showing $I$; 2: Two of the above (1 error); 1: One of the above',1359,NULL,'parse-v1',NULL,NULL,'[[3, "Correct answer"], [2, "Two of: correct $\\sin\\alpha$, correct $d$, correctly shows $I$ (or 1 error), or equivalent merit"], [1, "One of the above, or equivalent merit"]]','{"concern": "transcribed from handwritten solutions copy (images, no text layer)", "criteria_original": [[3, "Correct $\\sin\\alpha$, correct $d$, correctly showing $I$"], [2, "Two of the above (1 error)"], [1, "One of the above"]], "top_criterion_original": "Correct $\\sin\\alpha$, correct $d$, correctly showing $I$"}');
$$\begin{aligned} u&=ky & v&=\left(y^2 + a^2\right)^{\frac{3}{2}}\\ u''&=k & v''&=3y\left(y^2 + a^2\right)^{\frac{1}{2}}\end{aligned}$$
$$\begin{aligned} \dfrac{dI}{dy} &= \dfrac{k\left(y^2 + a^2\right)^{\frac{3}{2}} - ky \times 3y\left(y^2 + a^2\right)^{\frac{1}{2}}}{\left(\left(y^2 + a^2\right)^{\frac{3}{2}}\right)^2} \\ &= \dfrac{k\left(y^2 + a^2\right)^{\frac{1}{2}}\left[\left(y^2 + a^2\right) - 3y^2\right]}{\left(y^2 + a^2\right)^3} \\ &= \dfrac{k\left(y^2 + a^2\right)^{\frac{1}{2}}\left(a^2 - 2y^2\right)}{\left(y^2 + a^2\right)^3} \\ &= \dfrac{k\left(a^2 - 2y^2\right)}{\left(y^2 + a^2\right)^{\frac{5}{2}}} \end{aligned}$$
Stationary points occur when $\dfrac{dI}{dy} = 0$:
$$\begin{aligned} k\left(a^2 - 2y^2\right) &= 0 \\ 2y^2 &= a^2 \\ y^2 &= \dfrac{a^2}{2} \\ y &= \dfrac{a}{\sqrt{2}} \quad \text{as } y > 0 \end{aligned}$$
Only $a^2-2y^2$ decides the sign of $\dfrac{dI}{dy}$. Testing either side:

\extable{Ts1158_1}

$\therefore$ The intensity is a maximum when the light is at height $y = \dfrac{a}{\sqrt{2}}$ m.','3: Correct derivative, correctly finding $y$, testing for maximum; 2: Two of the above (or 1 error); 1: One of the above',1359,NULL,'parse-v1',NULL,NULL,'[[3, "Correct answer"], [2, "Two of: correct derivative, correctly finds $y$, tests for a maximum (or 1 error), or equivalent merit"], [1, "One of the above, or equivalent merit"]]','{"concern": "transcribed from handwritten solutions copy (images, no text layer)", "criteria_original": [[3, "Correct derivative, correctly finding $y$, testing for maximum"], [2, "Two of the above (or 1 error)"], [1, "One of the above"]], "top_criterion_original": "Correct derivative, correctly finding $y$, testing for maximum"}');
