Stationary points occur when $y''=0$:
$$\begin{aligned}3x^2-6x&=0\\3x(x-2)&=0\\x&=0\ \text{or}\ x=2\end{aligned}$$
For the nature of the stationary points, consider the sign of $y''''$:
$$\begin{aligned} x&=0: && y''''=-6<0,\ y=0 && \text{maximum}\\ x&=2: && y''''=6>0,\ y=-4 && \text{minimum}\end{aligned}$$
$\therefore$ Maximum turning point at $(0,0)$.

$\therefore$ Minimum turning point at $(2,-4)$.','3: Provides correct solution; 2: Provides correct x coordinates AND correct expression for y'''', or equivalent merit; 1: Provides correct x coordinates OR correct expression for y'''', or equivalent merit',1361,NULL,'parse-v1',NULL,NULL,'[[3, "Correct answer"], [2, "Provides correct $x$-coordinates and correct expression for $y''''$, or equivalent merit"], [1, "Provides correct $x$-coordinates or correct expression for $y''''$, or equivalent merit"]]','{"source_original": "(final line) maximum turning point at $x=\\dfrac{1}{3}$", "corrected": "Corrected from source: removed the stray final line \"maximum turning point at $x=\\dfrac{1}{3}$\"; the stationary points are $(0,0)$ and $(2,-4)$.", "criteria_original": [[3, "Provides correct solution"], [2, "Provides correct x coordinates AND correct expression for y'''', or equivalent merit"], [1, "Provides correct x coordinates OR correct expression for y'''', or equivalent merit"]], "top_criterion_original": "Provides correct solution"}');
