$$\begin{aligned} \text{RHS}&=\dfrac{\operatorname{cosec}A}{\operatorname{cosec}A-\sin A}\\ &=\operatorname{cosec}A\div\left(\operatorname{cosec}A-\sin A\right)\\ &=\dfrac{1}{\sin A}\div\left(\dfrac{1}{\sin A}-\sin A\right)\\ &=\dfrac{1}{\sin A}\div\dfrac{1-\sin^2A}{\sin A} \end{aligned}$$
Since $\cos^2A+\sin^2A=1$, $\cos^2A=1-\sin^2A$:
$$\begin{aligned} \text{RHS}&=\dfrac{1}{\sin A}\times\dfrac{\sin A}{\cos^2A}\\ &=\dfrac{1}{\cos^2A}\\ &=\sec^2A\\ &=\text{LHS} \end{aligned}$$
Or, using $\sec^2A=1+\tan^2A$:
$$\begin{aligned} \text{LHS}&=\sec^2A\\ &=1+\tan^2A\\ &=1+\dfrac{\sin^2A}{\cos^2A}\\ &=1+\dfrac{\sin^2A}{1-\sin^2A}\\ &=\dfrac{1-\sin^2A+\sin^2A}{1-\sin^2A}\\ &=\dfrac{1}{1-\sin^2A}\\ &=\dfrac{\frac{1}{\sin A}}{\frac{1}{\sin A}-\frac{\sin^2A}{\sin A}}\\ &=\dfrac{\operatorname{cosec}A}{\operatorname{cosec}A-\sin A}\\ &=\text{RHS} \end{aligned}$$','Marks not itemised on the copy (2 marks): correct manipulation using $\cos^2A=1-\sin^2A$; complete proof',1362,NULL,'parse-v1',NULL,NULL,'[[2, "Correct answer"], [1, "Correct manipulation using $\\cos^2A=1-\\sin^2A$, or equivalent merit"]]','{"criteria_original": [[null, "Marks not itemised on the copy (2 marks): correct manipulation using $\\cos^2A=1-\\sin^2A$; complete proof"]], "top_criterion_original": "Marks not itemised on the copy (2 marks): correct manipulation using $\\cos^2A=1-\\sin^2A$; complete proof", "rubric_note": "Source gives 2 marks without itemising them (manipulation using cos^2 A = 1 - sin^2 A; complete proof); written top-down."}');
$$\begin{aligned} (x-4)(y-2)&=20\\ y-2&=\dfrac{20}{x-4}\\ y&=\dfrac{20}{x-4}+2\\ &=\dfrac{20+2(x-4)}{x-4}\\ &=\dfrac{20+2x-8}{x-4}\\ &=\dfrac{12+2x}{x-4} \end{aligned}$$
$$\begin{aligned} A&=x\times y\\ &=x\times\dfrac{12+2x}{x-4}\\ &=\dfrac{12x+2x^2}{x-4}\\ &=\dfrac{2x^2+12x}{x-4} \end{aligned}$$
Or let the width of the photo be $y$, so $(x-4)\times y=20$ and $y=\dfrac{20}{x-4}$:
$$\begin{aligned} A&=x(y+2)\\ &=x\left(\dfrac{20}{x-4}+2\right)\\ &=x\times\dfrac{20+2(x-4)}{x-4}\\ &=x\times\dfrac{20+2x-8}{x-4}\\ &=\dfrac{x(12+2x)}{x-4}\\ &=\dfrac{12x+2x^2}{x-4}\\ &=\dfrac{2x^2+12x}{x-4} \end{aligned}$$','1: expression for the width in terms of $x$; 1: completes the show',1362,NULL,'parse-v1',NULL,NULL,'[[2, "Correct answer"], [1, "Finds an expression for the width in terms of $x$, or equivalent merit"]]','{"criteria_original": [[1, "expression for the width in terms of $x$"], [1, "completes the show"]], "top_criterion_original": "expression for the width in terms of $x$", "rubric_note": "Rewritten top-down from a one-mark-per-step rubric (owner rule, 11 Nov)."}');
$$\begin{aligned} u&=2x^2+12x & v&=x-4\\ u''&=4x+12 & v''&=1\end{aligned}$$
$$\begin{aligned} \dfrac{dA}{dx}&=\dfrac{(x-4)(4x+12)-(2x^2+12x)\times 1}{(x-4)^2}\\ &=\dfrac{4x^2+12x-16x-48-2x^2-12x}{(x-4)^2}\\ &=\dfrac{2x^2-16x-48}{(x-4)^2}\\ &=\dfrac{2(x^2-8x-24)}{(x-4)^2} \end{aligned}$$
The minimum or maximum area occurs when $\dfrac{dA}{dx}=0$:
$$x^2-8x-24=0$$
Using the quadratic formula:
$$\begin{aligned} x&=\dfrac{8\pm\sqrt{64-4\times 1\times(-24)}}{2}\\ &=\dfrac{8\pm\sqrt{64+96}}{2}\\ &=\dfrac{8\pm\sqrt{160}}{2}\\ &=\dfrac{8\pm 4\sqrt{10}}{2}\\ &=4\pm 2\sqrt{10} \end{aligned}$$
$x>0$, so $x=4+2\sqrt{10}\approx 10.32$.

Testing the sign of $\dfrac{dA}{dx}$ either side:

\extable{Ts1241_1}

$\therefore$ The area is a minimum when $x=4+2\sqrt{10}$ cm.','1: correct derivative (quotient rule); 1: solves $\dfrac{dA}{dx}=0$; 1: $x=4+2\sqrt{10}$ (discarding the negative solution); 1: first-derivative test shows a minimum',1362,NULL,'parse-v1',NULL,NULL,'[[4, "Correct answer"], [3, "Obtains $x=4+2\\sqrt{10}$ (discarding the negative solution), or equivalent merit"], [2, "Solves $\\dfrac{dA}{dx}=0$, or equivalent merit"], [1, "Finds the correct derivative (quotient rule), or equivalent merit"]]','{"criteria_original": [[1, "correct derivative (quotient rule)"], [1, "solves $\\dfrac{dA}{dx}=0$"], [1, "$x=4+2\\sqrt{10}$ (discarding the negative solution)"], [1, "first-derivative test shows a minimum"]], "top_criterion_original": "correct derivative (quotient rule)", "rubric_note": "Rewritten top-down from a one-mark-per-step rubric (owner rule, 11 Nov).", "marker_note": "First-derivative test needed to show a minimum.", "source_original": "$\\therefore$ Minimum area when $x=\\left(4+2\\sqrt{10}\\right)$ m", "corrected": "Corrected from source: the unit of x is cm (the frame is measured in cm), not m."}');
