$$\begin{aligned} y_1&=2\times 0\times e^0=0\\ y_2&=2\times\dfrac{1}{2}\times e^{\frac{1}{2}}=e^{\frac{1}{2}}\\ y_3&=2\times 1\times e^1=2e \end{aligned}$$
Using the trapezoidal rule, $\displaystyle\int_a^b f(x)\,dx\approx\dfrac{b-a}{2n}\left[f(a)+f(b)+2f(x_1)\right]$:
$$\begin{aligned} A&\approx\dfrac{1-0}{2\times 2}\left[0+2e+2\times e^{\frac{1}{2}}\right]\\ &=\dfrac{1}{4}\left[2e+2e^{\frac{1}{2}}\right]\\ &\approx 2.1835\\ &\approx 2.18 \text{ (3 sig. fig.)} \end{aligned}$$','1: correct function values and $h$; 1: $2.18$',1362,NULL,'parse-v1',NULL,NULL,'[[2, "Correct answer"], [1, "Finds the correct function values and $h$, or equivalent merit"]]','{"criteria_original": [[1, "correct function values and $h$"], [1, "$2.18$"]], "top_criterion_original": "correct function values and $h$", "rubric_note": "Rewritten top-down from a one-mark-per-step rubric (owner rule, 11 Nov)."}');

\exfigure{1362_q26_s1}','1: concave up, with reason',1362,NULL,'parse-v1',NULL,NULL,'[[1, "Correct answer"]]','{"criteria_original": [[1, "concave up, with reason"]], "top_criterion_original": "concave up, with reason", "marker_note": "Concave up, with a reason; not ''positive concavity''."}');
$$\int_0^a(3\sin 2a-3\sin 2x)\,dx=\int_a^{\frac{\pi}{4}}(3\sin 2x-3\sin 2a)\,dx$$
$$\begin{aligned} \left[3x\sin 2a-\left(-\dfrac{3}{2}\cos 2x\right)\right]_0^a&=\left[-\dfrac{3}{2}\cos 2x-3x\sin 2a\right]_a^{\frac{\pi}{4}}\\ 3a\sin 2a+\dfrac{3}{2}\cos 2a-\left(0+\dfrac{3}{2}\cos 0\right)&=-\dfrac{3}{2}\cos\dfrac{\pi}{2}-\dfrac{3\pi}{4}\sin 2a-\left(-\dfrac{3}{2}\cos 2a-3a\sin 2a\right)\\ 3a\sin 2a+\dfrac{3}{2}\cos 2a-\dfrac{3}{2}&=-\dfrac{3}{2}\times 0-\dfrac{3\pi}{4}\sin 2a+\dfrac{3}{2}\cos 2a+3a\sin 2a\\ -\dfrac{3}{2}&=-\dfrac{3\pi}{4}\sin 2a\\ -6&=-3\pi\sin 2a\\ \sin 2a&=\dfrac{6}{3\pi}=\dfrac{2}{\pi}\\ 2a&=\sin^{-1}\left(\dfrac{2}{\pi}\right)=0.6901070914\\ a&=0.3450535457\\ &=0.345 \text{ (3 d.p.)} \end{aligned}$$
Alternative (direct): $3\sin 2a$ is a constant, so
$$\int_0^{\frac{\pi}{4}}3\sin 2x\,dx=\int_0^{\frac{\pi}{4}}3\sin 2a\,dx$$
$$\begin{aligned} \left[-\dfrac{3}{2}\cos 2x\right]_0^{\frac{\pi}{4}}&=\Big[3x\sin 2a\Big]_0^{\frac{\pi}{4}}\\ \dfrac{3}{2}&=\dfrac{3\pi}{4}\sin 2a\\ a&=0.345 \end{aligned}$$','1: correct area statement; 1: correct integration; 1: $\sin 2a=\dfrac{2}{\pi}$; 1: $a=0.345$',1362,NULL,'parse-v1',NULL,NULL,'[[4, "Correct answer"], [3, "Obtains $\\sin 2a=\\dfrac{2}{\\pi}$, or equivalent merit"], [2, "Integrates correctly, or equivalent merit"], [1, "Writes a correct area statement, or equivalent merit"]]','{"criteria_original": [[1, "correct area statement"], [1, "correct integration"], [1, "$\\sin 2a=\\dfrac{2}{\\pi}$"], [1, "$a=0.345$"]], "top_criterion_original": "correct area statement", "rubric_note": "Rewritten top-down from a one-mark-per-step rubric (owner rule, 11 Nov).", "marker_note": "Students who got the correct answer without considering the area were awarded 3 out of 4."}');
$$\begin{aligned} y-1&=\dfrac{k}{x-1}\\ y&=1+\dfrac{k}{x-1} \end{aligned}$$
The curve passes through $(0,0)$:
$$\begin{aligned} 0&=1+\dfrac{k}{-1}\\ k&=1 \end{aligned}$$
$$\begin{aligned} y&=1+\dfrac{1}{x-1}\\ &=\dfrac{x-1+1}{x-1}\\ &=\dfrac{x}{x-1} \end{aligned}$$
Checking the asymptotes:
$$\lim_{x\to\infty}\left(1+\dfrac{1}{x-1}\right)=1+0=1$$
so the horizontal asymptote is $y=1$.

The domain is $x\ne 1$, so the vertical asymptote is $x=1$.','1: uses the asymptotes / transformation of $y=\dfrac{1}{x}$; 1: justifies the equation including the asymptotes',1362,NULL,'parse-v1',NULL,NULL,'[[2, "Correct answer"], [1, "Uses the asymptotes or a transformation of $y=\\dfrac{1}{x}$, or equivalent merit"]]','{"criteria_original": [[1, "uses the asymptotes / transformation of $y=\\dfrac{1}{x}$"], [1, "justifies the equation including the asymptotes"]], "top_criterion_original": "uses the asymptotes / transformation of $y=\\dfrac{1}{x}$", "rubric_note": "Rewritten top-down from a one-mark-per-step rubric (owner rule, 11 Nov).", "marker_note": "Stating $x\\ne 1$, $y\\ne 1$ without justification is not sufficient."}');
$$\begin{aligned} x&=mx(x-1)\\ x&=mx^2-mx\\ 0&=mx^2-mx-x\\ 0&=mx^2-(m+1)x \end{aligned}$$
$$\begin{aligned} \Delta&=b^2-4ac\\ &=\left(-(m+1)\right)^2-4\times m\times 0\\ &=(m+1)^2 \end{aligned}$$
$\Delta=0$ when $m=-1$.

Calculus approach: the curve passes through $(0,0)$, so the tangent through the origin touches at $x=0$. Using the quotient rule, $\dfrac{d}{dx}\left(\dfrac{u}{v}\right)=\dfrac{u''v-uv''}{v^2}$, with
$$\begin{aligned} u&=x & v&=x-1\\ u''&=1 & v''&=1\end{aligned}$$
$$\begin{aligned} f''(x)&=\dfrac{1(x-1)-x\cdot 1}{(x-1)^2}\\ &=\dfrac{-1}{(x-1)^2}\\ m&=f''(0)=\dfrac{-1}{(-1)^2}=-1 \end{aligned}$$','1: sets up the quadratic and discriminant (or derivative); 1: $m=-1$',1362,NULL,'parse-v1',NULL,NULL,'[[2, "Correct answer"], [1, "Sets up the quadratic and discriminant (or derivative), or equivalent merit"]]','{"criteria_original": [[1, "sets up the quadratic and discriminant (or derivative)"], [1, "$m=-1$"]], "top_criterion_original": "sets up the quadratic and discriminant (or derivative)", "rubric_note": "Rewritten top-down from a one-mark-per-step rubric (owner rule, 11 Nov).", "marker_note": "The calculus approach is only correct because $f(x)$ passes through $(0,0)$; students using calculus correctly got full marks."}');
$$\begin{aligned} 7&=8+4a+2b-4\\ 3&=4a+2b &&(1) \end{aligned}$$
$y''=3x^2+2ax+b$. The normal $y=-\dfrac{x}{2}+c$ has gradient $m_N=-\dfrac{1}{2}$, so the tangent gradient at $(2,7)$ is $m_T=2$:
$$\begin{aligned} 2&=3\times 2^2+2a\times 2+b\\ 2&=12+4a+b\\ -10&=4a+b &&(2) \end{aligned}$$
$(1)-(2)$:
$$b=13$$
Substituting into (1):
$$\begin{aligned} 3&=4a+2\times 13\\ 4a&=3-26=-23\\ a&=-\dfrac{23}{4}=-5\dfrac{3}{4} \end{aligned}$$
$(2,7)$ is on $y=-\dfrac{x}{2}+c$:
$$\begin{aligned} 7&=-\dfrac{2}{2}+c\\ 7&=-1+c\\ c&=8 \end{aligned}$$
$$\therefore\ a=-5\dfrac{3}{4},\quad b=13,\quad c=8$$','Marks not itemised on the copy (4 marks): equation from the point; gradient of tangent $m_T=2$ and second equation; $a$ and $b$; $c=8$',1362,NULL,'parse-v1',NULL,NULL,'[[4, "Correct answer"], [3, "Finds $a$ and $b$, or equivalent merit"], [2, "Finds the tangent gradient $m_T=2$ and forms the second equation, or equivalent merit"], [1, "Forms the equation from the point $(2,7)$, or equivalent merit"]]','{"criteria_original": [[null, "Marks not itemised on the copy (4 marks): equation from the point; gradient of tangent $m_T=2$ and second equation; $a$ and $b$; $c=8$"]], "top_criterion_original": "Marks not itemised on the copy (4 marks): equation from the point; gradient of tangent $m_T=2$ and second equation; $a$ and $b$; $c=8$", "source_original": "$$2=3\\times 2^2+2a\\times 2+b\\implies 2=12+4a+b\\implies -10=4a+2b \\quad (2)$$", "corrected": "Corrected from source: equation (2) is -10 = 4a + b (from 2 = 12 + 4a + b), not -10 = 4a + 2b; the later steps (b = 13) already use 4a + b.", "rubric_note": "Source gives 4 marks without itemising them (equation from the point; tangent gradient and second equation; a and b; c = 8); written top-down in that order."}');
Stationary points occur when $y''=0$:
$$\begin{aligned} 0&=\dfrac{x^2}{2}+\dfrac{x}{2}-3\\ &=\dfrac{1}{2}(x^2+x-6)\\ &=\dfrac{1}{2}(x+3)(x-2) \end{aligned}$$
$$\begin{aligned} x=-3&: &y&=\dfrac{-27}{6}+\dfrac{9}{4}+9+1=\dfrac{31}{4}=7\dfrac{3}{4}\\ x=2&: &y&=\dfrac{8}{6}+\dfrac{4}{4}-6+1=-\dfrac{8}{3}=-2\dfrac{2}{3} \end{aligned}$$
Testing the nature with $y''''=x+\dfrac{1}{2}$:
$$\begin{aligned} x&=-3: &y''''&=-3+\dfrac{1}{2}=-2\dfrac{1}{2}<0 &&\text{maximum turning point at } \left(-3,\,7\dfrac{3}{4}\right)\\ x&=2: &y''''&=2+\dfrac{1}{2}=2\dfrac{1}{2}>0 &&\text{minimum turning point at } \left(2,\,-2\dfrac{2}{3}\right) \end{aligned}$$
Possible points of inflexion occur when $y''''=0$:
$$\begin{aligned} x+\dfrac{1}{2}&=0\\ x&=-\dfrac{1}{2} \end{aligned}$$
$$\begin{aligned} y&=\dfrac{-1}{8\times 6}+\dfrac{1}{4\times 4}-3\left(-\dfrac{1}{2}\right)+1\\ &=-\dfrac{1}{48}+\dfrac{1}{16}+\dfrac{3}{2}+1\\ &=\dfrac{61}{24}=2\dfrac{13}{24}\approx 2.5416 \end{aligned}$$
Testing the concavity either side:

\extable{Ts1250_1}

There is a change in concavity, $\therefore$ there is a point of inflexion at $\left(-\dfrac{1}{2},\,2\dfrac{13}{24}\right)$.

The $y$-intercept occurs when $x=0$: $y=0+0-0+1=1$.

