$$\begin{aligned} y&=\lim_{x\to\infty}f(x)\\ &=\lim_{x\to\infty}\dfrac{2x^2}{x^2-4}\\ &=\lim_{x\to\infty}\dfrac{\frac{2x^2}{x^2}}{\frac{x^2}{x^2}-\frac{4}{x^2}}\\ &=\lim_{x\to\infty}\dfrac{2}{1-\frac{4}{x^2}}\\ &=\dfrac{2}{1-0}\\ &=2 \end{aligned}$$','1: Correctly uses limits to show that the horizontal asymptote is at $y=2$',1364,NULL,'parse-v1',NULL,NULL,'[[1, "Correct answer"]]','{"criteria_original": [[1, "Correctly uses limits to show that the horizontal asymptote is at $y=2$"]], "top_criterion_original": "Correctly uses limits to show that the horizontal asymptote is at $y=2$", "marker_note": "All students were given a mark for this question due to its difficulty and the vagueness of the syllabus."}');
