$$\begin{aligned}\int_{-a}^{a}\dfrac{e^x-e^{-x}}{e^x+e^{-x}}\,dx&=\Big[\ln\left(e^x+e^{-x}\right)\Big]_{-a}^{a}\\ &=\ln\left(e^a+e^{-a}\right)-\ln\left(e^{-a}+e^a\right)\\ &=0\end{aligned}$$','2: Correct integral and substitution to get final answer; 1: Correct integral but no final answer or equivalent',1364,NULL,'parse-v1',NULL,NULL,'[[2, "Correct answer"], [1, "Correct integral but no final answer, or equivalent merit"]]','{"criteria_original": [[2, "Correct integral and substitution to get final answer"], [1, "Correct integral but no final answer or equivalent"]], "top_criterion_original": "Correct integral and substitution to get final answer", "marker_note": "Many did not recognise that $\\ln\\left(e^a+e^{-a}\\right)-\\ln\\left(e^{-a}+e^a\\right)=0$."}');
$$\begin{aligned}p(x)&=\int-0.015e^{-0.01x}\,dx\\ &=-0.015\int e^{-0.01x}\,dx\\ &=\dfrac{-0.015}{-0.01}e^{-0.01x}+c\\ &=1.5e^{-0.01x}+c\end{aligned}$$
The price is $\$2.35$ per tube for $50$ tubes per week, so $p(50)=2.35$:
$$\begin{aligned}2.35&=1.5e^{-0.01(50)}+c\\ c&=2.35-0.91\\ &=1.44\end{aligned}$$
$$\therefore p(x)=1.5e^{-0.01x}+1.44$$','3: Correct integral + c; substitution of 50 and \$2.35 was correct; correct value of c',1364,NULL,'parse-v1',NULL,NULL,'[[3, "Correct answer"], [2, "Finds the correct integral with $+c$ and substitutes $x=50$ and $p=2.35$ correctly, or equivalent merit"], [1, "Finds the correct integral with $+c$, or equivalent merit"]]','{"criteria_original": [[3, "Correct integral + c; substitution of 50 and \\$2.35 was correct; correct value of c"]], "top_criterion_original": "Correct integral + c; substitution of 50 and \\$2.35 was correct; correct value of c", "marker_note": "A lot of students incorrectly divided $2.35$ by $\\dfrac{3}{2}$ or $1.5$ after substitution. Some incorrectly substituted the values for $x$ and $p(x)$."}');
$$\begin{aligned}A_1&=\int_0^{\frac{3}{2}}\left(6x^2-4x^3\right)dx\\ &=\Big[2x^3-x^4\Big]_0^{\frac{3}{2}}\\ &=\left[2\left(\dfrac{3}{2}\right)^3-\left(\dfrac{3}{2}\right)^4\right]-[0]\\ &=\dfrac{27}{4}-\dfrac{81}{16}\\ &=\dfrac{27}{16}\text{ u}^2\end{aligned}$$','2: Correct integral with boundaries; correct showing of all steps to get required answer',1364,NULL,'parse-v1',NULL,NULL,'[[2, "Correct answer"], [1, "Writes the correct integral with boundaries, or equivalent merit"]]','{"criteria_original": [[2, "Correct integral with boundaries; correct showing of all steps to get required answer"]], "top_criterion_original": "Correct integral with boundaries; correct showing of all steps to get required answer"}');
$$\begin{aligned}\int_{\frac{3}{2}}^{k}\left(6x^2-4x^3\right)dx&=-\dfrac{27}{16}\\ \Big[2x^3-x^4\Big]_{\frac{3}{2}}^{k}&=-\dfrac{27}{16}\\ \left[2k^3-k^4\right]-\left[2\left(\dfrac{3}{2}\right)^3-\left(\dfrac{3}{2}\right)^4\right]&=-\dfrac{27}{16}\\ 2k^3-k^4-\dfrac{27}{4}+\dfrac{81}{16}&=-\dfrac{27}{16}\\ 2k^3-k^4&=0\\ k^3(2-k)&=0\\ k&=0\text{ or } k=2\end{aligned}$$
Since $k>\dfrac{3}{2}$:
$$\therefore k=2$$
Alternatively, the signed areas above and below the axis add to $0$, so solve
$$\int_0^k\left(6x^2-4x^3\right)dx=0$$','3: Setting up correct integral equation with boundaries; substituting in boundaries and simplifying correctly to get two values for $k$; recognising/writing that $k>\dfrac{3}{2}$ and so only correct answer should be $k=2$',1364,NULL,'parse-v1',NULL,NULL,'[[3, "Correct answer"], [2, "Substitutes the boundaries and simplifies correctly to get two values for $k$, or equivalent merit"], [1, "Sets up the correct integral equation with boundaries, or equivalent merit"]]','{"criteria_original": [[3, "Setting up correct integral equation with boundaries; substituting in boundaries and simplifying correctly to get two values for $k$; recognising/writing that $k>\\dfrac{3}{2}$ and so only correct answer should be $k=2$"]], "top_criterion_original": "Setting up correct integral equation with boundaries; substituting in boundaries and simplifying correctly to get two values for $k$; recognising/writing that $k>\\dfrac{3}{2}$ and so only correct answer should be $k=2$", "marker_note": "Overall very poorly done. Many students did not recognise that the integral should equal a negative value as the area is below the $x$-axis. Many did take the absolute value of the integral, but then were not able to solve correctly to get the final answer."}');
$$\begin{aligned}\int\left(2x\ln x+x\right)dx&=x^2\ln x+C\\ \int 2x\ln x\,dx+\int x\,dx&=x^2\ln x+C\\ 2\int x\ln x\,dx+\dfrac{x^2}{2}&=x^2\ln x+C\\ 2\int x\ln x\,dx&=x^2\ln x-\dfrac{x^2}{2}+C\\ \int x\ln x\,dx&=\dfrac{1}{2}x^2\ln x-\dfrac{1}{4}x^2+C\end{aligned}$$','1: for setting up of line 3 or equivalent; 1: for correct final integral + c',1364,NULL,'parse-v1',NULL,NULL,'[[2, "Correct answer"], [1, "Sets up $\\displaystyle 2\\int x\\ln x\\,dx+\\dfrac{x^2}{2}=x^2\\ln x+C$, or equivalent merit"]]','{"criteria_original": [[1, "for setting up of line 3 or equivalent"], [1, "for correct final integral + c"]], "top_criterion_original": "for setting up of line 3 or equivalent"}');
