For $\dfrac{7}{\sin\theta}$:
$$\begin{aligned}u&=7 & v&=\sin\theta\\ u''&=0 & v''&=\cos\theta\end{aligned}$$
For $\dfrac{5}{\cos\theta}$:
$$\begin{aligned}u&=5 & v&=\cos\theta\\ u''&=0 & v''&=-\sin\theta\end{aligned}$$
$$\begin{aligned}\dfrac{dL}{d\theta}&=\dfrac{0-7\cos\theta}{\sin^2\theta}+\dfrac{0-5(-\sin\theta)}{\cos^2\theta}\\ &=\dfrac{-7\cos\theta}{\sin^2\theta}+\dfrac{5\sin\theta}{\cos^2\theta}\end{aligned}$$','2: Correctly differentiating both terms using the derivatives of the reciprocal trigonometric functions, or using quotient rule and writing the final answer in simplified form; 1: Correctly differentiating only one term while making a major error in the other',1364,NULL,'parse-v1',NULL,NULL,'[[2, "Correct answer"], [1, "Differentiates one term correctly while making a major error in the other, or equivalent merit"]]','{"criteria_original": [[2, "Correctly differentiating both terms using the derivatives of the reciprocal trigonometric functions, or using quotient rule and writing the final answer in simplified form"], [1, "Correctly differentiating only one term while making a major error in the other"]], "top_criterion_original": "Correctly differentiating both terms using the derivatives of the reciprocal trigonometric functions, or using quotient rule and writing the final answer in simplified form"}');
$$\begin{aligned}\dfrac{-7\cos\theta}{\sin^2\theta}+\dfrac{5\sin\theta}{\cos^2\theta}&=0\\ \dfrac{5\sin\theta}{\cos^2\theta}&=\dfrac{7\cos\theta}{\sin^2\theta}\\ 5\sin^3\theta&=7\cos^3\theta\\ \dfrac{\sin^3\theta}{\cos^3\theta}&=\dfrac{7}{5}\\ \tan^3\theta&=\dfrac{7}{5}\\ \tan\theta&=\sqrt[3]{\dfrac{7}{5}}\end{aligned}$$
$\tan\theta>0$, so $\theta$ is in the first or third quadrant:
$$\begin{aligned}\theta&=\tan^{-1}\left(\sqrt[3]{\dfrac{7}{5}}\right)\text{ or } \pi+\tan^{-1}\left(\sqrt[3]{\dfrac{7}{5}}\right)\\ &=0.841\text{ or } 3.983\ \text{(3 d.p.)}\end{aligned}$$','2: Correctly setting $\dfrac{dL}{d\theta}$ equal to zero and solving for $\theta$ using appropriate trigonometric identities or algebraic manipulation to find the numerical value(s) of $\theta$, rounded to three decimal places; 1: Correctly working out the equation $\tan^3\theta=\dfrac{7}{5}$ but making a minor error in solving for $\theta$, or making an error in setting up the equation but solving their incorrect equation correctly for $\theta$',1364,NULL,'parse-v1',NULL,NULL,'[[2, "Correct answer"], [1, "Obtains $\\tan^3\\theta=\\dfrac{7}{5}$ but makes a minor error in solving for $\\theta$, or makes an error in setting up the equation but solves their equation correctly, or equivalent merit"]]','{"criteria_original": [[2, "Correctly setting $\\dfrac{dL}{d\\theta}$ equal to zero and solving for $\\theta$ using appropriate trigonometric identities or algebraic manipulation to find the numerical value(s) of $\\theta$, rounded to three decimal places"], [1, "Correctly working out the equation $\\tan^3\\theta=\\dfrac{7}{5}$ but making a minor error in solving for $\\theta$, or making an error in setting up the equation but solving their incorrect equation correctly for $\\theta$"]], "top_criterion_original": "Correctly setting $\\dfrac{dL}{d\\theta}$ equal to zero and solving for $\\theta$ using appropriate trigonometric identities or algebraic manipulation to find the numerical value(s) of $\\theta$, rounded to three decimal places"}');
$$\begin{aligned}(PQ)^2&=(QR)^2+(PR)^2\\ (5-x)^2&=x^2+h^2\\ h^2&=(5-x)^2-x^2\\ &=25-10x+x^2-x^2\\ &=25-10x\\ h&=\sqrt{25-10x}\end{aligned}$$
Area of triangle $PQR$, with base $x$ and height $h$:
$$\begin{aligned}A&=\dfrac{1}{2}\times x\times\sqrt{25-10x}\\ &=\dfrac{1}{2}x\sqrt{25-10x}\end{aligned}$$','2: Correctly determining the length of PR (height h) in terms of x by applying the Pythagoras theorem, and showing the steps required for full simplification of this expression; correctly substituting the base and height into the formula for the area of a triangle and simplifying to obtain the required expression in the form given in the question; 1: Correctly identifying and setting up the relationship for the height PR but not showing the steps required for the full simplification, or making a minor error in determining the height but substituting their expression into the area formula',1364,NULL,'parse-v1',NULL,NULL,'[[2, "Correct answer"], [1, "Sets up the relationship for the height $PR$ but does not show the steps for full simplification, or makes a minor error in the height but substitutes their expression into the area formula, or equivalent merit"]]','{"criteria_original": [[2, "Correctly determining the length of PR (height h) in terms of x by applying the Pythagoras theorem, and showing the steps required for full simplification of this expression; correctly substituting the base and height into the formula for the area of a triangle and simplifying to obtain the required expression in the form given in the question"], [1, "Correctly identifying and setting up the relationship for the height PR but not showing the steps required for the full simplification, or making a minor error in determining the height but substituting their expression into the area formula"]], "top_criterion_original": "Correctly determining the length of PR (height h) in terms of x by applying the Pythagoras theorem, and showing the steps required for full simplification of this expression; correctly substituting the base and height into the formula for the area of a triangle and simplifying to obtain the required expression in the form given in the question"}');
$$\begin{aligned}u&=\dfrac{1}{2}x & v&=(25-10x)^{\frac{1}{2}}\\ u''&=\dfrac{1}{2} & v''&=\dfrac{1}{2}(25-10x)^{-\frac{1}{2}}\times(-10)\end{aligned}$$
($v''$ by the chain rule.)
$$\begin{aligned}\dfrac{dA}{dx}&=\dfrac{1}{2}\sqrt{25-10x}+\dfrac{1}{2}x\times\dfrac{1}{2}(25-10x)^{-\frac{1}{2}}(-10)\\ &=\dfrac{1}{2}\sqrt{25-10x}-\dfrac{5}{2}x(25-10x)^{-\frac{1}{2}}\\ &=\dfrac{\sqrt{25-10x}}{2}-\dfrac{5x}{2\sqrt{25-10x}}\\ &=\dfrac{25-10x-5x}{2\sqrt{25-10x}}\\ &=\dfrac{25-15x}{2\sqrt{25-10x}}\end{aligned}$$
Stationary points occur where $\dfrac{dA}{dx}=0$, with $0<x<2.5$:
$$\begin{aligned}25-15x&=0\\ x&=\dfrac{25}{15}=\dfrac{5}{3}\end{aligned}$$
\extable{Ts1315_1}

$\therefore$ $x=\dfrac{5}{3}$ is a maximum turning point and gives the maximum area.

Substituting $x=\dfrac{5}{3}$ into $A=\dfrac{1}{2}x\sqrt{25-10x}$:
$$\begin{aligned}A&=\dfrac{1}{2}\times\dfrac{5}{3}\times\sqrt{25-10\times\dfrac{5}{3}}\\ &=\dfrac{5}{6}\sqrt{\dfrac{75-50}{3}}\\ &=\dfrac{5}{6}\sqrt{\dfrac{25}{3}}\\ &=\dfrac{25}{6\sqrt{3}}\text{ u}^2\end{aligned}$$','4: Differentiating the area expression correctly with respect to x; setting the derivative equal to zero and accurately solving for the value of x corresponding to the stationary point; justifying that this value of x gives a maximum, using either the first derivative test or the second derivative test appropriately; substituting this value of x into the original area expression to obtain the correct maximum possible area; 3: A complete and mostly correct solution but missing one of the required steps, such as not fully justifying the maximum (e.g. not giving values for the first derivative test and only giving signs), making a minor error in differentiation, making an error in solving for x, or making an error in substitution into the area expression; 2: a mostly correct solution where only two of the above-mentioned steps for a fully correct solution have been completed correctly; 1: a partially correct solution where only one of the above-mentioned steps for a fully correct solution has been completed correctly',1364,NULL,'parse-v1',NULL,NULL,'[[4, "Correct answer"], [3, "A complete and mostly correct solution missing one required step (such as not fully justifying the maximum, e.g. signs but no values in the first derivative test), or with a minor error in differentiation, in solving for $x$ or in substitution, or equivalent merit"], [2, "Completes two of the required steps (differentiates, solves $\\dfrac{dA}{dx}=0$, justifies the maximum, finds the area) correctly, or equivalent merit"], [1, "Completes one of the required steps correctly, or equivalent merit"]]','{"criteria_original": [[4, "Differentiating the area expression correctly with respect to x; setting the derivative equal to zero and accurately solving for the value of x corresponding to the stationary point; justifying that this value of x gives a maximum, using either the first derivative test or the second derivative test appropriately; substituting this value of x into the original area expression to obtain the correct maximum possible area"], [3, "A complete and mostly correct solution but missing one of the required steps, such as not fully justifying the maximum (e.g. not giving values for the first derivative test and only giving signs), making a minor error in differentiation, making an error in solving for x, or making an error in substitution into the area expression"], [2, "a mostly correct solution where only two of the above-mentioned steps for a fully correct solution have been completed correctly"], [1, "a partially correct solution where only one of the above-mentioned steps for a fully correct solution has been completed correctly"]], "top_criterion_original": "Differentiating the area expression correctly with respect to x; setting the derivative equal to zero and accurately solving for the value of x corresponding to the stationary point; justifying that this value of x gives a maximum, using either the first derivative test or the second derivative test appropriately; substituting this value of x into the original area expression to obtain the correct maximum possible area", "source_original": "\\dfrac{dA}{dx}=\\dfrac{1}{2}\\sqrt{25-10x}+\\dfrac{1}{2}x\\times\\dfrac{1}{2}(25-10x)^{1-\\frac{1}{2}}(-10)", "corrected": "Corrected from source: the chain-rule factor is $(25-10x)^{-\\frac{1}{2}}$ (source printed the exponent as $1-\\frac{1}{2}$)."}');
