$$\begin{aligned}\ln\left(\dfrac{a}{3}\right)&=\ln\left(\dfrac{a+2}{a-2}\right)\\ \dfrac{a}{3}&=\dfrac{a+2}{a-2}\\ a^2-2a&=3a+6\\ (a-6)(a+1)&=0\\ a&=6\text{ or } a=-1\end{aligned}$$
$\ln a$ needs $a>0$ and $\ln(a-2)$ needs $a>2$, so $a=-1$ is rejected.
$$\therefore a=6$$','3: Correct single answer with correct working; 2: Two answers correctly found or only one arithmetic mistake leading to two answers and removing one; 1: Arithmetic mistake leads to one answer so no final removal',1366,NULL,'parse-v1',NULL,NULL,'[[3, "Correct answer"], [2, "Finds both answers correctly, or makes only one arithmetic mistake leading to two answers and rejects one, or equivalent merit"], [1, "Makes an arithmetic mistake leading to one answer, so no final rejection, or equivalent merit"]]','{"criteria_original": [[3, "Correct single answer with correct working"], [2, "Two answers correctly found or only one arithmetic mistake leading to two answers and removing one"], [1, "Arithmetic mistake leads to one answer so no final removal"]], "top_criterion_original": "Correct single answer with correct working", "source_original": "$\\therefore a$ can only be 6 because $a\\ge 0$", "corrected": "Corrected from source: $a=-1$ is rejected because the logarithms need $a>2$ (source gave the reason as $a\\ge 0$)."}');
$$\begin{aligned}f\big(g(x)\big)&=\dfrac{1}{\left(\sqrt{x+2}\right)^2+2}-3\\ &=\dfrac{1}{x+4}-3\end{aligned}$$
The square root needs $x+2\ge 0$, so the natural domain is
$$x\ge -2$$
As $x\to\infty$, $y\to -3$ (horizontal asymptote $y=-3$).

