$$\begin{aligned}P(x)&=\dfrac{3}{4}x^2(2-x)\\ P''(x)&=3x-\dfrac{9}{4}x^2\end{aligned}$$
Stationary points occur where $P''(x)=0$:
$$\begin{aligned}3x\left(1-\dfrac{3}{4}x\right)&=0\\ x&=0\text{ or } x=\dfrac{4}{3}\end{aligned}$$
Testing the nature with the second derivative:
$$\begin{aligned}P''''(x)&=3-\dfrac{9}{2}x\\ P''''\left(\dfrac{4}{3}\right)&=-3<0\end{aligned}$$
So $x=\dfrac{4}{3}$ is a maximum, and the mode is $x=\dfrac{4}{3}$.','2: Showing $P''''(x)=3-\dfrac{9}{2}x$ and solving for $-3$ or showing that the Nature of the points around the value determined a maximum point to give the mode was also acceptable; 1: Showing that $P''(x)=0$ and stating that $x=0$ or $\dfrac{4}{3}$',1366,NULL,'parse-v1',NULL,NULL,'[[2, "Correct answer"], [1, "Shows $P''(x)=0$ and states $x=0$ or $x=\\dfrac{4}{3}$, or equivalent merit"]]','{"criteria_original": [[2, "Showing $P''''(x)=3-\\dfrac{9}{2}x$ and solving for $-3$ or showing that the Nature of the points around the value determined a maximum point to give the mode was also acceptable"], [1, "Showing that $P''(x)=0$ and stating that $x=0$ or $\\dfrac{4}{3}$"]], "top_criterion_original": "Showing $P''''(x)=3-\\dfrac{9}{2}x$ and solving for $-3$ or showing that the Nature of the points around the value determined a maximum point to give the mode was also acceptable", "marker_note": "Showing the nature of the stationary point with the first derivative test (a maximum gives the mode) was also accepted."}');
