$$\begin{aligned}\int\left(2x\ln x+x\right)dx&=x^2\ln x\\ 2\int x\ln x\,dx+\int x\,dx&=x^2\ln x\\ 2\int x\ln x\,dx+\dfrac{x^2}{2}&=x^2\ln x\\ \int x\ln x\,dx&=\dfrac{x^2\ln x}{2}-\dfrac{x^2}{4}+C\end{aligned}$$','2: Letting the entire function equal $x^2\ln(x)$ and solving to for the RHS. Adding the C; 1: Taking the 2 common and showing that the $x^2$ will be halved.',1366,NULL,'parse-v1',NULL,NULL,'[[2, "Correct answer"], [1, "Takes the $2$ out as a common factor and shows that the $x^2$ term is halved, or equivalent merit"]]','{"criteria_original": [[2, "Letting the entire function equal $x^2\\ln(x)$ and solving to for the RHS. Adding the C"], [1, "Taking the 2 common and showing that the $x^2$ will be halved."]], "top_criterion_original": "Letting the entire function equal $x^2\\ln(x)$ and solving to for the RHS. Adding the C"}');
$$\begin{aligned}\tan 30^\circ&=\dfrac{OT}{18}\\ OT&=18\tan 30^\circ\\ &=\dfrac{18}{\sqrt{3}}\end{aligned}$$
$O$ is due west of $A$ (bearing $270^\circ$) and $B$ is on a bearing of $210^\circ$ from $A$, so $\angle OAB=60^\circ$.

Applying the cosine rule in triangle $OAB$:
$$\begin{aligned}OB&=\sqrt{18^2+12^2-2(18)(12)\cos 60^\circ}\\ &=\sqrt{252}\end{aligned}$$
In right-angled triangle $OBT$:
$$\begin{aligned}\tan\theta&=\dfrac{OT}{OB}\\ \theta&=\tan^{-1}\left(\dfrac{\frac{18}{\sqrt{3}}}{\sqrt{252}}\right)\\ &=33^\circ\ (\text{nearest degree})\end{aligned}$$','3: Calculating $\theta=63$ degrees; 2: Calculating OB correctly (leave in exact form); 1: One mark for calculating OT correctly.',1366,NULL,'parse-v1',NULL,NULL,'[[3, "Correct answer"], [2, "Calculates $OB$ correctly (exact form), or equivalent merit"], [1, "Calculates $OT$ correctly, or equivalent merit"]]','{"criteria_original": [[3, "Calculating $\\theta=63$ degrees"], [2, "Calculating OB correctly (leave in exact form)"], [1, "One mark for calculating OT correctly."]], "top_criterion_original": "Calculating $\\theta=63$ degrees", "source_original": "$$=\\tan^{-1}\\left(\\dfrac{\\frac{18}{\\sqrt{3}}}{\\sqrt{252}}\\right)$$ $$=63^\\circ\\ (\\text{nearest degree})$$; criterion: Calculating $\\theta=63$ degrees", "corrected": "Corrected from source: $\\tan\\theta=\\dfrac{\\frac{18}{\\sqrt{3}}}{\\sqrt{252}}\\approx 0.655$, so $\\theta=33^\\circ$ (source printed $63^\\circ$, in the working and the criterion)."}');
