$$\begin{aligned}d&=\int_1^2\left(3t^2-15t+18\right)dt+\left|\int_2^3\left(3t^2-15t+18\right)dt\right|\\ &=\dfrac{5}{2}+\left|-\dfrac{1}{2}\right|\\ &=3\text{ m}\end{aligned}$$','3: Provides correct answers; 2: Attempted to find two definite integrals using the correct bounds, but made minor arithmetic errors; 1: Recognises the change of direction at $t=2$ by either drawing a graph of $v(t)$ or equivalent merit, or, if a student did not identify the positive and negative areas under the velocity function, they integrated from $t=1$ to $t=3$, performed correct calculation subsequently.',1366,NULL,'parse-v1',NULL,NULL,'[[3, "Correct answer"], [2, "Finds two definite integrals using the correct bounds, but makes minor arithmetic errors, or equivalent merit"], [1, "Recognises the change of direction at $t=2$ (e.g. by drawing a graph of $v(t)$), or integrates from $t=1$ to $t=3$ without splitting and calculates correctly, or equivalent merit"]]','{"criteria_original": [[3, "Provides correct answers"], [2, "Attempted to find two definite integrals using the correct bounds, but made minor arithmetic errors"], [1, "Recognises the change of direction at $t=2$ by either drawing a graph of $v(t)$ or equivalent merit, or, if a student did not identify the positive and negative areas under the velocity function, they integrated from $t=1$ to $t=3$, performed correct calculation subsequently."]], "top_criterion_original": "Provides correct answers"}');
$$\begin{aligned}z_1&=\dfrac{62-72}{8}=-1.25\\ z_2&=\dfrac{92-72}{8}=2.5\end{aligned}$$
From the table and symmetry, $P(z<-1.25)=1-P(z<1.25)=0.1057$.
$$\begin{aligned}P(62<X<92)&=P(-1.25<z<2.5)\\ &=0.9938-0.1057\\ &=0.8881\end{aligned}$$','2: Provides correct answer; 1: Correctly finds the two corresponding z-scores',1366,NULL,'parse-v1',NULL,NULL,'[[2, "Correct answer"], [1, "Correctly finds the two corresponding $z$-scores, or equivalent merit"]]','{"criteria_original": [[2, "Provides correct answer"], [1, "Correctly finds the two corresponding z-scores"]], "top_criterion_original": "Provides correct answer", "source_original": "$$P(62<X<92)=P(-1.25<X<92)$$", "corrected": "Corrected from source: the probability in $z$ is $P(-1.25<z<2.5)$ (source wrote $P(-1.25<X<92)$)."}');
$$\begin{aligned}P(X<62\mid X<92)&=\dfrac{P(X<62\cap X<92)}{P(X<92)}\\ &=\dfrac{P(z<-1.25\cap z<2.5)}{P(z<2.5)}\\ &=\dfrac{0.1057}{0.9938}\\ &=0.10635\ldots\\ &=0.1064\ (4\text{ d.p.})\end{aligned}$$','2: Provides correct answer; 1: Provides correct conditional probability formulae in relation to the question',1366,NULL,'parse-v1',NULL,NULL,'[[2, "Correct answer"], [1, "Writes the correct conditional probability formula for the question, or equivalent merit"]]','{"criteria_original": [[2, "Provides correct answer"], [1, "Provides correct conditional probability formulae in relation to the question"]], "top_criterion_original": "Provides correct answer"}');
$$y''=2(x+2)$$
At $A(-3,1)$:
$$y''(-3)=-2$$
The normal is perpendicular to the tangent:
$$m_{\text{normal}}=\dfrac{1}{2}$$
Using the point–gradient form:
$$\begin{aligned}y-1&=\dfrac{1}{2}(x+3)\\ y&=\dfrac{1}{2}x+\dfrac{5}{2}\end{aligned}$$','2: Provides correct linear equation of the normal; 1: Provides correct gradient of the normal',1366,NULL,'parse-v1',NULL,NULL,'[[2, "Correct answer"], [1, "Finds the correct gradient of the normal, or equivalent merit"]]','{"criteria_original": [[2, "Provides correct linear equation of the normal"], [1, "Provides correct gradient of the normal"]], "top_criterion_original": "Provides correct linear equation of the normal", "source_original": "$$\\therefore y''(2)=-2$$", "corrected": "Corrected from source: the gradient at $A$ is $y''(-3)=-2$ (source printed $y''(2)=-2$)."}');
$$\begin{aligned}\dfrac{1}{2}x+\dfrac{5}{2}&=(x+2)^2\\ \dfrac{x}{2}+\dfrac{5}{2}&=x^2+4x+4\\ 2x^2+7x+3&=0\\ (2x+1)(x+3)&=0\\ x&=-\dfrac{1}{2}\text{ or } x=-3\end{aligned}$$
$x=-3$ is the point $A$, so at $B$, $x=-\dfrac{1}{2}$:
$$y\left(-\dfrac{1}{2}\right)=2\dfrac{1}{4}$$
$$\therefore B\left(-\dfrac{1}{2},2\dfrac{1}{4}\right)$$','2: Finds the second point of intersection (both x- and y- value) using simultaneous equation; 1: Uses simultaneous equations to solve for the x-coordinates of the points of intersection, but made minor errors',1366,NULL,'parse-v1',NULL,NULL,'[[2, "Correct answer"], [1, "Uses simultaneous equations to solve for the $x$-coordinates of the points of intersection, but makes minor errors, or equivalent merit"]]','{"criteria_original": [[2, "Finds the second point of intersection (both x- and y- value) using simultaneous equation"], [1, "Uses simultaneous equations to solve for the x-coordinates of the points of intersection, but made minor errors"]], "top_criterion_original": "Finds the second point of intersection (both x- and y- value) using simultaneous equation", "source_original": "$$\\therefore B\\left(-3,2\\dfrac{1}{4}\\right)$$", "corrected": "Corrected from source: $B\\left(-\\dfrac{1}{2},2\\dfrac{1}{4}\\right)$ (source printed $B\\left(-3,2\\dfrac{1}{4}\\right)$ on the last line)."}');

The gradient of the tangent at $B$:
$$\begin{aligned}y''&=2(x+2)\\ y''\left(-\dfrac{1}{2}\right)&=3\end{aligned}$$
The tangent at $B$ makes an angle $\alpha$ with the $x$-axis:
$$\begin{aligned}\tan\alpha&=3\\ \alpha&=\tan^{-1}(3)\\ &=71.565\ldots^\circ\end{aligned}$$
Line $AB$ (the normal, gradient $\dfrac{1}{2}$) makes an angle $\beta$ with the $x$-axis:
$$\begin{aligned}\tan\beta&=\dfrac{1}{2}\\ \beta&=\tan^{-1}\left(\dfrac{1}{2}\right)\\ &=26.565\ldots^\circ\end{aligned}$$
By the exterior angle of a triangle:
$$\begin{aligned}\theta&=\alpha-\beta\\ &=71.565\ldots^\circ-26.565\ldots^\circ\\ &=45^\circ\end{aligned}$$','2: Provides the correct answer; 1: Find the gradient of tangent B and converts it to angle of inclination',1366,NULL,'parse-v1',NULL,NULL,'[[2, "Correct answer"], [1, "Finds the gradient of the tangent at $B$ and converts it to an angle of inclination, or equivalent merit"]]','{"criteria_original": [[2, "Provides the correct answer"], [1, "Find the gradient of tangent B and converts it to angle of inclination"]], "top_criterion_original": "Provides the correct answer", "source_original": "$$y''\\left(\\dfrac{1}{2}\\right)=3\\ (\\text{gradient of the tangent at B})$$", "corrected": "Corrected from source: the gradient at $B$ is $y''\\left(-\\dfrac{1}{2}\\right)=3$ (source printed $y''\\left(\\dfrac{1}{2}\\right)=3$)."}');
$$f(x)=x^3-4x^2-11x+30$$
Stationary points occur where $f''(x)=0$:
$$\begin{aligned}f''(x)&=3x^2-8x-11\\ &=(3x-11)(x+1)\end{aligned}$$
$$\therefore x=\dfrac{11}{3}\text{ or } x=-1$$
$$\begin{aligned}f\left(\dfrac{11}{3}\right)&=-\dfrac{400}{27}\\ f(-1)&=36\end{aligned}$$
$\therefore$ the turning points are $(-1,36)$ and $\left(\dfrac{11}{3},-\dfrac{400}{27}\right)$.','3: Provides the correct y-coordinates of the stationary points; 2: Finds the correct $x$-values of the derivatives; 1: Differentiates f(x) correctly',1366,NULL,'parse-v1',NULL,NULL,'[[3, "Correct answer"], [2, "Finds the correct $x$-values where the derivative is zero, or equivalent merit"], [1, "Differentiates $f(x)$ correctly, or equivalent merit"]]','{"criteria_original": [[3, "Provides the correct y-coordinates of the stationary points"], [2, "Finds the correct $x$-values of the derivatives"], [1, "Differentiates f(x) correctly"]], "top_criterion_original": "Provides the correct y-coordinates of the stationary points"}');
