Differentiating (chain rule):
$$w''(t)=-\dfrac{11\pi}{60}\sin\left(\dfrac{\pi}{6}t\right)$$
The level rises faster than $0.55$ m/h when $w''(t)>0.55$. Solving $w''(t)=0.55$:
$$\begin{aligned}-\dfrac{11\pi}{60}\sin\left(\dfrac{\pi}{6}t\right)&=0.55\\ \sin\left(\dfrac{\pi}{6}t\right)&=-\dfrac{3}{\pi}\end{aligned}$$
Sine is negative in the third and fourth quadrants, with $0\le\dfrac{\pi}{6}t\le 2\pi$:
$$\begin{aligned}\dfrac{\pi}{6}t&=\pi+\sin^{-1}\left(\dfrac{3}{\pi}\right)\text{ or } 2\pi-\sin^{-1}\left(\dfrac{3}{\pi}\right)\\ t&=8.424418\ldots\text{ or } 9.5755811\ldots\end{aligned}$$
$w''(t)>0.55$ between these times:
$$\begin{aligned}\text{Time}&=9.5755811\ldots-8.424418\ldots\\ &=1.151162\ldots\text{ hours}\\ &\approx 1\text{ hour } 9\text{ minutes}\end{aligned}$$','3: Provide the correct solution; 2: Correctly solve $\dfrac{11\pi}{60}\sin\left(\dfrac{\pi}{6}t\right)=0.55$; 1: Correctly find the derivative of $w(t)$',1366,NULL,'parse-v1',NULL,NULL,'[[3, "Correct answer"], [2, "Correctly solves $-\\dfrac{11\\pi}{60}\\sin\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{6}t\\right)=0.55$, or equivalent merit"], [1, "Correctly finds the derivative of $w(t)$, or equivalent merit"]]','{"criteria_original": [[3, "Provide the correct solution"], [2, "Correctly solve $\\dfrac{11\\pi}{60}\\sin\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{6}t\\right)=0.55$"], [1, "Correctly find the derivative of $w(t)$"]], "top_criterion_original": "Provide the correct solution", "source_original": "$$w(t)=1.1\\cos\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{6}t\\right)+7.5$$ $$w''(t)=\\dfrac{11\\pi}{60}\\sin\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{6}t\\right)$$; criterion: Correctly solve $\\dfrac{11\\pi}{60}\\sin\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{6}t\\right)=0.55$", "corrected": "Corrected from source: $w(t)=1.1\\cos\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{6}t\\right)+7.4$ and $w''(t)=-\\dfrac{11\\pi}{60}\\sin\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{6}t\\right)$ (source printed $+7.5$ and dropped the minus sign, in the working and the criterion)."}');
$$\begin{aligned}u&=(t-1)^2 & v&=e^{-t}\\ u''&=2(t-1) & v''&=-e^{-t}\end{aligned}$$
$$\begin{aligned}f''(t)&=2e^{-t}(t-1)-e^{-t}(t-1)^2\\ &=e^{-t}(t-1)(3-t)\end{aligned}$$
Stationary points occur where $f''(t)=0$; since $e^{-t}>0$:
$$t=1\text{ or } t=3$$
$$\begin{aligned}f(1)&=0\\ f(3)&=4e^{-3}\end{aligned}$$
As $t\to\infty$, $f(t)\to 0$ (the exponential dominates), so the $t$-axis is an asymptote.

