$$\begin{aligned}y^2&=(2m-y)(2n-y)\\ y^2&=4mn-2my-2ny+y^2\\ 2my+2ny&=4mn\\ y&=\dfrac{2mn}{m+n}\\ \dfrac{1}{y}&=\dfrac{m+n}{2mn}\end{aligned}$$
Checking $T_2-T_1$:
$$\begin{aligned}T_2-T_1&=\dfrac{1}{y}-\dfrac{1}{m}\\ &=\dfrac{m+n}{2mn}-\dfrac{1}{m}\cdot\dfrac{2n}{2n}\\ &=\dfrac{m+n-2n}{2mn}\\ &=\dfrac{m-n}{2mn}\end{aligned}$$
Checking $T_3-T_2$:
$$\begin{aligned}T_3-T_2&=\dfrac{1}{n}-\dfrac{1}{y}\\ &=\dfrac{1}{n}\cdot\dfrac{2m}{2m}-\dfrac{m+n}{2mn}\\ &=\dfrac{2m-m-n}{2mn}\\ &=\dfrac{m-n}{2mn}\\ &=T_2-T_1\end{aligned}$$
As $T_3-T_2=T_2-T_1$, the three terms form an arithmetic sequence.','1: expansion of $y^2$ equation, or equivalent merit; 2: the values of $T_3-T_2$ and $T_2-T_1$ in terms of $y$, $m$ and $n$, or equivalent merit; 3: correct solution. Note that students who automatically assumed $T_3-T_2=T_2-T_1$ can only receive 2 out of 3 marks maximum, as they assumed the terms were equal prior to showing the terms form an arithmetic sequence',1367,NULL,'parse-v1',NULL,NULL,'[[3, "Correct answer"], [2, "Finds $T_3-T_2$ and $T_2-T_1$ in terms of $y$, $m$ and $n$, or equivalent merit"], [1, "Expands the $y^2$ equation, or equivalent merit"]]','{"marker_note": "Note that students who automatically assumed $T_3-T_2=T_2-T_1$ can only receive 2 out of 3 marks maximum, as they assumed the terms were equal prior to showing the terms form an arithmetic sequence", "criteria_original": [[1, "expansion of $y^2$ equation, or equivalent merit"], [2, "the values of $T_3-T_2$ and $T_2-T_1$ in terms of $y$, $m$ and $n$, or equivalent merit"], [3, "correct solution."]], "top_criterion_original": "correct solution.", "source_original": "&=\\dfrac{m+n-2n}{2n}\\\\&=\\dfrac{m-n}{2n}", "corrected": "Corrected from source: the denominator of $T_2-T_1$ is $2mn$ (source printed $\\dfrac{m+n-2n}{2n}=\\dfrac{m-n}{2n}$)."}');
$$\begin{aligned}\cos 60^\circ&=\dfrac{AO}{3\sqrt{3}}\\ AO&=\dfrac{3\sqrt{3}}{2}\end{aligned}$$
$A$ is due west of $O$ (bearing $270^\circ$) and $B$ is on a bearing of $240^\circ$ from $O$:
$$\angle AOB=270^\circ-240^\circ=30^\circ$$
Applying the cosine rule in triangle $AOB$:
$$\begin{aligned}10^2&=\left(\dfrac{3\sqrt{3}}{2}\right)^2+(OB)^2-2\left(\dfrac{3\sqrt{3}}{2}\right)(OB)\cos 30^\circ\\ 100&=\dfrac{27}{4}+(OB)^2-3\sqrt{3}\times\dfrac{\sqrt{3}}{2}(OB)\\ (OB)^2-\dfrac{9}{2}(OB)-\dfrac{373}{4}&=0\\ 4(OB)^2-18(OB)-373&=0\end{aligned}$$
Using the quadratic formula:
$$\begin{aligned}OB&=\dfrac{18\pm\sqrt{18^2-4\times 4\times(-373)}}{2\times 4}\\ &=\dfrac{18\pm\sqrt{6292}}{8}\\ &=\dfrac{18\pm 22\sqrt{13}}{8}\\ &=\dfrac{9\pm 11\sqrt{13}}{4}\end{aligned}$$
Since $OB>0$:
$$OB=\dfrac{9+11\sqrt{13}}{4}\text{ metres}$$','1: the correct value of $AO$; 2: applying cosine rule to $\triangle OAB$, or equivalent merit; 3: forming a quadratic equation in terms of $OB$; 4: correct answer',1367,NULL,'parse-v1',NULL,NULL,'[[4, "Correct answer"], [3, "Forms a quadratic equation in $OB$, or equivalent merit"], [2, "Applies the cosine rule to $\\triangle OAB$, or equivalent merit"], [1, "Finds the correct value of $AO$, or equivalent merit"]]','{"criteria_original": [[1, "the correct value of $AO$"], [2, "applying cosine rule to $\\triangle OAB$, or equivalent merit"], [3, "forming a quadratic equation in terms of $OB$"], [4, "correct answer"]], "top_criterion_original": "correct answer"}');
$$\begin{aligned}\sin 60^\circ&=\dfrac{OT}{3\sqrt{3}}\\ OT&=\dfrac{9}{2}\end{aligned}$$
Applying Pythagoras'' theorem in right-angled triangle $BOT$:
$$\begin{aligned}\ell^2&=OB^2+OT^2\\ &=\left(\dfrac{9+11\sqrt{13}}{4}\right)^2+\left(\dfrac{9}{2}\right)^2\\ \ell&=\sqrt{\left(\dfrac{9+11\sqrt{13}}{4}\right)^2+\left(\dfrac{9}{2}\right)^2}\quad(\ell>0)\\ &=12.97\ldots\\ &\approx 13\text{ m}\end{aligned}$$','1: the correct value of $OT$; 2: correct solution',1367,NULL,'parse-v1',NULL,NULL,'[[2, "Correct answer"], [1, "Finds the correct value of $OT$, or equivalent merit"]]','{"criteria_original": [[1, "the correct value of $OT$"], [2, "correct solution"]], "top_criterion_original": "correct solution"}');
$$\begin{aligned}y&=\ln(kx)\\ \dfrac{dy}{dx}&=\dfrac{k}{kx}=\dfrac{1}{x}\\ m&=\dfrac{1}{x}\end{aligned}$$
At the point of contact the line and the curve meet:
$$mx=\ln(kx)$$
Substituting $m=\dfrac{1}{x}$:
$$\begin{aligned}\ln(kx)&=\dfrac{1}{x}\times x\\ \ln(kx)&=1\\ kx&=e\\ x&=\dfrac{e}{k}\end{aligned}$$
$$\therefore m=\dfrac{1}{\frac{e}{k}}=\dfrac{k}{e}$$','1: finding $m$ in terms of $x$ or equivalent merit; 2: finding $x$ in terms of $k$ or equivalent merit; 3: correct answer',1367,NULL,'parse-v1',NULL,NULL,'[[3, "Correct answer"], [2, "Finds $x$ in terms of $k$, or equivalent merit"], [1, "Finds $m$ in terms of $x$, or equivalent merit"]]','{"criteria_original": [[1, "finding $m$ in terms of $x$ or equivalent merit"], [2, "finding $x$ in terms of $k$ or equivalent merit"], [3, "correct answer"]], "top_criterion_original": "correct answer"}');
