Or, by hand:
$$\begin{aligned}E(X)=\mu&=\sum\left[x\times p(x)\right]\\&=1\times0.1+2\times0.2+3\times0.3+4\times0.4\\&=3\end{aligned}$$
$$\begin{aligned}\sigma^2=\text{Var}(X)&=\sum\left[x^2p(x)\right]-\mu^2\\&=\left[1^2\times0.1+2^2\times0.2+3^2\times0.3+4^2\times0.4\right]-3^2\\&=10-9\\\sigma^2&=1\\\therefore\sigma&=\sqrt{1}=1\end{aligned}$$
Or, using $\text{Var}(X)=\sum(x-\mu)^2p(x)$:
$$\begin{aligned}\sigma^2&=(1-3)^2\times0.1+(2-3)^2\times0.2+(3-3)^2\times0.3+(4-3)^2\times0.4\\\sigma^2&=1\\\therefore\sigma&=\sqrt{1}=1\end{aligned}$$','1: correct answer from calculator; OR $\frac{1}{2}$ for expected value, $\frac{1}{2}$ for variance, $\frac{1}{2}$ for standard deviation (as marked on the guidelines).',1371,NULL,'parse-v1',NULL,NULL,'[[1, "Correct answer"]]','{"criteria_original": [[1, "correct answer from calculator; OR $\\dfrac{1}{2}$ for expected value, $\\dfrac{1}{2}$ for variance, $\\dfrac{1}{2}$ for standard deviation (as marked on the guidelines)."]], "top_criterion_original": "correct answer from calculator; OR $\\dfrac{1}{2}$ for expected value, $\\dfrac{1}{2}$ for variance, $\\dfrac{1}{2}$ for standard deviation (as marked on the guidelines).", "marker_note": "Full mark for the correct answer from the calculator; otherwise $\\dfrac{1}{2}$ for the expected value, $\\dfrac{1}{2}$ for the variance and $\\dfrac{1}{2}$ for the standard deviation (as marked on the guidelines)."}');
$$\begin{aligned}T_n&=-11+7(n-1)\\&=-11+7n-7\\&=7n-18\end{aligned}$$
For the terms under 100:
$$\begin{aligned}7n-18&<100\\7n&<118\\n&<16.857\ldots\end{aligned}$$
The largest whole number is $n=16$:
$$\begin{aligned}T_{16}&=7(16)-18\\&=94\end{aligned}$$
$\therefore$ the last term under 100 is the 16th term, $94$.','1: correct $n$ value; 1: correct 16th term. $1\frac{1}{2}$ marks if students stated the 16th term is the last term under 100 with no mention of 94. Marker: show all working and answer the question completely.',1371,NULL,'parse-v1',NULL,NULL,'[[2, "Correct answer"], [1, "Finds the correct value of $n$, or equivalent merit"]]','{"criteria_original": [[1, "correct $n$ value"], [1, "correct 16th term. $1\\dfrac{1}{2}$ marks if students stated the 16th term is the last term under 100 with no mention of 94. Marker: show all working and answer the question completely."]], "top_criterion_original": "correct $n$ value", "marker_note": "$1\\dfrac{1}{2}$ marks if students stated the 16th term is the last term under 100 with no mention of 94. Show all working and answer the question completely."}');
$$\begin{aligned}&\dfrac{d}{dx}\log_e\left(\dfrac{x+1}{x-2}\right)\\&=\dfrac{d}{dx}\left[\log_e(x+1)-\log_e(x-2)\right]\\&=\dfrac{1}{x+1}-\dfrac{1}{x-2}\end{aligned}$$
Method 2, using $\dfrac{d}{dx}\ln f(x)=\dfrac{f''(x)}{f(x)}$ and the quotient rule, $\dfrac{d}{dx}\left(\dfrac{u}{v}\right)=\dfrac{u''v-uv''}{v^2}$, with
$$\begin{aligned}u&=x+1 & v&=x-2\\u''&=1 & v''&=1\end{aligned}$$
$$\begin{aligned}&\dfrac{d}{dx}\log_e\left(\dfrac{x+1}{x-2}\right)\\&=\dfrac{1}{\frac{x+1}{x-2}}\times\dfrac{(x-2)\times1-(x+1)\times1}{(x-2)^2}\\&=\dfrac{x-2}{x+1}\times\dfrac{x-2-x-1}{(x-2)^2}\\&=\dfrac{-3}{(x+1)(x-2)}\end{aligned}$$','Method 1: 1: correct use of log law; 1: correct differentiation of both. Method 2: 1: $\dfrac{1}{\frac{x+1}{x-2}}$; 1: correct quotient rule $\dfrac{-3}{(x-2)^2}$. Marker: this type of question has appeared in many past papers; use Method 1.',1371,NULL,'parse-v1',NULL,NULL,'[[2, "Correct answer"], [1, "Uses a log law correctly (Method 1), or writes the factor $\\dfrac{1}{\\frac{x+1}{x-2}}$ (Method 2), or equivalent merit"]]','{"criteria_original": [[null, "Method 1: 1: correct use of log law"], [1, "correct differentiation of both. Method 2: 1: $\\dfrac{1}{\\frac{x+1}{x-2}}$"], [1, "correct quotient rule $\\dfrac{-3}{(x-2)^2}$. Marker: this type of question has appeared in many past papers; use Method 1."]], "top_criterion_original": "Method 1: 1: correct use of log law", "marker_note": "Method 1: 1 mark for the log law, 1 mark for differentiating both terms. Method 2: 1 mark for $\\dfrac{1}{\\frac{x+1}{x-2}}$, 1 mark for the quotient rule result $\\dfrac{-3}{(x-2)^2}$. This type of question has appeared in many past papers; use Method 1."}');
