$$\begin{aligned}&\text{horizontal dilation by factor } -1 &&\text{(reflect in the } y\text{-axis)}\\&\text{horizontal translation 1 unit right}\\&\text{vertical translation 2 units up}\end{aligned}$$
The horizontal dilation must happen before the horizontal translation; the vertical translation can come at any stage:
$$\begin{aligned}&\text{Option 1:} &&\text{dilation, horizontal translation, vertical translation}\\&\text{Option 2:} &&\text{vertical translation, dilation, horizontal translation}\\&\text{Option 3:} &&\text{dilation, vertical translation, horizontal translation}\end{aligned}$$
Using Option 1:
$$\begin{aligned}P(3,-5)&\mapsto(-3,-5) &&\text{reflect in the } y\text{-axis}\\&\mapsto(-2,-5) &&\text{translate 1 unit right}\\&\mapsto(-2,-3) &&\text{translate 2 units up}\end{aligned}$$
$\therefore$ the image is $P''(-2,-3)$.

The quick way: $P(x,y)\mapsto P''(1-x,\,y+2)$, so
$$P(3,-5)\mapsto P''(1-3,\,-5+2)=P''(-2,-3)$$','1: $(-3,-5)$; 1: $(-2,-5)$; $\frac{1}{2}$: $(-2,-3)$; $\frac{1}{2}$: all 3 transformations done in the correct order (zero if reflection order not demonstrated). Marker: many students did not write the function as $y=kf(a(x+b))+c$; some did not do the horizontal dilation before the horizontal translation; many did not use $P''$ notation for the image.',1371,NULL,'parse-v1',NULL,NULL,'[[3, "Correct answer"], [2, "Finds $(-2,-5)$ after the reflection and the horizontal translation, or equivalent merit"], [1, "Finds $(-3,-5)$ after the reflection, or equivalent merit"]]','{"criteria_original": [[1, "$(-3,-5)$"], [1, "$(-2,-5)$; $\\dfrac{1}{2}$: $(-2,-3)$; $\\dfrac{1}{2}$: all 3 transformations done in the correct order (zero if reflection order not demonstrated). Marker: many students did not write the function as $y=kf(a(x+b))+c$; some did not do the horizontal dilation before the horizontal translation; many did not use $P''$ notation for the image."]], "top_criterion_original": "$(-3,-5)$", "marker_note": "The last mark is $\\dfrac{1}{2}$ for $(-2,-3)$ and $\\dfrac{1}{2}$ for doing all 3 transformations in the correct order (zero if the reflection order is not demonstrated). The quick way does not show understanding but was still awarded. Many students did not write the function as $y=kf(a(x+b))+c$; some did not do the horizontal dilation before the horizontal translation; many did not use $P''$ notation for the image."}');
$$\begin{aligned}u&=x & v&=\ln x\\u''&=1 & v''&=\dfrac{1}{x}\end{aligned}$$
$$\begin{aligned}\dfrac{dy}{dx}&=x\times\dfrac{1}{x}+1\times\ln x\\&=1+\ln x\end{aligned}$$','1: correct derivative',1371,NULL,'parse-v1',NULL,NULL,'[[1, "Correct answer"]]','{"criteria_original": [[1, "correct derivative"]], "top_criterion_original": "correct derivative"}');
$$\begin{aligned}\int(1+\ln x)\,dx&=x\ln x+C\\\int1\,dx+\int\ln x\,dx&=x\ln x+C\\x+\int\ln x\,dx&=x\ln x+C\\\int\ln x\,dx&=x\ln x-x+C\end{aligned}$$
Method 2: from part (a), $\dfrac{d}{dx}(x\ln x)=\ln x+1$, so
$$\ln x=\dfrac{d}{dx}(x\ln x)-1$$
$$\begin{aligned}\int\ln x\,dx&=\int\left(\dfrac{d}{dx}(x\ln x)-1\right)dx\\&=x\ln x-x+C\end{aligned}$$','1: some progress, e.g. $\int 1+\ln x\,dx=x\ln x+C$ (or $\int\ln x\,dx=\int\frac{d}{dx}(x\ln x)-1\,dx$); 1: $x\ln x-x+C$. Lose $\frac{1}{2}$ for forgetting $+C$ (once only in Booklet 1).',1371,NULL,'parse-v1',NULL,NULL,'[[2, "Correct answer"], [1, "Some progress, e.g. $\\displaystyle\\int(1+\\ln x)\\,dx=x\\ln x+C$ or $\\displaystyle\\int\\ln x\\,dx=\\int\\left(\\dfrac{d}{dx}(x\\ln x)-1\\right)dx$, or equivalent merit"]]','{"criteria_original": [[1, "some progress, e.g. $\\int 1+\\ln x\\,dx=x\\ln x+C$ (or $\\int\\ln x\\,dx=\\int\\dfrac{d}{dx}(x\\ln x)-1\\,dx$)"], [1, "$x\\ln x-x+C$. Lose $\\dfrac{1}{2}$ for forgetting $+C$ (once only in Booklet 1)."]], "top_criterion_original": "some progress, e.g. $\\int 1+\\ln x\\,dx=x\\ln x+C$ (or $\\int\\ln x\\,dx=\\int\\dfrac{d}{dx}(x\\ln x)-1\\,dx$)", "marker_note": "Lose $\\dfrac{1}{2}$ mark for forgetting $+C$ (once only in Booklet 1)."}');

Using $S_n=\dfrac{n}{2}\big(2a+(n-1)d\big)$:
$$\begin{aligned}\dfrac{n}{2}\big(2(35)+(n-1)(-2)\big)&=260\\n(70-2n+2)&=520\\n(72-2n)&=520\\72n-2n^2&=520\\2n^2-72n+520&=0\\n^2-36n+260&=0\\n&=\dfrac{36\pm\sqrt{36^2-4(1)(260)}}{2}\\&=26\text{ or }10\end{aligned}$$
$$\begin{aligned}T_{26}&=35+(26-1)(-2)\\&=35+25(-2)\\&=-15\text{ cm}\end{aligned}$$
Lengths cannot be negative, so $n\ne26$.
$$\begin{aligned}T_{10}&=35+(10-1)(-2)\\&=17\text{ cm}\end{aligned}$$
$\therefore$ the length of the shortest piece is 17 cm.','1: correct equation $\dfrac{n}{2}\big(2(35)+(n-1)(-2)\big)=260$; 1: $n=26$ or $10$ (no $\frac{1}{2}$ mark awarded); $\frac{1}{2}$: $T_{26}=-15$ with appropriate justification for rejecting it; $\frac{1}{2}$: 17 cm. Marker: generally done well; a few did not find the $T_n$ values or did not eliminate the negative $T_n$ with an appropriate justification.',1371,NULL,'parse-v1',NULL,NULL,'[[3, "Correct answer"], [2, "Finds $n=26$ or $10$, or equivalent merit"], [1, "Forms the correct equation $\\dfrac{n}{2}\\big(2(35)+(n-1)(-2)\\big)=260$, or equivalent merit"]]','{"criteria_original": [[1, "correct equation $\\dfrac{n}{2}\\big(2(35)+(n-1)(-2)\\big)=260$"], [1, "$n=26$ or $10$ (no $\\dfrac{1}{2}$ mark awarded); $\\dfrac{1}{2}$: $T_{26}=-15$ with appropriate justification for rejecting it; $\\dfrac{1}{2}$: 17 cm. Marker: generally done well; a few did not find the $T_n$ values or did not eliminate the negative $T_n$ with an appropriate justification."]], "top_criterion_original": "correct equation $\\dfrac{n}{2}\\big(2(35)+(n-1)(-2)\\big)=260$", "marker_note": "The last mark is $\\dfrac{1}{2}$ for $T_{26}=-15$ with a justification for rejecting it and $\\dfrac{1}{2}$ for 17 cm. Generally done well; a few did not find the $T_n$ values or did not eliminate the negative $T_n$ with an appropriate justification."}');

