As the particle starts from the origin, it must end on the negative side, since the overall ''area'' is negative.

Note that the area under the velocity–time graph represents the distance travelled, not the displacement. The distance travelled to the left is more than the distance travelled to the right, hence the displacement is in the negative direction (towards the left).','1: correct explanation. Marker: not attempted well.',1371,NULL,'parse-v1',NULL,NULL,'[[1, "Correct answer"]]','{"criteria_original": [[1, "correct explanation. Marker: not attempted well."]], "top_criterion_original": "correct explanation. Marker: not attempted well.", "marker_note": "Not attempted well."}');
$$\begin{aligned}&\dfrac{(x+y)^2}{16}\\&=\dfrac{x^2+2xy+y^2}{16}\\&=\dfrac{14xy+2xy}{16}\\&=\dfrac{16xy}{16}\\&=xy\end{aligned}$$','1: expanding $(x+y)^2$; 1: substituting $x^2+y^2=14xy$ to get $\dfrac{16xy}{16}$',1371,NULL,'parse-v1',NULL,NULL,'[[2, "Correct answer"], [1, "Expands $(x+y)^2$, or equivalent merit"]]','{"criteria_original": [[1, "expanding $(x+y)^2$"], [1, "substituting $x^2+y^2=14xy$ to get $\\dfrac{16xy}{16}$"]], "top_criterion_original": "expanding $(x+y)^2$"}');
$$\begin{aligned}\text{LHS}&=\dfrac{1}{2}(\log x+\log y)\\&=\dfrac{1}{2}(\log xy)\\&=\dfrac{1}{2}\left(\log\dfrac{(x+y)^2}{16}\right)&&\text{from part (a)}\\&=\dfrac{1}{2}\left(\log\left(\dfrac{x+y}{4}\right)^2\right)\\&=\dfrac{1}{2}\times2\left(\log\left(\dfrac{x+y}{4}\right)\right)\\&=\log\left(\dfrac{x+y}{4}\right)\\&=\text{RHS}\end{aligned}$$
$\therefore$ LHS $=$ RHS, as required.

Alternative method:
$$\begin{aligned}\text{RHS}&=\log\left(\dfrac{x+y}{4}\right)\\&=\log\left(\sqrt{xy}\right)&&\text{from part (a)}\\&=\log(xy)^{\frac{1}{2}}\\&=\dfrac{1}{2}\log(xy)\\&=\dfrac{1}{2}(\log x+\log y)\\&=\text{LHS}\end{aligned}$$
Alternative method:
$$\begin{aligned}\text{RHS}&=\log\left(\dfrac{x+y}{4}\right)\\&=\log\left(\left(\dfrac{x+y}{4}\right)^2\right)^{\frac{1}{2}}\\&=\dfrac{1}{2}\log\left(\dfrac{(x+y)^2}{16}\right)\\&=\dfrac{1}{2}\log xy\\&=\dfrac{1}{2}(\log x+\log y)\end{aligned}$$
Alternative method:
$$\begin{aligned}\text{LHS}&=\dfrac{1}{2}\log(xy)\\&=\dfrac{1}{2}\log\left(\dfrac{(x+y)^2}{16}\right)&&\text{from part (a)}\\&=\dfrac{1}{2}\log(x+y)^2-\dfrac{1}{2}\log16\\&=\log(x+y)^{2\times\frac{1}{2}}-\log16^{\frac{1}{2}}\\&=\log(x+y)-\log4\\&=\log\left(\dfrac{x+y}{4}\right)\\&=\text{RHS}\end{aligned}$$
Alternative method:
$$\begin{aligned}\text{LHS}&=\dfrac{1}{2}(\log x+\log y)\\&=\dfrac{1}{2}\log x+\dfrac{1}{2}\log y\\&=\log x^{\frac{1}{2}}+\log y^{\frac{1}{2}}\\&=\log\left(x^{\frac{1}{2}}\times y^{\frac{1}{2}}\right)\\&=\log(xy)^{\frac{1}{2}}\\&=\dfrac{1}{2}\log xy\\&=\dfrac{1}{2}\log\left(\dfrac{x+y}{4}\right)^2\\&=\log\left(\dfrac{x+y}{4}\right)^{2\times\frac{1}{2}}\\&=\log\left(\dfrac{x+y}{4}\right)\\&=\text{RHS}\end{aligned}$$','1: using any 2 relevant logarithmic laws; 1: substantial progress towards the solution.',1371,NULL,'parse-v1',NULL,NULL,'[[2, "Correct answer"], [1, "Uses any 2 relevant logarithm laws, or equivalent merit"]]','{"criteria_original": [[1, "using any 2 relevant logarithmic laws"], [1, "substantial progress towards the solution."]], "top_criterion_original": "using any 2 relevant logarithmic laws", "marker_note": "The second mark is for substantial progress towards the solution."}');

