Points of intersection:
$$\begin{aligned}x^2-3x-3&=x+2\\x^2-4x-5&=0\\(x-5)(x+1)&=0\\x=5\text{ or }x&=-1\end{aligned}$$
The line $y=x+2$ is above the parabola on $[-1,5]$, so the area is
$$\int_{-1}^{5}(\text{top curve}-\text{bottom curve})\,dx$$
Note: the area between the curves does not change if the region is translated, so no absolute value is needed as long as one graph is above the other throughout the interval, even if some of the region is below the $x$-axis.
$$\begin{aligned}&\int_{-1}^{5}\big((x+2)-(x^2-3x-3)\big)\,dx\\&=\int_{-1}^{5}(x+2-x^2+3x+3)\,dx\\&=\int_{-1}^{5}(-x^2+4x+5)\,dx\\&=\left[-\dfrac{x^3}{3}+2x^2+5x\right]_{-1}^{5}\\&=\left(-\dfrac{5^3}{3}+2(5)^2+5(5)\right)-\left(\dfrac{1}{3}+2-5\right)\\&=36\text{ units}^2\end{aligned}$$','1: points of intersection $x=5$ or $x=-1$; 1: correct integrand $-x^2+4x+5$; 1: correct primitive; 1: 36 units$^2$. Marker: poorly attempted.',1371,NULL,'parse-v1',NULL,NULL,'[[4, "Correct answer"], [3, "Finds the correct primitive, or equivalent merit"], [2, "Finds the correct integrand $-x^2+4x+5$, or equivalent merit"], [1, "Finds the points of intersection $x=5$ and $x=-1$, or equivalent merit"]]','{"criteria_original": [[1, "points of intersection $x=5$ or $x=-1$"], [1, "correct integrand $-x^2+4x+5$"], [1, "correct primitive"], [1, "36 units$^2$. Marker: poorly attempted."]], "top_criterion_original": "points of intersection $x=5$ or $x=-1$", "source_original": "$-\\left(+\\dfrac{1}{3}-2+5\\right)$", "corrected": "Corrected from source: the lower-limit bracket read (+1/3 − 2 + 5); substituting x = −1 gives (1/3 + 2 − 5). Answer 36 units² unchanged.", "marker_note": "Poorly attempted."}');
$$\begin{aligned}a&=\sin^2x\cos^2x\\r&=\dfrac{2\sin^2x\cos^4x}{\sin^2x\cos^2x}\\&=2\cos^2x\end{aligned}$$
$$\begin{aligned}\therefore S&=\dfrac{\sin^2x\cos^2x}{1-2\cos^2x}\\&=\dfrac{\sin^2x\cos^2x}{1-\cos^2x-\cos^2x}\\&=\dfrac{\sin^2x\cos^2x}{\sin^2x-\cos^2x}&&(\sin^2x=1-\cos^2x)\end{aligned}$$
as required.','2: correct steps to show $S$; $1\frac{1}{2}$: finding the common ratio $r=2\cos^2x$ and using the limiting sum formula correctly and writing $S=\dfrac{\sin^2x\cos^2x}{1-2\cos^2x}$; 1: using the limiting sum formula correctly with an incorrect common ratio; $\frac{1}{2}$: the common ratio was correctly found. Marker: answered well overall but some students applied Ext 1 trig identities to finish the proof, e.g. $\dfrac{\sin^2x\cos^2x}{1-2\cos^2x}=\dfrac{\sin^2x\cos^2x}{-(2\cos^2x-1)}=\dfrac{\sin^2x\cos^2x}{-\cos2x}=\dfrac{\sin^2x\cos^2x}{-(\cos^2x-\sin^2x)}$; this was accepted but is not intended in an Advanced paper.',1371,NULL,'parse-v1',NULL,NULL,'[[2, "Correct answer"], [1, "Uses the limiting sum formula correctly with an incorrect common ratio, or equivalent merit"]]','{"criteria_original": [[2, "correct steps to show $S$; $1\\dfrac{1}{2}$: finding the common ratio $r=2\\cos^2x$ and using the limiting sum formula correctly and writing $S=\\dfrac{\\sin^2x\\cos^2x}{1-2\\cos^2x}$"], [1, "using the limiting sum formula correctly with an incorrect common ratio; $\\dfrac{1}{2}$: the common ratio was correctly found. Marker: answered well overall but some students applied Ext 1 trig identities to finish the proof, e.g. $\\dfrac{\\sin^2x\\cos^2x}{1-2\\cos^2x}=\\dfrac{\\sin^2x\\cos^2x}{-(2\\cos^2x-1)}=\\dfrac{\\sin^2x\\cos^2x}{-\\cos2x}=\\dfrac{\\sin^2x\\cos^2x}{-(\\cos^2x-\\sin^2x)}$; this was accepted but is not intended in an Advanced paper."]], "top_criterion_original": "correct steps to show $S$; $1\\dfrac{1}{2}$: finding the common ratio $r=2\\cos^2x$ and using the limiting sum formula correctly and writing $S=\\dfrac{\\sin^2x\\cos^2x}{1-2\\cos^2x}$", "marker_note": "$1\\dfrac{1}{2}$ marks for finding the common ratio $r=2\\cos^2x$, using the limiting sum formula correctly and writing $S=\\dfrac{\\sin^2x\\cos^2x}{1-2\\cos^2x}$; $\\dfrac{1}{2}$ mark for finding the common ratio correctly. Answered well overall, but some students applied Extension 1 trig identities to finish the proof, e.g. $\\dfrac{\\sin^2x\\cos^2x}{1-2\\cos^2x}=\\dfrac{\\sin^2x\\cos^2x}{-(2\\cos^2x-1)}=\\dfrac{\\sin^2x\\cos^2x}{-\\cos2x}=\\dfrac{\\sin^2x\\cos^2x}{-(\\cos^2x-\\sin^2x)}$; this was accepted but is not intended in an Advanced paper."}');
$$\begin{aligned}\dfrac{\sin^2x\cos^2x}{\sin^2x-\cos^2x}&=\dfrac{\sin^2x\cos^2x\div\cos^2x}{(\sin^2x-\cos^2x)\div\cos^2x}\\&=\dfrac{\sin^2x}{\frac{\sin^2x}{\cos^2x}-1}\\&=\dfrac{\sin^2x}{\tan^2x-1}\\&=\dfrac{1}{\operatorname{cosec}^2x(\tan^2x-1)}&&\text{since }\operatorname{cosec}x=\dfrac{1}{\sin x}\end{aligned}$$
Alternative method:
$$\begin{aligned}\dfrac{1}{\operatorname{cosec}^2x(\tan^2x-1)}&=\dfrac{1}{\frac{1}{\sin^2x}\left(\frac{\sin^2x}{\cos^2x}-1\right)}\\&=\dfrac{\sin^2x}{\frac{\sin^2x-\cos^2x}{\cos^2x}}\times\dfrac{\cos^2x}{\cos^2x}\\&=\dfrac{\sin^2x\cos^2x}{\sin^2x-\cos^2x}\end{aligned}$$
Alternative method:
$$\begin{aligned}\dfrac{1}{\operatorname{cosec}^2x(\tan^2x-1)}&=\dfrac{1}{\frac{1}{\sin^2x}\left(\frac{\sin^2x}{\cos^2x}-1\right)}\\&=\dfrac{1}{\frac{1}{\cos^2x}-\frac{1}{\sin^2x}}\\&=\dfrac{1}{\frac{\sin^2x-\cos^2x}{\cos^2x\sin^2x}}\times\dfrac{\cos^2x\sin^2x}{\cos^2x\sin^2x}\\&=\dfrac{\sin^2x\cos^2x}{\sin^2x-\cos^2x}\end{aligned}$$','2: complete solution; 1: progress towards solution ($\frac{1}{2}$ mark each for using $\csc^2x=\dfrac{1}{\sin^2x}$ and $\tan^2x=\dfrac{\sin^2x}{\cos^2x}$); $\frac{1}{2}$: incorrect methods used to prove but at least one trig identity used correctly. Marker: not well answered; many used $\sec^2x=\tan^2x-1$, which is not correct; the correct identity is $1+\tan^2x=\sec^2x$.',1371,NULL,'parse-v1',NULL,NULL,'[[2, "Correct answer"], [1, "Progress towards the solution, or equivalent merit"]]','{"criteria_original": [[2, "complete solution"], [1, "progress towards solution ($\\dfrac{1}{2}$ mark each for using $\\csc^2x=\\dfrac{1}{\\sin^2x}$ and $\\tan^2x=\\dfrac{\\sin^2x}{\\cos^2x}$); $\\dfrac{1}{2}$: incorrect methods used to prove but at least one trig identity used correctly. Marker: not well answered; many used $\\sec^2x=\\tan^2x-1$, which is not correct; the correct identity is $1+\\tan^2x=\\sec^2x$."]], "top_criterion_original": "complete solution", "marker_note": "$\\dfrac{1}{2}$ mark each for using $\\operatorname{cosec}^2x=\\dfrac{1}{\\sin^2x}$ and $\\tan^2x=\\dfrac{\\sin^2x}{\\cos^2x}$; $\\dfrac{1}{2}$ mark for incorrect methods with at least one trig identity used correctly. Not well answered: many used $\\sec^2x=\\tan^2x-1$, which is not correct; the correct identity is $1+\\tan^2x=\\sec^2x$."}');
$$\dfrac{x(2-x)}{e^x}=0$$
$e^x>0$, so $x=0$ or $x=2$.

When $x=0$, $y=0$; when $x=2$, $y=4e^{-2}$.

At $(0,0)$:
$$\dfrac{d^2y}{dx^2}=\dfrac{2}{1}=2>0$$
so the curve is concave up and $(0,0)$ is a local minimum.

At $(2,4e^{-2})$:
$$\dfrac{d^2y}{dx^2}=\dfrac{4-8+2}{e^2}=-\dfrac{2}{e^2}<0$$
so the curve is concave down and $(2,4e^{-2})$ is a local maximum.','2: complete solution; $1\frac{1}{2}$: almost the complete solution but not including the stationary points $(0,0)$ and $(2,4e^{-2})$; 1: finding the stationary points; $\frac{1}{2}$: finding $x=0$, $x=2$. Marker: well done overall.',1371,NULL,'parse-v1',NULL,NULL,'[[2, "Correct answer"], [1, "Finds the stationary points, or equivalent merit"]]','{"criteria_original": [[2, "complete solution; $1\\dfrac{1}{2}$: almost the complete solution but not including the stationary points $(0,0)$ and $(2,4e^{-2})$"], [1, "finding the stationary points; $\\dfrac{1}{2}$: finding $x=0$, $x=2$. Marker: well done overall."]], "top_criterion_original": "complete solution; $1\\dfrac{1}{2}$: almost the complete solution but not including the stationary points $(0,0)$ and $(2,4e^{-2})$", "source_original": "At $(2,4e^{-2})$, $\\dfrac{d^2y}{dx^2}=\\dfrac{4-8+2}{e^2}=-\\dfrac{6}{e^2}<0$", "corrected": "Corrected from source: (4 − 8 + 2)/e² = −2/e², not −6/e²; the nature (local maximum) is unchanged.", "marker_note": "$1\\dfrac{1}{2}$ marks for an almost complete solution without stating the stationary points $(0,0)$ and $(2,4e^{-2})$; $\\dfrac{1}{2}$ mark for finding $x=0$, $x=2$. Well done overall."}');

\exfigure{1371_q32_s1}','$\frac{1}{2}$: shape; 1: turning points; $\frac{1}{2}$: points of inflection. Marker: overall this graph was well drawn.',1371,NULL,'parse-v1',NULL,NULL,'[[2, "Correct answer"], [1, "Shows the turning points correctly, or equivalent merit"]]','{"criteria_original": [[null, "$\\dfrac{1}{2}$: shape"], [1, "turning points; $\\dfrac{1}{2}$: points of inflection. Marker: overall this graph was well drawn."]], "top_criterion_original": "$\\dfrac{1}{2}$: shape", "marker_note": "Marked as $\\dfrac{1}{2}$ for the shape, 1 for the turning points and $\\dfrac{1}{2}$ for the points of inflection. Overall this graph was well drawn."}');
$$P=3r+r\theta\qquad(1)$$
Substituting $P=10$ into (1):
$$\begin{aligned}3r+r\theta&=10\\r\theta&=10-3r\\\theta&=\dfrac{10-3r}{r}\qquad(2)\end{aligned}$$
Using the sector area $\dfrac{1}{2}r^2\theta$ and (2):
$$\begin{aligned}A_1=\text{Area of sector}&=\dfrac{1}{2}r^2\theta\\&=\dfrac{1}{2}r^2\left(\dfrac{10-3r}{r}\right)\\&=5r-\dfrac{3}{2}r^2\end{aligned}$$
Using $\dfrac{1}{2}ab\sin C$ for the equilateral triangle (each angle $60^\circ$):
$$\begin{aligned}A_2=\text{Area of triangle}&=\dfrac{1}{2}r^2\sin(60^\circ)\\&=\dfrac{1}{2}r^2\times\dfrac{\sqrt{3}}{2}\\&=\dfrac{\sqrt{3}}{4}r^2\end{aligned}$$
$$\begin{aligned}\therefore A&=A_1+A_2\\&=5r-\dfrac{3}{2}r^2+\dfrac{\sqrt{3}}{4}r^2\\&=5r+\dfrac{-6}{4}r^2+\dfrac{\sqrt{3}}{4}r^2\\A&=5r+\dfrac{\sqrt{3}-6}{4}r^2\end{aligned}$$','3: correct solution; 2: finding $\theta=\dfrac{10-3r}{r}$ and the area of the sector and the area of the triangle correctly; 1: expressing $\theta$ as $\dfrac{10-3r}{r}$, or finding the area of the triangle and area of sector; $\frac{1}{2}$: expressing $P=3r+r\theta$, or finding either the area of the triangle or the area of the sector correctly. Marker: review the arc length ($l=r\theta$) and sector area ($A=\frac{1}{2}r^2\theta$) formulas; many wrote $P=3r+AB$ or used $\frac{\theta}{360^\circ}\times\pi r^2$ with an angle in radians.',1371,NULL,'parse-v1',NULL,NULL,'[[3, "Correct answer"], [2, "Finds $\\theta=\\dfrac{10-3r}{r}$ and the areas of the sector and the triangle correctly, or equivalent merit"], [1, "Expresses $\\theta$ as $\\dfrac{10-3r}{r}$, or finds the areas of the triangle and the sector, or equivalent merit"]]','{"criteria_original": [[3, "correct solution"], [2, "finding $\\theta=\\dfrac{10-3r}{r}$ and the area of the sector and the area of the triangle correctly"], [1, "expressing $\\theta$ as $\\dfrac{10-3r}{r}$, or finding the area of the triangle and area of sector; $\\dfrac{1}{2}$: expressing $P=3r+r\\theta$, or finding either the area of the triangle or the area of the sector correctly. Marker: review the arc length ($l=r\\theta$) and sector area ($A=\\dfrac{1}{2}r^2\\theta$) formulas; many wrote $P=3r+AB$ or used $\\dfrac{\\theta}{360^\\circ}\\times\\pi r^2$ with an angle in radians."]], "top_criterion_original": "correct solution", "marker_note": "$\\dfrac{1}{2}$ mark for $P=3r+r\\theta$, or for finding either the area of the triangle or the area of the sector. Review the arc length ($l=r\\theta$) and sector area ($A=\\dfrac{1}{2}r^2\\theta$) formulas; many wrote $P=3r+AB$ or used $\\dfrac{\\theta}{360^\\circ}\\times\\pi r^2$ with an angle in radians."}');
At the maximum area, $\dfrac{dA}{dr}=0$:
$$\begin{aligned}\dfrac{dA}{dr}=5+\dfrac{\sqrt{3}-6}{2}r&=0\\\therefore r&=-5\times\dfrac{2}{\sqrt{3}-6}\\&=\dfrac{10}{6-\sqrt{3}}\end{aligned}$$
Testing the nature:
$$\dfrac{d^2A}{dr^2}=\dfrac{\sqrt{3}-6}{2}\approx-2.134<0$$
so the maximum occurs at $r=\dfrac{10}{6-\sqrt{3}}$.
