$\therefore\dfrac{t}{t^2+1}\ge0$, since the quotient of two positives is positive.

$\therefore 2+\dfrac{t}{t^2+1}\ge2$

$\therefore v$ is always positive and never equals zero, so the particle is never at rest.

Or, using the discriminant: if the particle is at rest, $v=0$:
$$\begin{aligned}2+\dfrac{t}{t^2+1}&=0\\2t^2+2+t&=0\\2t^2+t+2&=0\\\Delta&=1^2-4(2)(2)\\&=-15<0\end{aligned}$$
$\Delta<0$, so there is no solution: the particle is never at rest.','0.5 for any mention of $t^2\ge0$ or $t^2+1>0$. If $\dfrac{t}{t^2+1}>0$ is stated: 1 mark if $t^2>0$ stated previously; 1.5 marks if $t^2>0$ AND $t>0$ stated; 0 marks if only $t>0$ stated. 0.5 for observing large values of $t$ (e.g. stating $\dfrac{t}{t^2+1}>0$ hence $\ne-2$; $\lim_{t\to\infty}\dfrac{t}{t^2+1}=0$). Discriminant method: 1 for reaching $2t^2+t+2=0$; 0.5 for the discriminant or quadratic formula; 0.5 for interpretation of the discriminant. Marker: observing $t\to\infty$ alone does not consider earlier values (e.g. $\lim_{t\to\infty}\frac{\sin t}{t}+\frac{1}{5}=\frac{1}{5}$ but $\frac{\sin t}{t}+\frac{1}{5}=0$ at $t\approx4.10462$ and $4.9063$); practise supporting arguments in explain questions.',1371,NULL,'parse-v1',NULL,NULL,'[[2, "Correct answer"], [1, "Shows $\\dfrac{t}{t^2+1}\\ge0$ with a partial justification, or reaches $2t^2+t+2=0$, or equivalent merit"]]','{"criteria_original": [[null, "0.5 for any mention of $t^2\\ge0$ or $t^2+1>0$. If $\\dfrac{t}{t^2+1}>0$ is stated: 1 mark if $t^2>0$ stated previously; 1.5 marks if $t^2>0$ AND $t>0$ stated; 0 marks if only $t>0$ stated. 0.5 for observing large values of $t$ (e.g. stating $\\dfrac{t}{t^2+1}>0$ hence $\\ne-2$; $\\lim_{t\\to\\infty}\\dfrac{t}{t^2+1}=0$). Discriminant method: 1 for reaching $2t^2+t+2=0$; 0.5 for the discriminant or quadratic formula; 0.5 for interpretation of the discriminant. Marker: observing $t\\to\\infty$ alone does not consider earlier values (e.g. $\\lim_{t\\to\\infty}\\dfrac{\\sin t}{t}+\\dfrac{1}{5}=\\dfrac{1}{5}$ but $\\dfrac{\\sin t}{t}+\\dfrac{1}{5}=0$ at $t\\approx4.10462$ and $4.9063$); practise supporting arguments in explain questions."]], "top_criterion_original": "0.5 for any mention of $t^2\\ge0$ or $t^2+1>0$. If $\\dfrac{t}{t^2+1}>0$ is stated: 1 mark if $t^2>0$ stated previously; 1.5 marks if $t^2>0$ AND $t>0$ stated; 0 marks if only $t>0$ stated. 0.5 for observing large values of $t$ (e.g.