$$\begin{aligned}\int_{\frac{1}{3}}^{\frac{1}{2}}\sec^2\left(\dfrac{\pi x}{2}\right)dx&=\dfrac{2}{\pi}\int_{\frac{1}{3}}^{\frac{1}{2}}\dfrac{\pi}{2}\sec^2\left(\dfrac{\pi x}{2}\right)dx\\&=\dfrac{2}{\pi}\left[\tan\left(\dfrac{\pi x}{2}\right)\right]_{\frac{1}{3}}^{\frac{1}{2}}\\&=\dfrac{2}{\pi}\left[\tan\left(\dfrac{\pi}{4}\right)-\tan\left(\dfrac{\pi}{6}\right)\right]\\&=\dfrac{2}{\pi}\left[1-\dfrac{1}{\sqrt{3}}\right]\\&=\dfrac{6-2\sqrt{3}}{3\pi}\\&=\dfrac{2(3-\sqrt{3})}{3\pi}\end{aligned}$$','2: Correct answer; 1: Correct primitive $\dfrac{2}{\pi}\left[\tan\left(\dfrac{\pi x}{2}\right)\right]$, or equivalent merit',1372,NULL,'parse-v1',NULL,NULL,'[[2, "Correct answer"], [1, "Correct primitive $\\dfrac{2}{\\pi}\\left[\\tan\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi x}{2}\\right)\\right]$, or equivalent merit"]]','{"source_original": "$=\\dfrac{2(2-\\sqrt{3})}{3\\pi}$", "corrected": "Corrected from source: the last line factorised 6 − 2√3 as 2(2 − √3); it is 2(3 − √3), so the answer is 2(3 − √3)/(3π) = (6 − 2√3)/(3π)."}');

Substituting into $5x+y^2=39$:
$$\begin{aligned}5(12-2y)+y^2&=39\\60-10y+y^2&=39\\y^2-10y+21&=0\\(y-7)(y-3)&=0\end{aligned}$$
