$$\begin{aligned}\dfrac{d}{dx}\Big[\ln\left(e^x\sin x\right)\Big]&=\dfrac{d}{dx}\Big[\ln e^x+\ln(\sin x)\Big]\\&=\dfrac{d}{dx}\Big[x+\ln(\sin x)\Big]\\&=1+\cot x\end{aligned}$$
Alternative, using the product rule, $\dfrac{d}{dx}(uv)=u''v+uv''$, with
$$\begin{aligned}u&=e^x & v&=\sin x\\u''&=e^x & v''&=\cos x\end{aligned}$$
$$\dfrac{d}{dx}\left(e^x\sin x\right)=e^x\cos x+e^x\sin x$$
Then, using $\dfrac{d}{dx}\ln f(x)=\dfrac{f''(x)}{f(x)}$:
$$\begin{aligned}\dfrac{d}{dx}\Big[\ln\left(e^x\sin x\right)\Big]&=\dfrac{e^x(\cos x+\sin x)}{e^x\sin x}\\&=\dfrac{\cos x+\sin x}{\sin x}\\&=1+\cot x\end{aligned}$$','2: Provides the correct solution; 1: Attempted to use the product rule correctly',1372,NULL,'parse-v1',NULL,NULL,'[[2, "Correct answer"], [1, "Attempts to use the product rule correctly, or equivalent merit"]]','{"criteria_original": [[2, "Provides the correct solution"], [1, "Attempted to use the product rule correctly"]], "top_criterion_original": "Provides the correct solution"}');
$$\int\dfrac{\cos2x}{\sin2x}\,dx+\int x^{-\frac{3}{2}}\,dx$$
Using $\displaystyle\int\dfrac{f''(x)}{f(x)}\,dx=\ln|f(x)|+c$ with $f(x)=\sin2x$, $f''(x)=2\cos2x$:
$$\begin{aligned}&\int\dfrac{1}{2}\left(\dfrac{2\cos2x}{\sin2x}\right)dx+\int x^{-\frac{3}{2}}\,dx\\&=\dfrac{1}{2}\ln|\sin2x|+\dfrac{x^{-\frac{1}{2}}}{-\frac{1}{2}}+c\\&=\dfrac{1}{2}\ln|\sin2x|-2x^{-\frac{1}{2}}+c\\&=\dfrac{1}{2}\ln|\sin2x|-\dfrac{2}{\sqrt{x}}+c\end{aligned}$$','2: Provides the correct solution with absolute value; 1.5: $\dfrac{1}{2}\ln(\sin 2x)-2x^{-\frac{1}{2}}+c$, i.e. left out the absolute value; 0.5: Rewrites the integral as $\displaystyle\int\dfrac{\cos 2x}{\sin 2x}\,dx+\int x^{-\frac{3}{2}}\,dx$',1372,NULL,'parse-v1',NULL,NULL,'[[2, "Correct answer"]]','{"criteria_original": [[2, "Provides the correct solution with absolute value; 1.5: $\\dfrac{1}{2}\\ln(\\sin 2x)-2x^{-\\frac{1}{2}}+c$, i.e. left out the absolute value; 0.5: Rewrites the integral as $\\displaystyle\\int\\dfrac{\\cos 2x}{\\sin 2x}\\,dx+\\int x^{-\\frac{3}{2}}\\,dx$"]], "top_criterion_original": "Provides the correct solution with absolute value; 1.5: $\\dfrac{1}{2}\\ln(\\sin 2x)-2x^{-\\frac{1}{2}}+c$, i.e. left out the absolute value; 0.5: Rewrites the integral as $\\displaystyle\\int\\dfrac{\\cos 2x}{\\sin 2x}\\,dx+\\int x^{-\\frac{3}{2}}\\,dx$", "marker_note": "$1\\dfrac{1}{2}$ marks for $\\dfrac{1}{2}\\ln(\\sin2x)-2x^{-\\frac{1}{2}}+c$ (absolute value left out); $\\dfrac{1}{2}$ mark for rewriting the integral as $\\displaystyle\\int\\dfrac{\\cos2x}{\\sin2x}\\,dx+\\int x^{-\\frac{3}{2}}\\,dx$. The absolute value is required for full marks."}');
