To find the points of intersection, equate the two expressions for $y$:
$$\begin{aligned} \dfrac{x}{e}&=xe^{-x}\\ xe^{-1}&=xe^{-x} \end{aligned}$$
So $x=0$ or $e^{-1}=e^{-x}$, giving $x=1$.

Using the primitive of $xe^{-x}$ from part (b), the shaded area is
$$\begin{aligned} \int_0^1 \left(xe^{-x}-\dfrac{x}{e}\right)dx&=\left[-e^{-x}-xe^{-x}-\dfrac{x^2}{2e}\right]_0^1\\ &=\left(-e^{-1}-e^{-1}-\dfrac{1}{2e}\right)-(-1-0-0)\\ &=1-\dfrac{5}{2e} \end{aligned}$$','4: Correct final answer; 3: Correctly integrates the expression; 2: Provides a correct expression for the shaded area; 1: Correctly determines the points of intersection',1372,NULL,'parse-v1',NULL,NULL,'[[4, "Correct answer"], [3, "Correctly integrates the expression, or equivalent merit"], [2, "Provides a correct expression for the shaded area, or equivalent merit"], [1, "Correctly determines the points of intersection, or equivalent merit"]]','{"criteria_original": [[4, "Correct final answer"], [3, "Correctly integrates the expression"], [2, "Provides a correct expression for the shaded area"], [1, "Correctly determines the points of intersection"]], "top_criterion_original": "Correct final answer"}');

The numerator is a multiple of the derivative of $5-x^2$, which is $-2x$:
$$\begin{aligned} \int_0^2\dfrac{kx}{5-x^2}\,dx&=1\\ \dfrac{k}{-2}\int_0^2\dfrac{-2x}{5-x^2}\,dx&=1\\ \dfrac{k}{-2}\Big[\ln\left(5-x^2\right)\Big]_0^2&=1\\ k(\ln 1-\ln 5)&=-2 \end{aligned}$$
$$\therefore k=\dfrac{-2}{-\ln 5}=\dfrac{2}{\ln 5}$$','2: Correct proof; 1: Correct primitive $\dfrac{k}{-2}\ln\left(5-x^2\right)$, or equivalent merit',1372,NULL,'parse-v1',NULL,NULL,'[[2, "Correct answer"], [1, "Correct primitive $\\dfrac{k}{-2}\\ln\\left(5-x^2\\right)$, or equivalent merit"]]','{"criteria_original": [[2, "Correct proof"], [1, "Correct primitive $\\dfrac{k}{-2}\\ln\\left(5-x^2\\right)$, or equivalent merit"]], "top_criterion_original": "Correct proof"}');
$$F(x)=\begin{cases} 0, & x<0\\ p(x), & 0\le x\le 2\\ 1, & x>2 \end{cases}$$
where
$$\begin{aligned} p(x)&=\int_0^x\dfrac{kt}{5-t^2}\,dt\\ &=\dfrac{k}{-2}\int_0^x\dfrac{-2t}{5-t^2}\,dt\\ &=\dfrac{k}{-2}\Big[\ln\left(5-t^2\right)\Big]_0^x\\ &=-\dfrac{1}{\ln 5}\Big(\ln\left(5-x^2\right)-\ln 5\Big)\\ &=-\dfrac{1}{\ln 5}\ln\left(5-x^2\right)+1 \end{aligned}$$','2: Correct piecewise $F(x)$ including the pieces for $x<0$ and $x>2$; 1: Finds $-\dfrac{1}{\ln 5}\ln\left(5-x^2\right)+1$ for $0\le x\le 2$, or equivalent merit',1372,NULL,'parse-v1',NULL,NULL,'[[2, "Correct answer"], [1, "Finds $-\\dfrac{1}{\\ln 5}\\ln\\left(5-x^2\\right)+1$ for $0\\le x\\le 2$, or equivalent merit"]]','{"criteria_original": [[2, "Correct piecewise $F(x)$ including the pieces for $x<0$ and $x>2$"], [1, "Finds $-\\dfrac{1}{\\ln 5}\\ln\\left(5-x^2\\right)+1$ for $0\\le x\\le 2$, or equivalent merit"]], "top_criterion_original": "Correct piecewise $F(x)$ including the pieces for $x<0$ and $x>2$", "marker_note": "Full marks need the pieces for $x<0$ and $x>2$."}');

Method 1:
$$\begin{aligned} -\dfrac{1}{\ln 5}\ln\left(5-m^2\right)+1&=\dfrac{1}{2}\\ \dfrac{1}{\ln 5}\ln\left(5-m^2\right)&=\dfrac{1}{2}\\ \ln\left(5-m^2\right)&=\dfrac{1}{2}\ln 5\\ &=\ln\sqrt{5}\\ 5-m^2&=\sqrt{5}\\ m^2&=5-\sqrt{5} \end{aligned}$$
$$\therefore m=\sqrt{5-\sqrt{5}}\quad(\text{since }0\le m\le 2)$$
Method 2: using $\ln\sqrt{5}=\dfrac{1}{2}\ln 5$,
$$\begin{aligned} p\left(\sqrt{5-\sqrt{5}}\right)&=-\dfrac{1}{\ln 5}\ln\left[5-\left(\sqrt{5-\sqrt{5}}\right)^2\right]+1\\ &=-\dfrac{1}{\ln 5}\ln\left[5-\left(5-\sqrt{5}\right)\right]+1\\ &=-\dfrac{1}{\ln 5}\ln\sqrt{5}+1\\ &=-\dfrac{1}{2}+1\\ &=\dfrac{1}{2} \end{aligned}$$
$\therefore$ the median is $\sqrt{5-\sqrt{5}}$.','2: Correct proof; 1: Sets $F(m)=\dfrac{1}{2}$ using the expression from part (b), or equivalent merit',1372,NULL,'parse-v1',NULL,NULL,'[[2, "Correct answer"], [1, "Sets $F(m)=\\dfrac{1}{2}$ using the expression from part (b), or equivalent merit"]]','{"source_original": "Method 2 first line: $p\\left(\\sqrt{5-\\sqrt{5}}\\right)=-\\frac{1}{\\ln 5}\\ln\\left(5-m^2\\right)+1$; part (b) uses $x$ as both limit and integration variable", "corrected": "Corrected from source: Method 2 first line wrote ln(5 - m^2) before substituting; dummy variable renamed t in part (b). Mathematics unchanged.", "criteria_original": [[2, "Correct proof"], [1, "Sets $F(m)=\\dfrac{1}{2}$ using the expression from part (b), or equivalent merit"]], "top_criterion_original": "Correct proof"}');
The pdf is $F''(x)$:
$$F''(x)=\dfrac{1}{16}\left(10x-5x^{1.5}\right)$$
The mode is where the pdf takes its greatest value in $0\le x\le 4$, so solve $F''''(x)=0$:
$$\begin{aligned} F''''(x)=\dfrac{1}{16}\left(10-7.5x^{\frac{1}{2}}\right)&=0\\ 7.5x^{\frac{1}{2}}&=10\\ x^{\frac{1}{2}}&=\dfrac{10}{7.5}\\ &=\dfrac{4}{3}\\ x&=\dfrac{16}{9} \end{aligned}$$
Testing with the third derivative:
$$F''''''(x)=\dfrac{1}{32}\left(-7.5x^{-\frac{1}{2}}\right)$$
$F''''''\left(\dfrac{16}{9}\right)<0$, so the pdf has a global maximum there.
$$\therefore \text{mode}=\dfrac{16}{9}$$','3: Correct answer; 2: Solves $F''''(x)=0$ for $x$, or equivalent merit; 1: Finds the pdf $F''(x)$, or equivalent merit',1372,NULL,'parse-v1',NULL,NULL,'[[3, "Correct answer"], [2, "Solves $F''''(x)=0$ for $x$, or equivalent merit"], [1, "Finds the pdf $F''(x)$, or equivalent merit"]]','{}');
$$\begin{aligned} n(n+1)(n+2)(n+3)&=n(n+3)\times(n+1)(n+2)\\ &=\left(n^2+3n\right)\left(n^2+3n+2\right) \end{aligned}$$
$$\therefore \mu=3$$','1: Correct answer',1372,NULL,'parse-v1',NULL,NULL,'[[1, "Correct answer"]]','{}');
$$2022\times 2023\times 2024\times 2025+1=\left(n^2+3n\right)\left(n^2+3n+2\right)+1$$
Let $k=n^2+3n$ (an integer):
$$\begin{aligned} 2022\times 2023\times 2024\times 2025+1&=k(k+2)+1\\ &=k^2+2k+1\\ &=(k+1)^2 \end{aligned}$$
where $k+1$ is an integer.

$\therefore 2022\times 2023\times 2024\times 2025+1$ is a perfect square.

Alternate method: let $n=2022$ and $k=n^2+3n+1$. Then
$$\begin{aligned} 2022\times 2023\times 2024\times 2025+1&=(k-1)(k+1)+1\\ &=k^2\\ &=\left(n^2+3n+1\right)^2 \end{aligned}$$','2: Correct deduction; 1: Writes the expression as $\left(n^2+3n\right)\left(n^2+3n+2\right)+1$ with $n=2022$, or equivalent merit',1372,NULL,'parse-v1',NULL,NULL,'[[2, "Correct answer"], [1, "Writes the expression as $\\left(n^2+3n\\right)\\left(n^2+3n+2\\right)+1$ with $n=2022$, or equivalent merit"]]','{"criteria_original": [[2, "Correct deduction"], [1, "Writes the expression as $\\left(n^2+3n\\right)\\left(n^2+3n+2\\right)+1$ with $n=2022$, or equivalent merit"]], "top_criterion_original": "Correct deduction"}');

