$P(1,-2)$ and $Q(7,1)$ lie on the circle:
$$\begin{aligned}(1-a)^2+(-2-b)^2&=r^2 &&\ldots(1)\\(7-a)^2+(1-b)^2&=r^2 &&\ldots(2)\end{aligned}$$
Expanding (1):
$$\begin{aligned}1-2a+a^2+4+4b+b^2&=r^2\\a^2-2a+b^2+4b+5&=r^2\end{aligned}$$
Expanding (2):
$$\begin{aligned}49-14a+a^2+1-2b+b^2&=r^2\\a^2-14a+b^2-2b+50&=r^2\end{aligned}$$
$(1)-(2)$:
$$\begin{aligned}12a+6b-45&=0\\4a+2b&=15 &&(\div 3)\end{aligned}$$
$\therefore$ the centre $(a,b)$ satisfies $4x+2y=15$, so it lies on the line.','1: forming circle equations; 1: expanding and simplifying; 1: showing equation 4a+2b=15. Substituting the midpoint (4, -1/2) of PQ into 4x+2y=15 does not prove the midpoint is the centre and was awarded 2 marks.',1373,NULL,'parse-v1',NULL,NULL,'[[3, "Correct answer"], [2, "Expands and simplifies, or equivalent merit"], [1, "Forms the circle equations, or equivalent merit"]]','{"criteria_original": [[1, "forming circle equations"], [1, "expanding and simplifying"], [1, "showing equation 4a+2b=15. Substituting the midpoint (4, -1/2) of PQ into 4x+2y=15 does not prove the midpoint is the centre and was awarded 2 marks."]], "top_criterion_original": "showing equation 4a+2b=15. Substituting the midpoint (4, -1/2) of PQ into 4x+2y=15 does not prove the midpoint is the centre and was awarded 2 marks.", "marker_note": "Substituting the midpoint $\\left(4,-\\dfrac{1}{2}\\right)$ of $PQ$ into $4x+2y=15$ does not prove the midpoint is the centre and was awarded 2 marks."}');

