$$\begin{aligned}3(1-\cos^2x)+1&=4\cos x\\0&=3\cos^2x+4\cos x-4\\0&=(3\cos x-2)(\cos x+2)\end{aligned}$$
$\cos x=\dfrac{2}{3}$ or $\cos x=-2$.

$-1\le\cos x\le 1$, so $\cos x\ne -2$ and $\cos x=\dfrac{2}{3}$.

In $[-\pi,\pi]$, $\cos x$ is positive in the first and fourth quadrants:
$$\therefore x\approx\pm 0.841$$','1: using $\sin^2x=1-\cos^2x$; 1: $\cos x=\frac{2}{3}$; 1: $x=\pm 0.841$',1373,NULL,'parse-v1',NULL,NULL,'[[3, "Correct answer"], [2, "Finds $\\cos x=\\dfrac{2}{3}$, or equivalent merit"], [1, "Uses $\\sin^2x=1-\\cos^2x$, or equivalent merit"]]','{"criteria_original": [[1, "using $\\sin^2x=1-\\cos^2x$"], [1, "$\\cos x=\\dfrac{2}{3}$"], [1, "$x=\\pm 0.841$"]], "top_criterion_original": "$x=\\pm 0.841$", "source_original": "$$\\begin{aligned}3(1-\\cos^2x)+1&=4\\cos x\\\\0&=3\\cos^2x+4\\cos x-3\\end{aligned}$$", "corrected": "Corrected from source: the quadratic read $0=3\\cos^2x+4\\cos x-3$; it is $3\\cos^2x+4\\cos x-4=0$, which matches the factorisation $(3\\cos x-2)(\\cos x+2)$. Answer unchanged."}');

