$$\begin{aligned}\text{Area}&=\int_{-\ln 6}^{\ln 2}\left(-e^{2x}+4-e^x+5\right)dx\\&=\int_{-\ln 6}^{\ln 2}\left(-e^{2x}-e^x+9\right)dx\\&=\left[\dfrac{-e^{2x}}{2}-e^x+9x\right]_{-\ln 6}^{\ln 2}\\&=\dfrac{-e^{2\ln 2}}{2}-e^{\ln 2}+9\ln 2-\left(\dfrac{-e^{-2\ln 6}}{2}-e^{-\ln 6}-9\ln 6\right)\\&=\dfrac{-4}{2}-2+9\ln 2-\left(\dfrac{-6^{-2}}{2}-\dfrac{1}{6}-9\ln 6\right)\\&=9\ln 2+9\ln 6-\dfrac{275}{72}\\&=9\ln 12-\dfrac{275}{72}\end{aligned}$$','3: Provides the correct answer, showing all necessary working; 2: Correctly evaluates to get the constant term or the ln term; 1: Provides correct expression for area and correctly integrates it before substituting the boundaries. Comments: easiest to use the area between two curves formula; many used two areas with the x-axis and did not use absolute values; many careless errors.',1373,NULL,'parse-v1',NULL,NULL,'[[3, "Correct answer"], [2, "Correctly evaluates to get the constant term or the $\\ln$ term, or equivalent merit"], [1, "Provides a correct expression for the area and correctly integrates it before substituting the boundaries, or equivalent merit"]]','{"criteria_original": [[3, "Provides the correct answer, showing all necessary working"], [2, "Correctly evaluates to get the constant term or the ln term"], [1, "Provides correct expression for area and correctly integrates it before substituting the boundaries. Comments: easiest to use the area between two curves formula; many used two areas with the x-axis and did not use absolute values; many careless errors."]], "top_criterion_original": "Provides the correct answer, showing all necessary working", "marker_note": "Easiest to use the area between two curves formula; many used two areas with the $x$-axis and did not use absolute values; many careless errors."}');
$$\begin{aligned}s(t)&=\int 6\cos\left(2t+\dfrac{\pi}{3}\right)dt\\&=3\sin\left(2t+\dfrac{\pi}{3}\right)+C\end{aligned}$$
When $t=0$, $s(0)=2$:
$$\begin{aligned}2&=3\sin\left(\dfrac{\pi}{3}\right)+C\\C&=2-3\sin\left(\dfrac{\pi}{3}\right)\\&=2-\dfrac{3\sqrt{3}}{2}\end{aligned}$$
$$\therefore s(t)=3\sin\left(2t+\dfrac{\pi}{3}\right)+2-\dfrac{3\sqrt{3}}{2}$$','2: Provides the correct answer using the initial condition; 1: Correctly finds $3\sin\left(2t+\frac{\pi}{3}\right)+C$. Comment: answered well.',1373,NULL,'parse-v1',NULL,NULL,'[[2, "Correct answer"], [1, "Correctly finds $3\\sin\\left(2t+\\dfrac{\\pi}{3}\\right)+C$, or equivalent merit"]]','{"criteria_original": [[2, "Provides the correct answer using the initial condition"], [1, "Correctly finds $3\\sin\\left(2t+\\dfrac{\\pi}{3}\\right)+C$. Comment: answered well."]], "top_criterion_original": "Provides the correct answer using the initial condition", "marker_note": "Answered well.", "source_original": "$s(t)=\\displaystyle\\int 6\\cos\\left(2t+\\dfrac{\\pi}{3}\\right)dx$", "corrected": "Corrected from source: the integral was written with $dx$; the variable is $t$, so $dt$."}');
$$\begin{aligned}a(t)&=\dfrac{dv}{dt}\\&=-12\sin\left(2t+\dfrac{\pi}{3}\right)\end{aligned}$$','1: Provides correct answer. Comment: answered well.',1373,NULL,'parse-v1',NULL,NULL,'[[1, "Correct answer"]]','{"criteria_original": [[1, "Provides correct answer. Comment: answered well."]], "top_criterion_original": "Provides correct answer. Comment: answered well.", "marker_note": "Answered well."}');
$$\begin{aligned}6\cos\left(2t+\dfrac{\pi}{3}\right)&=0\\\cos\left(2t+\dfrac{\pi}{3}\right)&=0\\2t+\dfrac{\pi}{3}&=\dfrac{\pi}{2}\\2t&=\dfrac{\pi}{6}\\t&=\dfrac{\pi}{12}\text{ seconds}\end{aligned}$$
