$$\begin{aligned} u&=4x-1 & v&=e^{-\frac{x}{2}}\\ u''&=4 & v''&=-\dfrac{1}{2}e^{-\frac{x}{2}}\end{aligned}$$
$$\begin{aligned}y''&=4e^{-\frac{x}{2}}-\dfrac{1}{2}e^{-\frac{x}{2}}(4x-1)\\&=e^{-\frac{x}{2}}\left(4-\dfrac{1}{2}(4x-1)\right)\\&=e^{-\frac{x}{2}}\left(4-2x+\dfrac{1}{2}\right)\\&=e^{-\frac{x}{2}}\left(\dfrac{9}{2}-2x\right)\end{aligned}$$
Stationary points occur where $y''=0$. $e^{-\frac{x}{2}}\ne 0$, so
$$\begin{aligned}\dfrac{9}{2}-2x&=0\\x&=\dfrac{9}{4}\end{aligned}$$
When $x=\dfrac{9}{4}$, $y=8e^{-\frac{9}{8}}$, so the stationary point is $\left(\dfrac{9}{4},8e^{-\frac{9}{8}}\right)$.

Testing the sign of $y''$ either side ($e^{-\frac{x}{2}}>0$, so the sign is that of $\dfrac{9}{2}-2x$):

\extable{Ts1575_1}

$\therefore$ maximum turning point at $\left(\dfrac{9}{4},8e^{-\frac{9}{8}}\right)$.','3: correct stationary point and nature; 1 mark was deducted if $e^{-\frac{x}{2}}\ne 0$ was not written',1373,NULL,'parse-v1',NULL,NULL,'[[3, "Correct answer"]]','{"criteria_original": [[3, "correct stationary point and nature; 1 mark was deducted if $e^{-\\frac{x}{2}}\\ne 0$ was not written"]], "top_criterion_original": "correct stationary point and nature; 1 mark was deducted if $e^{-\\frac{x}{2}}\\ne 0$ was not written", "marker_note": "1 mark was deducted if $e^{-\\frac{x}{2}}\\ne 0$ was not written."}');
$$F_1=500000(1.07)-2000$$
$$\begin{aligned}F_2&=\big(500000(1.07)-2000\big)1.07-2000(1.09)\\&=500000(1.07)^2-2000(1.07)-2000(1.09)\end{aligned}$$
$$\begin{aligned}F_3&=\big(500000(1.07)^2-2000(1.07)-2000(1.09)\big)1.07-2000(1.09)^2\\&=500000(1.07)^3-2000(1.07)^2-2000(1.09)(1.07)-2000(1.09)^2\\&=500000(1.07)^3-2000\left(1.07^2+(1.09)(1.07)+1.09^2\right)\end{aligned}$$','2: all steps shown ($F_1$, $F_2$, $F_3$). Just putting $F_1$ or $F_2$ was not accepted.',1373,NULL,'parse-v1',NULL,NULL,'[[2, "Correct answer"]]','{"criteria_original": [[2, "all steps shown ($F_1$, $F_2$, $F_3$). Just putting $F_1$ or $F_2$ was not accepted."]], "top_criterion_original": "all steps shown ($F_1$, $F_2$, $F_3$). Just putting $F_1$ or $F_2$ was not accepted.", "marker_note": "All steps ($F_1$, $F_2$, $F_3$) must be shown; just putting $F_1$ or $F_2$ was not accepted."}');
$$F_n=500000(1.07)^n-2000\left(1.07^{n-1}+1.07^{n-2}(1.09)+1.07^{n-3}(1.09)^2+\cdots+1.09^{n-1}\right)$$
The bracket is a geometric series with first term $1.07^{n-1}$, ratio $\dfrac{1.09}{1.07}$ and $n$ terms. There are no fish left when $F_n=0$:
$$\begin{aligned}500000(1.07)^n&=2000\left(\dfrac{1.07^{n-1}\left(\left(\frac{1.09}{1.07}\right)^n-1\right)}{\frac{1.09}{1.07}-1}\right)\\500000(1.07)^n&=2000\left(\dfrac{\frac{1.07^n}{1.07}\left(\left(\frac{1.09}{1.07}\right)^n-1\right)}{\frac{1.09-1.07}{1.07}}\right)\\500(1.07)^n&=100\left(1.07^n\left(\left(\dfrac{1.09}{1.07}\right)^n-1\right)\right)\\5(1.07)^n&=1.07^n\left(\dfrac{1.09^n}{1.07^n}\right)-1.07^n\\6(1.07)^n&=1.09^n\\6&=\left(\dfrac{1.09}{1.07}\right)^n\\\ln 6&=n\ln\left(\dfrac{1.09}{1.07}\right)\\n&=\dfrac{\ln 6}{\ln\left(\frac{1.09}{1.07}\right)}\\&=96.75\end{aligned}$$
The fish run out during the 97th year, and $n=1$ is 2025.

$\therefore$ Year 2121','1: writing the expression for $F_n$; full marks (3) for the correct answer',1373,NULL,'parse-v1',NULL,NULL,'[[3, "Correct answer"], [1, "Writes the expression for $F_n$, or equivalent merit"]]','{"criteria_original": [[1, "writing the expression for $F_n$; full marks (3) for the correct answer"]], "top_criterion_original": "writing the expression for $F_n$; full marks (3) for the correct answer"}');

In each right-angled triangle at the sides (hypotenuse $7$), using the sine and cosine ratios:
$$\begin{aligned}\sin\theta&=\dfrac{h}{7} & h&=7\sin\theta\\\cos\theta&=\dfrac{x}{7} & x&=7\cos\theta\end{aligned}$$
The cross-section is a trapezium with parallel sides $7$ and $7+2x$:
$$\begin{aligned}A&=\dfrac{1}{2}h(a+b)\\&=\dfrac{1}{2}(7\sin\theta)(7+7+2x)\\&=\dfrac{1}{2}(7\sin\theta)\big(14+2(7\cos\theta)\big)\\&=\dfrac{1}{2}(7\sin\theta)(14+14\cos\theta)\\&=\dfrac{1}{2}(7\sin\theta)\,14(1+\cos\theta)\\&=49\sin\theta(1+\cos\theta)\\&=49(\sin\theta+\sin\theta\cos\theta)\end{aligned}$$','2: all steps shown properly',1373,NULL,'parse-v1',NULL,NULL,'[[2, "Correct answer"]]','{"criteria_original": [[2, "all steps shown properly"]], "top_criterion_original": "all steps shown properly", "marker_note": "All steps must be shown properly."}');
$$\begin{aligned} u&=\sin\theta & v&=\cos\theta\\ u''&=\cos\theta & v''&=-\sin\theta\end{aligned}$$
$$\begin{aligned}A&=49(\sin\theta+\sin\theta\cos\theta)\\A''&=49(\cos\theta+\cos\theta\cos\theta-\sin\theta\sin\theta)\\&=49(\cos\theta+\cos^2\theta-\sin^2\theta)\\A''''&=49(-\sin\theta-2\cos\theta\sin\theta-2\sin\theta\cos\theta)\\&=49(-\sin\theta-4\sin\theta\cos\theta)\end{aligned}$$
For a maximum or minimum, $A''=0$:
$$\begin{aligned}\cos\theta+\cos^2\theta-\sin^2\theta&=0\\\cos\theta+\cos^2\theta-(1-\cos^2\theta)&=0\\2\cos^2\theta+\cos\theta-1&=0\\2\cos^2\theta+2\cos\theta-\cos\theta-1&=0\\2\cos\theta(\cos\theta+1)-(\cos\theta+1)&=0\\(2\cos\theta-1)(\cos\theta+1)&=0\end{aligned}$$
$\cos\theta=\dfrac{1}{2}$ or $\cos\theta=-1$.

$\theta$ must be acute, so $\theta\ne\pi$ and $\theta=\dfrac{\pi}{3}$.

Testing with the second derivative:
$$\begin{aligned}A''''\left(\dfrac{\pi}{3}\right)&=49\left(-\sin\dfrac{\pi}{3}-4\sin\dfrac{\pi}{3}\cos\dfrac{\pi}{3}\right)\\&=49\left(-\dfrac{\sqrt{3}}{2}-4\left(\dfrac{\sqrt{3}}{2}\right)\left(\dfrac{1}{2}\right)\right)\\&=49\left(-\dfrac{\sqrt{3}}{2}-\dfrac{2\sqrt{3}}{2}\right)\\&=-\dfrac{147\sqrt{3}}{2}\\&<0\end{aligned}$$
$\therefore$ the cross-sectional area is a maximum when $\theta=\dfrac{\pi}{3}$.','3: correct angle with nature tested. Lost 1 mark if not tested by $A''''$ or table. Radian measure must be used in calculus.',1373,NULL,'parse-v1',NULL,NULL,'[[3, "Correct answer"]]','{"criteria_original": [[3, "correct angle with nature tested. Lost 1 mark if not tested by $A''''$ or table. Radian measure must be used in calculus."]], "top_criterion_original": "correct angle with nature tested. Lost 1 mark if not tested by $A''''$ or table. Radian measure must be used in calculus.", "marker_note": "Lost 1 mark if the nature was not tested by $A''''$ or a table. Radian measure must be used in calculus."}');

