From the diagram, the base and each sloping side are $2$ m, so the height is $2\sin\theta$ and each side adds $2\cos\theta$ to the top width.

Using the area of a trapezium, $A=\dfrac{1}{2}h(a+b)$ (or a rectangle plus two triangles):
$$\begin{aligned}A&=\dfrac{1}{2}(2\sin\theta)(2+2+4\cos\theta)\\&=\sin\theta(4+4\cos\theta)\end{aligned}$$','1: if on diagram ($2\sin\theta$, $2\cos\theta$); 1: area must be explicitly shown',1375,NULL,'parse-v1',NULL,NULL,'[[2, "Correct answer"], [1, "Finds the height $2\\sin\\theta$ and the width $2\\cos\\theta$ (may be shown on the diagram), or equivalent merit"]]','{"criteria_original": [[1, "if on diagram ($2\\sin\\theta$, $2\\cos\\theta$)"], [1, "area must be explicitly shown"]], "top_criterion_original": "area must be explicitly shown", "marker_note": "The area must be explicitly shown."}');
$$A=4\sin\theta+4\sin\theta\cos\theta$$
Using the product rule, $\dfrac{d}{d\theta}(uv)=u''v+uv''$, on $4\sin\theta\cos\theta$, with
$$\begin{aligned}u&=4\sin\theta & v&=\cos\theta\\u''&=4\cos\theta & v''&=-\sin\theta\end{aligned}$$
$$\begin{aligned}\dfrac{dA}{d\theta}&=4\cos\theta+4\sin\theta(-\sin\theta)+4\cos\theta\cos\theta\\&=4\cos\theta-4(1-\cos^2\theta)+4\cos^2\theta\\&=8\cos^2\theta+4\cos\theta-4\end{aligned}$$
For a maximum or minimum, $\dfrac{dA}{d\theta}=0$:
$$\begin{aligned}4(2\cos^2\theta+\cos\theta-1)&=0\\(2\cos\theta-1)(\cos\theta+1)&=0\end{aligned}$$
So $\cos\theta=\dfrac{1}{2}$ or $\cos\theta=-1$.

$\theta$ is an angle in a right-angled triangle, so $0<\theta<\dfrac{\pi}{2}$ and $\cos\theta=\dfrac{1}{2}$ only:
$$\theta=\dfrac{\pi}{3}$$
Testing with the second derivative:
$$\dfrac{d^2A}{d\theta^2}=16\cos\theta(-\sin\theta)-4\sin\theta$$
At $\theta=\dfrac{\pi}{3}$:
$$\begin{aligned}\dfrac{d^2A}{d\theta^2}&=16\cos\dfrac{\pi}{3}\times\left(-\sin\dfrac{\pi}{3}\right)-4\sin\dfrac{\pi}{3}\\&=16\times\dfrac{1}{2}\times\left(-\dfrac{\sqrt{3}}{2}\right)-4\times\dfrac{\sqrt{3}}{2}\\&=-4\sqrt{3}-2\sqrt{3}\\&=-6\sqrt{3}<0\end{aligned}$$
So the area is a maximum when $\theta=\dfrac{\pi}{3}$.

$\therefore$ the angle that maximises the area is $\dfrac{\pi}{3}$ (or $60^\circ$).','3: correct angle with test (no breakdown given)',1375,NULL,'parse-v1',NULL,NULL,'[[3, "Correct answer"]]','{"criteria_original": [[3, "correct angle with test (no breakdown given)"]], "top_criterion_original": "correct angle with test (no breakdown given)", "marker_note": "Full marks need the correct angle with a test of its nature. As it is a trig function, $\\theta$ is in radians; this should be reflected in the students'' solutions.", "source_original": "$=16\\times\\dfrac{1}{2}\\times-\\dfrac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}-4\\times-\\dfrac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}$ / $=-4\\sqrt{3}+2\\sqrt{3}$ / $=-2\\sqrt{3}<0$", "corrected": "Corrected from source: $4\\sin\\dfrac{\\pi}{3}$ was substituted as $4\\times\\left(-\\dfrac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\right)$, giving $-4\\sqrt{3}+2\\sqrt{3}=-2\\sqrt{3}$; it is $-4\\sqrt{3}-2\\sqrt{3}=-6\\sqrt{3}$. Conclusion (maximum) unchanged."}');
