$$\begin{aligned}40&=2\pi r+2\pi r+4x\\ 40&=4\pi r+4x\\ 10&=\pi r+x\\ x&=10-\pi r\end{aligned}$$
The total area is two circles ($\pi r^2$ each) and the square ($x^2$):
$$\begin{aligned}\therefore\ A&=2\pi r^2+(10-\pi r)^2\\ &=(10-\pi r)^2+2\pi r^2\text{ as required}\end{aligned}$$','1: $40=2\pi r+2\pi r+4x$; 1: shows the area formula',166,NULL,'parse-v1',NULL,NULL,'[[2, "Correct answer"], [1, "Writes $40=2\\pi r+2\\pi r+4x$, or equivalent merit"]]','{"criteria_original": [[1, "$40=2\\pi r+2\\pi r+4x$"], [1, "shows the area formula"]], "top_criterion_original": "shows the area formula"}');
$$\begin{aligned}A&=(10-\pi r)^2+2\pi r^2\\ \dfrac{dA}{dr}&=2(10-\pi r)\times(-\pi)+4\pi r\\ &=-20\pi+2\pi^2r+4\pi r\end{aligned}$$
Stationary points when $\dfrac{dA}{dr}=0$:
$$\begin{aligned}0&=-20\pi+2\pi^2r+4\pi r\\ 20\pi&=2\pi^2r+4\pi r\\ 10&=\pi r+2r\\ 10&=r(\pi+2)\\ r&=\dfrac{10}{\pi+2}\end{aligned}$$
Checking the nature using $\dfrac{d^2A}{dr^2}$:
$$\dfrac{d^2A}{dr^2}=2\pi^2+4\pi>0\text{ for all }r$$
$\therefore\ A$ is always concave up, so there is a minimum at $r=\dfrac{10}{\pi+2}$.

At $r=\dfrac{10}{\pi+2}$:
$$\begin{aligned}x&=10-\pi\left(\dfrac{10}{\pi+2}\right)\\ &=\dfrac{10(\pi+2)-10\pi}{\pi+2}\\ &=\dfrac{20}{\pi+2}\end{aligned}$$
$\therefore$ the area is minimised when $r=\dfrac{10}{\pi+2}$ cm and the side of the square is $\dfrac{20}{\pi+2}$ cm.','1: $\dfrac{dA}{dr}$; 1: $r=\dfrac{10}{\pi+2}$; 1: verifies minimum; 1: side length $\dfrac{20}{\pi+2}$',166,NULL,'parse-v1',NULL,NULL,'[[4, "Correct answer"], [3, "Finds $r=\\dfrac{10}{\\pi+2}$ and verifies the minimum, or equivalent merit"], [2, "Finds $r=\\dfrac{10}{\\pi+2}$, or equivalent merit"], [1, "Finds $\\dfrac{dA}{dr}$, or equivalent merit"]]','{"criteria_original": [[1, "$\\dfrac{dA}{dr}$"], [1, "$r=\\dfrac{10}{\\pi+2}$"], [1, "verifies minimum"], [1, "side length $\\dfrac{20}{\\pi+2}$"]], "top_criterion_original": "side length $\\dfrac{20}{\\pi+2}$"}');

Standardising, $z=\dfrac{x-\mu}{\sigma}$:
