For $k>0$, $2^{-kt}\to0$ as $t\to\infty$, hence $C\to100$ as $t\to\infty$.

Describing the rate of change using the first and second derivatives:
$$\begin{aligned} C''&=100k\ln(2)\times2^{-kt}\\ C''''&=-100k^2(\ln2)^2\times2^{-kt} \end{aligned}$$
Given $k>0$, for all $t>0$:
$$\begin{aligned} C''&>0 &&\text{capacity increasing}\\ C''''&<0 &&\text{rate of increase decreasing} \end{aligned}$$
Hence the battery charge capacity increases from 0 at a decreasing rate over time, but can never (theoretically) reach $100\%$ charge.','2: Provides correct solution; 1: Provides a brief description of the behaviour of the function by either considering the rate of change over time OR the behaviour of $C$, or equivalent merit',641,NULL,'parse-v1',NULL,NULL,'[[2, "Correct answer"], [1, "Provides a brief description of the behaviour of the function by either considering the rate of change over time OR the behaviour of $C$, or equivalent merit"]]','{"criteria_original": [[2, "Provides correct solution"], [1, "Provides a brief description of the behaviour of the function by either considering the rate of change over time OR the behaviour of $C$, or equivalent merit"]], "top_criterion_original": "Provides correct solution"}');
