$$\begin{aligned} \text{Area}&=\int_0^{\frac{\pi}{6}}3\tan x\,dx+\int_{\frac{\pi}{6}}^{\frac{\pi}{2}}\cot x\,dx\\ &=3\int_0^{\frac{\pi}{6}}\dfrac{\sin x}{\cos x}\,dx+\int_{\frac{\pi}{6}}^{\frac{\pi}{2}}\dfrac{\cos x}{\sin x}\,dx\\ &=-3\int_0^{\frac{\pi}{6}}\dfrac{-\sin x}{\cos x}\,dx+\int_{\frac{\pi}{6}}^{\frac{\pi}{2}}\dfrac{\cos x}{\sin x}\,dx\\ &=-3\Big[\ln|\cos x|\Big]_0^{\frac{\pi}{6}}+\Big[\ln|\sin x|\Big]_{\frac{\pi}{6}}^{\frac{\pi}{2}}\\ &=-3\left[\ln\left(\cos\dfrac{\pi}{6}\right)-\ln(\cos 0)\right]+\left[\ln\left(\sin\dfrac{\pi}{2}\right)-\ln\left(\sin\dfrac{\pi}{6}\right)\right]\\ &=-3\left[\ln\left(\dfrac{\sqrt{3}}{2}\right)-\ln(1)\right]+\left[\ln(1)-\ln\left(\dfrac{1}{2}\right)\right]\\ &=-3\ln\left(\dfrac{\sqrt{3}}{2}\right)-\ln\left(\dfrac{1}{2}\right)\\ &=\ln\left(\dfrac{8}{3\sqrt{3}}\right)+\ln 2\\ &=\ln\left(\dfrac{16}{3\sqrt{3}}\right) \end{aligned}$$','3: Provides correct solution; 2: Provides an antiderivative, or equivalent merit; 1: Provides an integral expression for the area, or equivalent merit',335,NULL,'parse-v1',NULL,NULL,'[[3, "Correct answer"], [2, "Provides an antiderivative, or equivalent merit"], [1, "Provides an integral expression for the area, or equivalent merit"]]','{"criteria_original": [[3, "Provides correct solution"], [2, "Provides an antiderivative, or equivalent merit"], [1, "Provides an integral expression for the area, or equivalent merit"]], "top_criterion_original": "Provides correct solution"}');

Using the empirical rule:

$16\%$ lies below 150 g, so 150 g is one standard deviation below the mean.

$2.5\%$ lies above 300 g, so 300 g is two standard deviations above the mean.

So 150 g to 300 g spans 3 standard deviations:
$$\begin{aligned} \text{Standard deviation}&=(300-150)\div 3\\ &=50\text{ g} \end{aligned}$$
$$\begin{aligned} \text{Mean}&=150+50\\ &=200\text{ g} \end{aligned}$$','2: Provides correct solution; 1: Makes some progress towards finding a correct solution',335,NULL,'parse-v1',NULL,NULL,'[[2, "Correct answer"], [1, "Makes some progress towards finding a correct solution, or equivalent merit"]]','{"criteria_original": [[2, "Provides correct solution"], [1, "Makes some progress towards finding a correct solution"]], "top_criterion_original": "Provides correct solution"}');
$$\begin{aligned} \int_0^3 k\big(\sin(\pi x)+2x\big)\,dx&=k\left[-\dfrac{1}{\pi}\cos(\pi x)+x^2\right]_0^3\\ 1&=k\left[-\dfrac{1}{\pi}\cos(3\pi)+3^2-\left(-\dfrac{1}{\pi}\cos(0)+0^2\right)\right]\\ 1&=k\left[-\dfrac{1}{\pi}(-1)+9+\dfrac{1}{\pi}(1)-0\right]\\ 1&=k\left(\dfrac{2}{\pi}+9\right)\\ 1&=k\left(\dfrac{2+9\pi}{\pi}\right)\\ k&=\dfrac{\pi}{2+9\pi} \end{aligned}$$','3: Provides correct solution; 2: Provides a correct equation involving a definite integral equal to 1 AND finds the antiderivative, or equivalent merit; 1: Provides a correct equation involving a definite integral equal to 1 OR finds an antiderivative, or equivalent merit',335,NULL,'parse-v1',NULL,NULL,'[[3, "Correct answer"], [2, "Provides a correct equation involving a definite integral equal to 1 AND finds the antiderivative, or equivalent merit"], [1, "Provides a correct equation involving a definite integral equal to 1 OR finds an antiderivative, or equivalent merit"]]','{"criteria_original": [[3, "Provides correct solution"], [2, "Provides a correct equation involving a definite integral equal to 1 AND finds the antiderivative, or equivalent merit"], [1, "Provides a correct equation involving a definite integral equal to 1 OR finds an antiderivative, or equivalent merit"]], "top_criterion_original": "Provides correct solution"}');

The mode occurs at the maximum value on the graph.

The median occurs at the point which divides the area under the curve in half.

From the graph, the area to the left of the mode is clearly greater than half the total area.

Thus the median must be to the left of the mode, i.e. $\text{mode}>\text{median}$.','1: Provides a correct reason',335,NULL,'parse-v1',NULL,NULL,'[[1, "Correct answer"]]','{"criteria_original": [[1, "Provides a correct reason"]], "top_criterion_original": "Provides a correct reason"}');
$$\begin{aligned} f(x)&=\dfrac{\pi}{2+9\pi}\big(\sin(\pi x)+2x\big)\\ f''(x)&=\dfrac{\pi}{2+9\pi}\big(\pi\cos(\pi x)+2\big)\\ 0&=\pi\cos(\pi x)+2\\ \cos(\pi x)&=-\dfrac{2}{\pi}\\ \pi x&=\cos^{-1}\left(-\dfrac{2}{\pi}\right) \end{aligned}$$
