$$\begin{aligned} u &= x & v &= \cos x \\ u'' &= 1 & v'' &= -\sin x \end{aligned}$$

$$\dfrac{d}{dx}(x\cos x)=\cos x-x\sin x$$

Integrate both sides:

$$\begin{aligned} \int(\cos x-x\sin x)\,dx &= x\cos x \\ \int\cos x\,dx-\int x\sin x\,dx &= x\cos x \\ \int x\sin x\,dx &= \int\cos x\,dx-x\cos x \\ &= \sin x-x\cos x+C \end{aligned}$$','1: correct derivative; 1: correct use to obtain the integral (ticks in source)',339,NULL,'parse-v1',NULL,NULL,'[[2, "Correct answer"], [1, "Correct derivative, or equivalent merit"]]','{"criteria_original": [[1, "correct derivative"], [1, "correct use to obtain the integral (ticks in source)"]], "top_criterion_original": "correct derivative"}');

$$\begin{aligned} \sin x &= x\sin x \\ \sin x(1-x) &= 0 \end{aligned}$$

So $\sin x=0$ or $x=1$.

In $0\le x\le\pi$, the curves meet at $x=0,\ 1,\ \pi$.

The intersection at $x=1$ lies between $0$ and $\pi$, so split the area there.

Using part (a), $\displaystyle\int x\sin x\,dx=\sin x-x\cos x+C$:

$$\begin{aligned} \text{Area} &= \int_0^1(\sin x-x\sin x)\,dx+\int_1^{\pi}(x\sin x-\sin x)\,dx \\ &= \Big[-\cos x-(\sin x-x\cos x)\Big]_0^1+\Big[(\sin x-x\cos x)+\cos x\Big]_1^{\pi} \\ &= (-\cos 1-\sin 1+\cos 1)-(-\cos 0-\sin 0+0)+\Big[(\sin\pi-\pi\cos\pi+\cos\pi)-(\sin 1-\cos 1+\cos 1)\Big] \\ &= (1-\sin 1)+(\pi-1-\sin 1) \\ &= \pi-2\sin 1\text{ units}^2 \end{aligned}$$','4 marks (ticks in source: intersection points; correct integrals set up; correct primitives; correct exact answer)',339,NULL,'parse-v1',NULL,NULL,'[[4, "Correct answer"], [3, "Finds correct primitives, or equivalent merit"], [2, "Sets up the correct integrals, or equivalent merit"], [1, "Finds the intersection points, or equivalent merit"]]','{"criteria_original": [[null, "4 marks (ticks in source: intersection points; correct integrals set up; correct primitives; correct exact answer)"]], "top_criterion_original": "4 marks (ticks in source: intersection points; correct integrals set up; correct primitives; correct exact answer)"}');

