$$\begin{aligned} \text{LHS}&=\dfrac{\tan^2(2\theta)+1}{\tan^2(2\theta)} \\ &=1+\dfrac{1}{\tan^2(2\theta)} \\ &=1+\cot^2(2\theta) \\ &=\operatorname{cosec}^2(2\theta) \\ &=\text{RHS} \end{aligned}$$
Alternatively, writing $\sec$ in terms of $\cos$:
$$\begin{aligned} \text{LHS}&=\dfrac{\frac{1}{\cos^2(2\theta)}}{\frac{1}{\cos^2(2\theta)}-1} \\ &=\dfrac{\frac{1}{\cos^2(2\theta)}}{\frac{1-\cos^2(2\theta)}{\cos^2(2\theta)}} \\ &=\dfrac{1}{1-\cos^2(2\theta)} \\ &=\dfrac{1}{\sin^2(2\theta)} \\ &=\operatorname{cosec}^2(2\theta) \\ &=\text{RHS} \end{aligned}$$','2: provide correct solutions; 1: use trigonometric identities to simplify LHS to $1+\dfrac{1}{\tan^2 2\theta}$ or $\dfrac{1}{1-\cos^2 2\theta}$',361,NULL,'parse-v1',NULL,NULL,'[[2, "Correct answer"], [1, "Uses trigonometric identities to simplify the LHS to $1+\\dfrac{1}{\\tan^2 2\\theta}$ or $\\dfrac{1}{1-\\cos^2 2\\theta}$, or equivalent merit"]]','{"criteria_original": [[2, "provide correct solutions"], [1, "use trigonometric identities to simplify LHS to $1+\\dfrac{1}{\\tan^2 2\\theta}$ or $\\dfrac{1}{1-\\cos^2 2\\theta}$"]], "top_criterion_original": "provide correct solutions"}');
$$\begin{aligned} 0&\le\theta\le\pi \\ 0&\le2\theta\le2\pi &&(\text{multiply by } 2) \end{aligned}$$
$$\begin{aligned} \operatorname{cosec}^2(2\theta)&=2 \\ \operatorname{cosec}(2\theta)&=\pm\sqrt{2} \\ \sin(2\theta)&=\pm\dfrac{1}{\sqrt{2}} \end{aligned}$$
