Ms Kim wins on her first, second or third turn. Write $W$ for her winning draw and $L$ for a draw that does not end the game:
$$\begin{aligned} P&=P(W)+P(LLW)+P(LLLLW) \\ &=\dfrac{4}{7}+\dfrac{3}{7}\times\dfrac{4}{7}\times\dfrac{4}{7}+\dfrac{3}{7}\times\dfrac{4}{7}\times\dfrac{3}{7}\times\dfrac{4}{7}\times\dfrac{4}{7} \\ &\approx0.75 \end{aligned}$$','2: correct answer; 1: correct expression for the three winning sequences',373,NULL,'parse-v1',NULL,NULL,'[[2, "Correct answer"], [1, "Correct expression for the three winning sequences, or equivalent merit"]]','{"criteria_original": [[2, "correct answer"], [1, "correct expression for the three winning sequences"]]}');

Ms Kim wins by drawing red ($\dfrac{4}{7}$); each earlier round has her draw black ($\dfrac{3}{7}$) and Ms Lee draw red ($\dfrac{4}{7}$):
$$\begin{aligned} P(1 \text{ turn})&=\dfrac{4}{7} \\ P(2 \text{ turns or less})&=\dfrac{4}{7}+\dfrac{3}{7}\times\dfrac{4}{7}\times\dfrac{4}{7}=\dfrac{4}{7}+\dfrac{3}{7}\left(\dfrac{4}{7}\right)^2 \\ P(3 \text{ turns or less})&=\dfrac{4}{7}+\dfrac{3}{7}\left(\dfrac{4}{7}\right)^2+\left(\dfrac{3}{7}\right)^2\left(\dfrac{4}{7}\right)^3 \\ P(4 \text{ turns or less})&=\dfrac{4}{7}+\dfrac{3}{7}\left(\dfrac{4}{7}\right)^2+\left(\dfrac{3}{7}\right)^2\left(\dfrac{4}{7}\right)^3+\left(\dfrac{3}{7}\right)^3\left(\dfrac{4}{7}\right)^4 \end{aligned}$$
So for $n$ turns,
$$P(n \text{ turns or less})=\dfrac{4}{7}+\dfrac{3}{7}\left(\dfrac{4}{7}\right)^2+\cdots+\left(\dfrac{3}{7}\right)^{n-1}\left(\dfrac{4}{7}\right)^n$$
This is a GP with $a=\dfrac{4}{7}$ and $r=\dfrac{3}{7}\times\dfrac{4}{7}$. The probability that Ms Kim wins the game is its limiting sum:
$$\begin{aligned} S_\infty&=\dfrac{a}{1-r} \\ &=\dfrac{\frac{4}{7}}{1-\frac{3}{7}\times\frac{4}{7}} \\ &=\dfrac{28}{37} \end{aligned}$$','3: correct answer; 2: correct limiting sum expression; 1: identifies a GP with r = (3/7)(4/7)',373,NULL,'parse-v1',NULL,NULL,'[[3, "Correct answer"], [2, "Correct limiting sum expression, or equivalent merit"], [1, "Identifies a GP with $r=\\dfrac{3}{7}\\times\\dfrac{4}{7}$, or equivalent merit"]]','{"criteria_original": [[3, "correct answer"], [2, "correct limiting sum expression"], [1, "identifies a GP with r = (3/7)(4/7)"]]}');
$$\begin{aligned} \text{LHS}&=\dfrac{\cos^2\theta+(1+\sin\theta)^2}{\cos\theta(1+\sin\theta)} \\ &=\dfrac{1-\sin^2\theta+1+2\sin\theta+\sin^2\theta}{\cos\theta(1+\sin\theta)} \\ &=\dfrac{2+2\sin\theta}{\cos\theta(1+\sin\theta)} \\ &=\dfrac{2(1+\sin\theta)}{\cos\theta(1+\sin\theta)} \\ &=\dfrac{2}{\cos\theta} \\ &=2\sec\theta \\ &=\text{RHS} \end{aligned}$$','2: correct proof; 1: combines fractions and uses cos^2 = 1 - sin^2',373,NULL,'parse-v1',NULL,NULL,'[[2, "Correct answer"], [1, "Combines fractions and uses $\\cos^2\\theta=1-\\sin^2\\theta$, or equivalent merit"]]','{"criteria_original": [[2, "correct proof"], [1, "combines fractions and uses cos^2 = 1 - sin^2"]], "top_criterion_original": "correct proof"}');

Sine rule in $\triangle ABC$:
$$\begin{aligned} \dfrac{AC}{\sin45^\circ}&=\dfrac{x}{\sin30^\circ} \\ \dfrac{AC}{\frac{1}{\sqrt{2}}}&=\dfrac{x}{\frac{1}{2}} \\ \sqrt{2}AC&=2x \\ AC&=\dfrac{2x}{\sqrt{2}} &&(1) \end{aligned}$$
Sine rule in $\triangle ADC$:
$$\begin{aligned} \dfrac{AC}{\sin60^\circ}&=\dfrac{y}{\sin45^\circ} \\ \dfrac{AC}{\frac{\sqrt{3}}{2}}&=\dfrac{y}{\frac{1}{\sqrt{2}}} \\ \dfrac{2AC}{\sqrt{3}}&=\sqrt{2}y \\ AC&=\dfrac{\sqrt{6}y}{2} &&(2) \end{aligned}$$
Equate (1) and (2):
$$\begin{aligned} \dfrac{2x}{\sqrt{2}}&=\dfrac{\sqrt{6}y}{2} \\ \dfrac{x}{y}&=\dfrac{\sqrt{12}}{4} \\ &=\dfrac{2\sqrt{3}}{4} \\ &=\dfrac{\sqrt{3}}{2} \end{aligned}$$','3: correct proof; 2: AC in terms of x and in terms of y; 1: one correct sine rule expression for AC',373,NULL,'parse-v1',NULL,NULL,'[[3, "Correct answer"], [2, "Finds $AC$ in terms of $x$ and in terms of $y$, or equivalent merit"], [1, "One correct sine rule expression for $AC$, or equivalent merit"]]','{"criteria_original": [[3, "correct proof"], [2, "AC in terms of x and in terms of y"], [1, "one correct sine rule expression for AC"]], "top_criterion_original": "correct proof"}');
Taking the ratio:
$$\begin{aligned} \dfrac{\text{Area}_{\triangle ABC}}{\text{Area}_{\triangle ADC}}&=\dfrac{\frac{1}{2}x\,AC\sin105^\circ}{\frac{1}{2}y\,AC\sin75^\circ} \\ &=\dfrac{x\sin105^\circ}{y\sin75^\circ} \\ &=\dfrac{x\sin(180^\circ-75^\circ)}{y\sin75^\circ} \\ &=\dfrac{x\sin75^\circ}{y\sin75^\circ} \\ &=\dfrac{x}{y} \\ &=\dfrac{\sqrt{3}}{2} \end{aligned}$$
$$\therefore \text{Area}_{\triangle ABC}:\text{Area}_{\triangle ADC}=\sqrt{3}:2$$','3: correct proof; 2: uses sin 105 = sin(180 - 75) = sin 75; 1: correct ratio of the two area formulas',373,NULL,'parse-v1',NULL,NULL,'[[3, "Correct answer"], [2, "Uses $\\sin105^\\circ=\\sin(180^\\circ-75^\\circ)=\\sin75^\\circ$, or equivalent merit"], [1, "Correct ratio of the two area formulas, or equivalent merit"]]','{"criteria_original": [[3, "correct proof"], [2, "uses sin 105 = sin(180 - 75) = sin 75"], [1, "correct ratio of the two area formulas"]], "top_criterion_original": "correct proof"}');

In the right-angled triangles with the tower of height $h$:
$$\begin{aligned} \tan25^\circ&=\dfrac{h}{OY} &&\text{so } OY=\dfrac{h}{\tan25^\circ} \\ \tan31^\circ&=\dfrac{h}{OX} &&\text{so } OX=\dfrac{h}{\tan31^\circ} \end{aligned}$$
Pythagoras'' theorem in triangle $OXY$:
$$\begin{aligned} 150^2+OX^2&=OY^2 \\ 150^2+\left(\dfrac{h}{\tan31^\circ}\right)^2&=\left(\dfrac{h}{\tan25^\circ}\right)^2 \\ 150^2+\dfrac{h^2}{\tan^231^\circ}&=\dfrac{h^2}{\tan^225^\circ} \\ h^2\left(\dfrac{1}{\tan^225^\circ}-\dfrac{1}{\tan^231^\circ}\right)&=150^2 \\ h^2\left(\dfrac{\tan^231^\circ-\tan^225^\circ}{\tan^225^\circ\tan^231^\circ}\right)&=150^2 \\ h^2&=\dfrac{150^2\tan^225^\circ\tan^231^\circ}{\tan^231^\circ-\tan^225^\circ} \\ h&=\dfrac{150\tan25^\circ\tan31^\circ}{\sqrt{\tan^231^\circ-\tan^225^\circ}} \end{aligned}$$','3: correct proof; 2: Pythagoras in triangle OXY with OX and OY in terms of h; 1: OX and OY in terms of h',373,NULL,'parse-v1',NULL,NULL,'[[3, "Correct answer"], [2, "Pythagoras in triangle $OXY$ with $OX$ and $OY$ in terms of $h$, or equivalent merit"], [1, "$OX$ and $OY$ in terms of $h$, or equivalent merit"]]','{"criteria_original": [[3, "correct proof"], [2, "Pythagoras in triangle OXY with OX and OY in terms of h"], [1, "OX and OY in terms of h"]], "top_criterion_original": "correct proof"}');
