For stationary points, $\dfrac{dA}{d\alpha}=0$.
$$\begin{aligned} \cos\alpha-\sin\alpha &= 0 \\ \tan\alpha &= 1 \\ \alpha &= \dfrac{\pi}{4} \end{aligned}$$
Test the nature with the second derivative.
$$\begin{aligned} \dfrac{d^2A}{d\alpha^2} &= 5000(-\sin\alpha-\cos\alpha) \\ &= 5000\left(-\dfrac{1}{\sqrt{2}}-\dfrac{1}{\sqrt{2}}\right) &&\left(\text{at } \alpha=\dfrac{\pi}{4}\right) \\ &= -5000\sqrt{2}<0 \end{aligned}$$
So the area is a maximum when $\alpha=\dfrac{\pi}{4}$.
$$\begin{aligned} A_{\max} &= 5000\left(\sin\dfrac{\pi}{4}+\cos\dfrac{\pi}{4}\right) \\ &= 5000\left(\dfrac{1}{\sqrt{2}}+\dfrac{1}{\sqrt{2}}\right) \\ &= 5000\sqrt{2} \text{ m}^2 \end{aligned}$$','1: correct derivative; 1: $\alpha=\frac{\pi}{4}$; 1: second derivative test for maximum; 1: shows $5000\sqrt{2}$',377,NULL,'parse-v1',NULL,NULL,'[[4, "Correct answer"], [3, "Tests the second derivative for a maximum, or equivalent merit"], [2, "Finds $\\alpha=\\dfrac{\\pi}{4}$, or equivalent merit"], [1, "Finds the correct derivative, or equivalent merit"]]','{"criteria_original": [[1, "correct derivative"], [1, "$\\alpha=\\dfrac{\\pi}{4}$"], [1, "second derivative test for maximum"], [1, "shows $5000\\sqrt{2}$"]], "top_criterion_original": "shows $5000\\sqrt{2}$"}');
$$\begin{aligned} y &= 2f(1-x)+6 \\ &= 2\big[(1-x)^2-4(1-x)\big]+6 \\ &= 2\big[1-2x+x^2-4+4x\big]+6 \\ &= 2(x^2+2x-3)+6 \\ &= 2x^2+4x \\ &= 2x(x+2) \end{aligned}$$
The $x$-intercepts are $0$ and $-2$, so the axis of symmetry is $x=-1$ and the vertex is $(-1,-2)$.

Check by transformations: $2f(1-x)+6=2f\big(-(x-1)\big)+6$ reflects in the $y$-axis, shifts right by $1$, dilates by a factor of $2$ vertically and shifts up by $6$, so the vertex $(2,-4)$ of $f$ becomes $(-1,-2)$.

\exfigure{377_q30_s1}','1: some useful working; 1: intercept(s); 1: vertex',377,NULL,'parse-v1',NULL,NULL,'[[3, "Correct answer"], [2, "Finds the intercepts or the vertex, or equivalent merit"], [1, "Provides some relevant working, or equivalent merit"]]','{"criteria_original": [[1, "some useful working"], [1, "intercept(s)"], [1, "vertex"]], "top_criterion_original": "vertex", "source_original": "Since $2f(1-x)+6=2f(-(x-1))+6$, our graph will be $f(x)$ transformed as follows: reflect in $y$-axis; shift right by 1; dilate by factor 2 vertically; shift up by 6.\n\nHence new vertex is $(2,-4)\\to(-1,-2)$\n\nEasiest is just to transform algebraically:\n\n$y=2f(1-x)+6=2\\big[(1-x)^2-4(1-x)\\big]+6$\n\n$\\therefore y=2\\big[1-2x+x^2-4+4x\\big]+6=2(x^2+2x-3)+6$\n\n$\\therefore y=x^2+2x=x(x+2)$\n\n$x$-ints are $0,-2$ & axis is $x=-1$ $\\therefore V=(-1,-2)$\n\n\\exfigure{377_q30_s1}", "corrected": "Corrected from source: 2(x^2+2x-3)+6 simplifies to 2x^2+4x = 2x(x+2), not x^2+2x (the intercepts and vertex in the source were already right)."}');
$$\begin{aligned} \dfrac{x}{x^2+1} &= mx \\ x &= mx^3+mx \\ mx^3+(m-1)x &= 0 \\ x\big(mx^2+(m-1)\big) &= 0 \end{aligned}$$
One solution is $x=0$.

The other two need $x^2=\dfrac{1-m}{m}=\dfrac{1}{m}-1$, so $x=\pm\sqrt{\dfrac{1}{m}-1}$.

For two solutions:
$$\begin{aligned} \dfrac{1}{m}-1 &> 0 \\ \dfrac{1}{m} &> 1 \end{aligned}$$
$$\therefore\ 0<m<1$$
Alternatively, $mx^2+(m-1)=0$ needs a positive discriminant.
$$\begin{aligned} \Delta=-4m(m-1) &> 0 \\ 4m(m-1) &< 0 \end{aligned}$$
$$\therefore\ 0<m<1$$','2: correct answer $0<m<1$ (two ticks shown)',377,NULL,'parse-v1',NULL,NULL,'[[2, "Correct answer"]]','{"criteria_original": [[2, "correct answer $0<m<1$ (two ticks shown)"]], "top_criterion_original": "correct answer $0<m<1$ (two ticks shown)"}');
$$\begin{aligned} \text{Area} &= 2\times\int_0^{\sqrt{\frac{1}{m}-1}}\left(\dfrac{x}{x^2+1}-mx\right)dx \\ &= 2\times\left[\dfrac{1}{2}\ln|x^2+1|-\dfrac{mx^2}{2}\right]_0^{\sqrt{\frac{1}{m}-1}} \\ &= \ln\left|\dfrac{1}{m}-1+1\right|-m\left(\dfrac{1}{m}-1\right)-(\ln 1-0) \\ &= \ln\left|\dfrac{1}{m}\right|+m-1 \end{aligned}$$
$$\therefore\ \text{Area}=\ln\left|\dfrac{1}{m}\right|+m-1 \text{ u}^2$$','1: correct integral using symmetry; 1: correct primitive; 1: correct answer',377,NULL,'parse-v1',NULL,NULL,'[[3, "Correct answer"], [2, "Finds the correct primitive, or equivalent merit"], [1, "Writes the correct integral using symmetry, or equivalent merit"]]','{"criteria_original": [[1, "correct integral using symmetry"], [1, "correct primitive"], [1, "correct answer"]], "top_criterion_original": "correct answer"}');
$$\begin{aligned} f(0) &= e^0-1-0=0 \\ f''(x) &= e^x-1>0 \text{ for } x>0 \end{aligned}$$
So $f(x)$ is increasing for $x\ge 0$ and $f(0)=0$, hence $f(x)\ge 0$ for $x\ge 0$.
$$\begin{aligned} e^x-1-x &\ge 0 \\ e^x &\ge 1+x \end{aligned}$$','2: complete proof (two ticks shown)',377,NULL,'parse-v1',NULL,NULL,'[[2, "Correct answer"]]','{"criteria_original": [[2, "complete proof (two ticks shown)"]], "top_criterion_original": "complete proof (two ticks shown)"}');

Using (i) with $x^2$ in place of $x$:
$$1+x^2\le e^{x^2}\le e^x$$
Integrate over $0\le x\le 1$.
$$\int_0^1(1+x^2)\,dx\le\int_0^1 e^{x^2}\,dx\le\int_0^1 e^x\,dx$$
$$\begin{aligned} \left[x+\dfrac{x^3}{3}\right]_0^1 &\le \int_0^1 e^{x^2}\,dx \le \Big[e^x\Big]_0^1 \\ \left(1+\dfrac{1}{3}\right)-0 &\le \int_0^1 e^{x^2}\,dx \le e^1-e^0 \\ \dfrac{4}{3} &\le \int_0^1 e^{x^2}\,dx \le e-1 \end{aligned}$$
Since $e-1<e$:
$$\dfrac{4}{3}\le\int_0^1 e^{x^2}\,dx\le e$$','1: $1+x^2\le e^{x^2}\le e^x$; 1: integrates the inequality; 1: evaluates bounds',377,NULL,'parse-v1',NULL,NULL,'[[3, "Correct answer"], [2, "Integrates the inequality, or equivalent merit"], [1, "Shows $1+x^2\\le e^{x^2}\\le e^x$, or equivalent merit"]]','{"criteria_original": [[1, "$1+x^2\\le e^{x^2}\\le e^x$"], [1, "integrates the inequality"], [1, "evaluates bounds"]], "top_criterion_original": "evaluates bounds"}');
CREATE TABLE tags (id INTEGER PRIMARY KEY, part_id INTEGER REFERENCES parts(id), question_id INTEGER REFERENCES questions(id), scheme TEXT NOT NULL, focus_area TEXT, dot_point TEXT, code TEXT, year_level INTEGER, coverage TEXT, is_primary INTEGER DEFAULT 0, confidence REAL, tag_source TEXT NOT NULL, mapping_version TEXT, prompt_version TEXT);
CREATE TRIGGER qg_tags_qid_insert BEFORE INSERT ON tags
  WHEN NEW.part_id IS NOT NULL AND NEW.question_id IS NOT NULL AND NEW.question_id != (SELECT question_id FROM parts WHERE id=NEW.part_id)
  BEGIN SELECT RAISE(ABORT,'tags.question_id must match its part''s question'); END;
CREATE TRIGGER qg_tags_qid_update BEFORE UPDATE ON tags
  WHEN NEW.part_id IS NOT NULL AND NEW.question_id IS NOT NULL AND NEW.question_id != (SELECT question_id FROM parts WHERE id=NEW.part_id)
  BEGIN SELECT RAISE(ABORT,'tags.question_id must match its part''s question'); END;
CREATE TRIGGER qg_tags_qid_fill AFTER INSERT ON tags
  WHEN NEW.part_id IS NOT NULL AND NEW.question_id IS NULL
  BEGIN UPDATE tags SET question_id=(SELECT question_id FROM parts WHERE id=NEW.part_id) WHERE rowid=NEW.rowid; END;
CREATE TRIGGER qg_solutions_qid_insert BEFORE INSERT ON solutions
  WHEN NEW.part_id IS NOT NULL AND NEW.question_id IS NOT NULL AND NEW.question_id != (SELECT question_id FROM parts WHERE id=NEW.part_id)
  BEGIN SELECT RAISE(ABORT,'solutions.question_id must match its part''s question'); END;
